\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro steady above 1-month low but seen as vulnerable\b0\par\par\uc2 822 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 2011\par 07:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Signs of Rome political deadlock easing support euro\par \par \uc2 * Outlook for the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  still bleak\par \par \uc2 * Euro/yen may drop below 100 yen by year-end -analyst\par \par \uc2 * Talk of hedge fund demand for $1.26 euro puts\par \par \uc2 (Updates levels)\par \par \uc2 By Masayuki Kitano\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Nov 11 (Reuters) - The euro held steady above its recent one-month low on Friday, but market players were sceptical that it would see a sustained bounce due to uncertainty over whether Italy will make progress on fiscal reforms under new leadership.\par \par \uc2 The euro has gained some reprieve after a sell-off earlier in the week when Italian 10-year bond yields shot above the 7 percent level that is widely regarded as unsustainable.\par \par \uc2 Relief that yields at an Italian bond sale on Thursday remained below 7 percent lent support to the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as did signs of easing political deadlock in Rome and Greece.\par \par \uc2 Former European Commissioner Mario Monti has emerged as favourite to replace Italian Prime Minister Silvio Berlusconi within days and lead an emergency government. In Greece, former European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Vice President Lucas Papademos was appointed to head an interim crisis cabinet in Greece.\par \par \uc2 The euro was little changed from late U.S. trade on Thursday, changing hands at $1.3621 and staying above a one-month low of $1.3484 touched on Thursday. For the week, the euro is still down about 1.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 While political developments were positive, analysts are sceptical that they would be enough to spur a sustained drop in Italian bond yields or a rise in the euro.\par \par \uc2 "Euro, to me, is still very much a sell on rallies," said Mitul Kotecha, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy for \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  CIB in Hong Kong, adding that his bank's forecast was for the euro to drop to $1.33 by year-end.\par \par \uc2 "I still think it's going to be some time before we see a sustained drop in Italian bond yields," he added.\par \par \uc2 Market positioning in options suggests that investors are bracing for a further slide in the euro, with euro/dollar one-month risk reversals quoted at 4.25/3.75\par \par \uc2 in favour of euro puts.\par \par \uc2 That is close to the extreme levels seen back in September, before the euro hit a nine-month low of $1.3145 in early October.\par \par \uc2 In a sign of bearish sentiment against the euro, some traders said hedge funds have recently bought euro puts with strikes around $1.26 that are due to expire in six weeks.\par \par \uc2 DOLLAR/YEN DIPS\par \par \uc2 Closer to current levels, one possible downside target for the euro lies near $1.3405, the 76.4 percent retracement of the euro's $1.3145 to $1.4248 rally.\par \par \uc2 Resistance on the daily Ichimoku chart, a popular technical analysis tool, lies near $1.3678, where the tenkan sen, or conversion line, now comes in.\par \par \uc2 The euro held steady against the yen at 105.64 yen , staying above a one-month low near 104.74 yen hit on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Daisuke Karakama, market economist for \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2  in Tokyo, said he thought there was a better than even chance of euro/yen dropping below 100 yen by year-end.\par \par \uc2 "To think that the debt problems will settle down just because a potential successor to (Italian Prime Minister Silvio) Berlusconi has emerged, would be being too optimistic," Karakama said.\par \par \uc2 "We don't know whether a new government would be able to persuade the public and impose painful measures," he said, adding that it was unclear whether progress would be made toward fiscal consolidation.\par \par \uc2 The dollar dipped 0.1 percent versus against the yen to 77.54 yen. It dipped to as low as 77.49 yen at one point, the lowest since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Oct. 31.\par \par \uc2 On Oct. 31, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent an estimated 7.7 trillion yen ($98.5 billion), a daily record for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to curb the yen's strength. Market sources have told Reuters that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has probably conducted more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Oct. 31.\par \par \uc2 Asked if Japanese authorities had continued to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since Oct. 31, Japanese Finance Minister Jun Azumi declined to comment on Friday, only repeating that he would take firm steps when necessary.\par \par \uc2 Both the dollar and the yen are considered safe haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in times of market stress, but speculation that the U.S. Federal Reserve may eventually launch another asset-buying programme, or QE3, has helped weigh on the dollar versus the yen.\par \par \uc2 One possible support for dollar/yen lies near 77.43 yen, the 50 percent retracement of the dollar's rise on Oct. 31 from a post-World War Two record low near 75.31 yen to a three-month high near 79.55 yen.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Ian Chua in Sydney and Antoni Slodkowski in Tokyo)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111110e7ba000mv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
