\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA DAILY  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  OUTLOOK - Majors\b0\par\par\uc2 1,915 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 2011\par 00:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Following are expected trading ranges and outlooks for nine major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs today:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Immediate Range   Larger Range \par USD/JPY    77.54-77.88      77.43-78.10 \par EUR/USD   1.3523-1.3650    1.3346-1.3730 \par AUD/USD   1.0114-1.0210    1.0098-1.0271 \par NZD/USD   0.7739-0.7858    0.7671-0.7907 \par GBP/USD   1.5875-1.5968    1.5844-1.6012 \par USD/CHF   0.9021-0.9110    0.8918-0.9125 \par USD/CAD   1.0160-1.0272    1.0068-1.0338 \par EUR/JPY   104.76-105.80    104.20-106.23 \par EUR/GBP   0.8497-0.8548    0.8458-0.8599 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (Ranges are calculated using recent high and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis - Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY - to range-trade. Undermined by selling of yen crosses and capital flows to safe-haven yen amid elevated risk aversion (VIX fear gauge spiked 31.59% to 36.16; DJIA tumbled 3.2% overnight) as worries over euro-zone financial crisis intensified after Italian government's borrowing costs accelerated toward unsustainable levels and report that German chancellor Merkel's party proposing fresh legislation to allow troubled countries to leave the euro zone without losing EU membership. USD/JPY also weighed by lower U.S. Treasury yields, Japan exporter sales; surprise 0.1% drop in U.S. wholesale inventories in September (vs forecast for 0.6% increase) for first decline since 2009 as sales slowed. But USD/JPY downside limited by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  importer demand, expectations of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Data focus: 2350 GMT \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  September orders received for machinery, October money stock, 0500 GMT Japan October consumer confidence survey, 0600 GMT Japan October preliminary machine tool orders, 1330 GMT U.S. September trade balance, Nov 5 weekly jobless claims, October import & export price indexes. USD/JPY daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics in bearish mode. Resistance at 77.88 (yesterday's high), then at 78.10 (Tuesday's high), 78.22-78.27 band (Monday's high-Friday's high) and 78.41 (Nov. 2 high); breach would expose upside to 79.10 (Nov. 1 high), then 79.55 (Oct. 31 high), 79.72 (200-day moving average) and 80.24 (Aug. 4 high). Support at 77.54 (yesterday's low), then at 77.43 (50% Fibonacci correction of Oct. 31's 75.31-79.55 spike); breach would expose downside to 76.93 (61.8% correction), then 75.31 (Oct. 31 record low) and psychological 75.00 level.\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to trade with bearish bias. Euro sentiment hurt after clearing house LCH.Clearnet raised its initial margin requirements on Italian bonds, triggering jump in yield on benchmark 10-year Italian government bonds well above 7% - to levels previously associated with Greek, Irish, and Portuguese bailout requests. EUR/USD also weighed by report in Germany suggesting that German chancellor Merkel's party is trying to allow countries to leave the euro while remaining in the European Union; German government's Council of Economic Advisers saying break-up of euro zone can't be ruled out if member countries don't repair their budgets; investor worries that Italy can't swiftly address its massive debt pile and overcome its fiscal challenges given the uncertainty following Berlusconi's announcement he will step down as Italy's prime minister; continuing political uncertainty in Greece as President Papoulias called a meeting of political leaders today after country's 2 main parties failed to agree on who will lead interim coalition government; worries over European economic recession; expectations of rate cuts from ECB in coming months. But EUR/USD losses tempered by sovereign demand for euro. EUR/USD daily chart negative-biased as MACD bearish, stochastics revert to bearish mode near oversold; bearish outside-day-range pattern completed yesterday; 5-day moving average below 15-day MA and falling. Support at 1.3523 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.3346 (Oct. 10 low), then 1.3241 (Oct. 6 low) and 1.3145 (Oct. 4 swing low). Resistance at 1.3650 (hourly chart), then at 1.3730 (hourly chart), 1.3859 (yesterday's high) and 1.3870 (Friday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.4021 (100-day moving average), then 1.4102 (200-day moving average) and 1.4172 (Oct. 31 high).\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD - to trade with bearish bias. Undermined by increased risk aversion as European financial crisis deepens; softer commodity prices (CRB spot index closed down 4.15 yesterday at 318.46). But AUD/USD losses tempered by AUD-USD interest-rate differential, sovereign demand for Aussie. Data focus: 0000 GMT Australia November consumer inflationary expectations survey, 0030 GMT Australia October labour force. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics in bearish mode, 5-day moving average below 15-day MA and falling. Support at 1.0114 (Oct. 18 low), then at 1.0098 (Oct. 13 low); breach would target 1.0069 (50% Fibonacci correction of 0.9386-1.0752 Oct.4-Oct. 27 advance), then 0.9908 (61.8% correction) and 0.9963 (Oct. 12 low). Resistance at 1.0210 (hourly chart), then at 1.0271 (hourly chart) and 1.0404 (Tuesday's high); breach would target 1.0426 (Monday's high, then 1.0439-1.0446 band (Friday's high-Nov. 3 high) and 1.0566 (Nov. 1 high).\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD - to trade with bearish bias. Undermined by heightened risk aversion, softer commodity prices. But NZD/USD losses tempered by NZD-USD yield gap. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD bearish, stochastics reverting to bearish mode; 5-day moving average below 15-day MA and falling. Support at 0.7739 (Oct. 12 low); breach would expose downside to 0.7671 (Oct. 7 low), then 0.7466 (Oct. 4 swing low). Resistance at 0.7858 (hourly chart), then at 0.7907 (hourly chart); breach would expose upside to 0.7987-0.7997 band (yesterday's high-Monday's high), then 0.8029 (55-day moving average) and 0.8105 (Nov. 1 high).\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to consolidate with bearish bias as markets await BOE monetary policy announcement at 1200 GMT: \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to stay pat on its policy rate and bond-buying facility which stand at 0.5% and GBP275 billion respectively. GBP/USD undermined by elevated risk aversion; widening of UK global goods trade deficit to record GBP9.8 billion in September from revised GBP8.6 billion in August. But GBP/USD losses tempered by GBP demand on soft EUR/GBP cross. Daily chart negative-biased as stochastics falling from overbought; MACD staging bearish crossover against its exponential moving average. Support at 1.5875 (Nov. 3 low); breach would expose downside to 1.5844 (55-day moving average), then 1.5752 (Oct. 21 low), 1.5679 (Oct. 20 low) and 1.5629 (Oct. 18 low). Resistance at 1.5968 (hourly chart), then at 1.6012 (hourly chart) and 1.6119-1.6129 band (yesterday's high-Tuesday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.6165 (Oct. 31 high), then 1.6253 (Sept. 2 high) and 1.6455 (Aug. 29 high).\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to trade with bullish bias. Buoyed by broadly firmer USD undertone as investor risk aversion intensifies; expectations of \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's CHF-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics in bullish mode, although latter at overbought; 5-day moving average above 15-day MA and rising. Resistance at 0.9110 (yesterday's high), then at 0.9125 (Oct. 11 high); breach would expose upside to 0.9290 (Oct. 10 high), then 0.9317 (Oct. 6 high) and 0.9340 (April 1 high). Support at 0.9021 (hourly chart), then at 0.8918 (Tuesday's low) and 0.8865 (Monday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.8805 (55-day moving average), then 0.8766-0.8758 band (Friday's low-Nov. 3 low), 0.8709 (200-day moving average) and 0.8566 (Oct. 27 low).\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD - to trade with bullish bias. Underpinned by mounting risk aversion, weaker commodity & oil prices (Nymex crude settled down $1.06 yesterday at $95.74/bbl). Data focus: 1330 GMT Canada September trade balance. USD/CAD daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics in bullish mode, although latter near overbought; 5-day moving average above 15-day MA and rising. Resistance at 1.0264-1.0272 band (Oct. 18 high-Oct. 13 high); breach would expose upside to 1.0328-1.0338 band (Oct. 12 high-Oct. 11 high), then 1.0363 (61% Fibonacci correction of Oct. 4-Oct. 27 1.0657-0.9888 decline). Support at 1.0160 (hourly chart); breach would expose downside to 1.0073-1.0068 band (Tuesday's low-Friday's low), then 1.0052 (Nov. 3 low), 0.9970 (Nov. 1 low), 0.9910 (Oct. 31 low, near 100-day moving average) and 0.9892-0.9888 band (Oct. 28 low-Oct. 27 low).\par \par \uc2 EUR/JPY - to trade with bearish bias. Undermined by worries over Europe's deepening debt crisis; Japan exporter sales. But EUR/JPY downside limited by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  importer demand, expectations of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics in bearish mode; 5-day moving average staging bearish crossover against 15-day MA; bearish parabolic stop-and-reverse signal hit at 106.47 yesterday. Support at 104.76 (Oct. 26 low); breach would expose downside to 104.20 (Oct. 12 low), then 104.01 (Oct. 11 low) and 102.47 (Oct. 10 low). Resistance at 105.80 (hourly chart), then at 106.23 (hourly chart); breach would expose upside to 107.60-107.65 (yesterday's high-Tuesday's high), then 108.18 (Monday's high) and 108.29 (Friday's high).\par \par \uc2 EUR/GBP - to trade with bearish bias. Undermined by jitters over worsening euro-zone financial crisis. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics bearish, although latter at oversold; 5- & 15-day moving averages falling. Support at 0.8497 (yesterday's low); breach would target 0.8458 (March 1 reaction low), then 0.8381 (Feb. 22 low). Resistance at 0.8548 (hourly chart), then at 0.8599 (yesterday's high), 0.8611 (Monday's high) and 0.8655 (Friday's high); breach would target 0.8666 (previous base set Oct. 21), then 0.8784 (Oct. 31 high) and 0.8821-0.8830 band (Oct. 28 high-Oct. 27 high).\par \par \uc2 -By Jerry Tan, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 6415-4046; Jerry.tan@dowjones.com [ 09-11-11 2330GMT ]\par \par \uc2 This is a financial news and information service. It is provided in general terms and does not take account of or address any individual user's position. To the extent that this article includes suggestions as to various possible investment strategies which users might consider, it does so in only general terms without reference to the personal factors which should determine any user's investment decisions. Nothing contained in this service constitutes personalized investment advice. Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors shall not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article. This article does not constitute or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security.\par \par \uc2 [ 09-11-11 2330GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1075 4033   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111109e7b9001os\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
