\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan Current Account Surplus Shrinks Again In September\b0\par\par\uc2 472 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2011\par 01:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Japan's current account surplus falls 21.4% in September to Y1.585 trillion\par \par \uc2 -- Number is better than expected, market had been looking for a 26% decline\par \par \uc2 -- Future cloudier given expected growing impact of strong yen\par \par \uc2 (Adds economist reaction, background, details 5th-10th paragraphs)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's current account surplus fell 21.4% in September from a year earlier, contracting for the seventh straight month, although the figures were better than expected and showed a continued recovery from the March earthquake.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The surplus in the current account, the broadest measure of Japan's trade with the rest of the world, stood at Y1.585 trillion in September before seasonal adjustment, data from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  showed Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires and the Nikkei had estimated that the surplus would fall 26% on year to Y1.5 trillion in September.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rate of decline was the smallest since the earthquake and tsunami on March 11, reflecting a recovery in the country's production and exports from the disasters. Japan's current account surplus was down a steep 64.3% from a year earlier in August.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The trade surplus in goods stood at Y373.2 billion, down 59.0% from last year, due partly to the rise in the cost of imported fuel, which helped to push imports up 22.4%. Japan's imports of fossil fuels have also risen sharply due to the shutdown of nuclear power plants after the March accident at the Fukushima Daiichi plant.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The broader surplus in "goods and services" was Y255.2 billion, down 70.4 percent from a year earlier, but marking the first surplus in three months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There have been concerns about whether exports can continue to rise, given the strength of the yen and uncertain prospects in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's major export markets. Despite \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, the yen has remained near record-high levels. As of 0029 GMT, the dollar was trading at Y77.69, compared with the all-time low of Y75.31 reached on Oct. 31.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some economists said the figures showed that Japan's exports are solid and likely to remain so in the months ahead.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In our view, the U.S. economy can keep growing at 2% and emerging nations should remain strong," said UBS Securities senior economist Takuji Aida.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The current account measures trade in goods, services, tourism and investment. It is calculated by determining the difference between Japan's income from foreign sources against payments on foreign obligations and excludes net capital investment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Web site: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mof.go.jp/bpoffice/e1c004.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.mof.go.jp/bpoffice/e1c004.htm}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com [ 09-11-11 0034GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111109e7b900019\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
