\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-Japan economy to be restrained by strong yen\b0\par\par\uc2 613 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2011\par 14:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * For poll data\par \par \uc2 * Japan econ headed for modest growth but self-sustained expansion unlikely\par \par \uc2 * Analysts see 1-in-3 chance of recession in next 12 mths\par \par \uc2 * Deflation set to persist; more BOJ asset purchases possible\par \par \uc2 By Kaori Kaneko\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 9 (Reuters) - Japan's economy is expected to grow moderately in the next year after a sharp rebound in the third quarter, restrained by faltering global growth and a strong yen, a Reuters poll showed on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Japan will likely log its first quarter of economic growth in July-September after three straight quarters of contraction, helped by reconstruction after the devastating March earthquake and nuclear crisis at Fukushima.\par \par \uc2 Preliminary gross domestic product (GDP) data due Nov. 14 (2350 GMT Nov. 13) are expected to show a rebound of 1.4 percent rebound, according to the poll.\par \par \uc2 But the consensus is still for a significant one-in-three chance of another recession in the next 12 months, in line with last month's survey.\par \par \uc2 "The economy is returning to normal from slumps following the disaster and it will be supported by public spending as effects from extra budgets emerge next year." said Takeshi Minami, chief economist at Norinchukin Research Institute.\par \par \uc2 "But it will not be self-sustaining growth."\par \par \uc2 The world's third largest economy is expected to grow 0.5 percent in October-December, the same as last month's poll, following four straight months of downgrades. It is expected to grow 0.6 percent in the first quarter of 2012.\par \par \uc2 But the rush of global capital flowing into the yen, despite increasingly frequent attempts by the Japanese authorities to stop it from rising, has been relentless.\par \par \uc2 "A persistently strong yen is affecting the economy and a slowdown in the global economy is intensifying, which could weigh on exports. So we cannot expect strong growth," said Minami.\par \par \uc2 STRONG YEN\par \par \uc2 Europe's unsuccessful efforts to contain its sovereign debt crisis have jolted financial markets, driving the yen to record highs and prompting Japanese policymakers to worry about damage to the export-led economy.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  unilaterally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last month, for the third time this year, to tame the soaring yen after it hit a record peak against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The move came only days after the Bank of Japan eased policy by boosting its asset buying programme.\par \par \uc2 But despite Japan's efforts, analysts expect the yen will stay strong against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "At the moment, we think the euro zone will contain its debt crisis and prevent it from turning into a credit crunch," said Yoshimasa Maruyama, chief economist at Itochu Economic Research Institute.\par \par \uc2 "But if such risks spread beyond the euro zone area, the global economy will tumble."\par \par \uc2 Interest rates are expected to remain virtually at zero at least until the end of the fiscal year to March 2013.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets continue to be the key to the outlook for the BOJ's monetary policy," said Junko Nishioka, chief economist at RBS securities.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said it was possible that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could increase its asset buying programme, including buying riskier assets if stock prices drop towards the year-end.\par \par \uc2 The consensus showed Japan's battle with deflation will continue into 2013 as the annual change in core consumer prices is expected to remain negative until Q113. These forecasts were almost unchanged from last month's poll.\par \par \uc2 (For poll data click on or ) (For other stories from the poll click on )\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Kaori Kaneko, polling led by Somya Gupta in Bangalore; Editing by Ross Finley and Catherine Evans)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/POLL (EMBARGO 1220 GMT)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111112e7b9000g6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
