\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  WEEK AHEAD: Greece Still Near-Term Risk For Euro\b0\par\par\uc2 813 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2011\par 14:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Updates with Greek prime minister expected to resign, speculation over Italian prime minister's future.)\par \par \uc2 --Greece will likely continue to dominate the euro's direction\par \par \uc2 --Analysts see Greece's effect on markets as disproportionate, but "right behind Greece is Italy"\par \par \uc2 --Greek prime minister said over weekend he would resign\par \par \uc2 --Speculation Italian prime minister will resign\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     By Javier E. David \par     Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The euro is in for another week of being at the mercy of headlines out of Greece, just days after the ink had dried on a sweeping accord meant to reduce the threat posed by the country's financial problems.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the wake of an unexpected decision last week to put Greece's bailout terms to a national vote, Prime Minister George Papandreou survived a weekend confidence vote. But the road ahead is still fraught with uncertainty for the euro. With Papandreou expected to resign, analysts are uncertain about who will replace him in a new unity government, which may delay parliamentary approval of key reforms imposed upon Greece by the European Union and the International Monetary Fund.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the wake of Papandreou's call for a referendum on its recently negotiated bailout agreement, the euro fell to a near three-week low at $1.3608. But Greece is far from being the only source of uncertainty in the euro zone: political turmoil and fiscal woes are buffeting Italy, where sources are saying that Prime Minister Silvio Berlusconi is talking to his family about the prospects of him resigning.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So, after the wrenching market volatility that led analysts to question whether Greece may pull itself out of the 17-nation \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bloc, this week looks like it may be subject to the same. Already, Italian bond yields are trading Monday at new euro zone-era highs. That's setting the euro up for the same sort of swings this week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  recently traded at $1.3729 compared with $1.3792 late Friday in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Barring a major surprise on the economic front, the unyielding stream of headlines from Europe will likely continue to dominate the euro's direction, especially with little in the way of major economic indicators coming.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sovereign-debt concerns all but overwhelmed the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's surprise interest rate cut on Thursday, as well as monthly U.S. jobs data. Analysts lamented the effect Greece is having worldwide, which they see as disproportionate to its size and relative importance to the global economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The whole global market is being held in thrall by a country the size of Delaware," said Thomas Molloy, chief dealer at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Solutions\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "The world is trying to remind itself there's more than just Greece, [but] right behind Greece is Italy."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's relative poise has defied Italy's surging 10-year bond yields, which last week widened to a record 450 basis points above those of comparable German debt. Investors remain unconvinced that Italy's reform efforts can tame its burgeoning government debt, which at about $2 trillion is approximately 120% of its gross domestic product.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italy's financial distress has forced the ECB to buy its debt in markets, in an attempt to suppress selling pressure stemming from Greece's uncertainty. Meanwhile, a summit of the world's 20 largest industrialized and developed nations adjourned Friday without a concrete action plan on Europe's debt problems.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The lack of resolution led some analysts to predict new woes for the euro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This looks a bearish environment for EUR/USD and risk assets and suggests EUR/USD will probably break below 1.36 next week," said Chris Turner, Head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Strategy at ING Bank on Friday, who in a research note pronounced the G-20 meeting "a disappointment".\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts say that new ECB President Mario Draghi made a big statement by breaking with the single-minded emphasis on inflation espoused by his predecessor, Jean-Claude Trichet. While that will undermine the interest-rate advantage the common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  enjoys against the dollar and yen, markets welcomed the effort to support growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Elsewhere, markets are also on guard for more signs that Japan could sell yen in order to keep it weak. Last Monday, the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  massively in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets after the dollar sank to a new post World War II low against the yen near Y75.31. Market watchers say that ultra-cheap interest rates in the U.S. and concerns about the economy make it more probable that the dollar will revisit its record lows. In recent trading, the dollar bought Y78.07, compared with Y78.23 late Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --By Javier E. David, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-4564; javier.david@dowjones.com [ 11-07-11 0850ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865