said Wednesday that it is too early to summarize the effects of Tokyo's recent yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA VICE FINANCE MINISTER: GLOBAL INFLATIONARY PRESSURES TO REMAIN HIGH IN 2012 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China still faces many challenges, including rising protectionism, global inflationary pressures and the ongoing sovereign debt crisis in Europe, China Vice Finance Minister Li Yong said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA VICE FINANCE MINISTER: CHINA HAS STABILIZED PRICE INCREASES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China has stabilized price increases and the economy is moving in the right direction, China Vice Finance Minister Li Yong said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA OFFICIAL: INFLATION RISKS SUCH AS EXCHANGE-RATE VOLATILITY REMAIN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean Vice Finance Minister Shin Je-yoon said the government remains committed to price stability despite easing inflation in October, citing risks such as exchange-rate volatility.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA PRIME MINISTER: HAVE TO GUARD AGAINST IMPORTED INFLATION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India needs to guard against imported inflationary pressure as the euro-zone continues to reel under the debt crisis, Prime Minister Manmohan Singh said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TAIWAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : 'IMPOSSIBLE' TO BE IMMUNE FROM GLOBAL SLOWDOWN AS EXPORTS FORM OVER 70% OF GDP \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It is impossible for Taiwan to be immune from a global economic slowdown as exports form 70% of the island's gross domestic product, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Gov. Perng Fai-nan told legislators Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BANK INDONESIA OFFICIAL: ROOM FOR FURTHER RATE CUTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia sees room for further rate cuts after inflation eased in October, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA'S ZHUHAI CITY IMPOSES CEILING ON HOME PRICES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Zhuhai city in southern China's Guangdong province has set a ceiling of CNY11,285 per square meter for residential property transactions, becoming the first Chinese city to set a limit on residential property prices and triggering concerns of similar curbs in other cities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par FOCUS: NEXT UP, A RATE CUT IN KOREA? NOT LIKELY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Countries in the Asia-Pacific region are starting to cut interest rates to buttress their economies against a global slowdown. Despite easing inflation and slowing growth in South Korea, the nation's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  isn't likely to be among the next to ease policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INTERVIEW:FINNISH FIN MIN SEES NO ALERNATIVE FOR GREECE TO OCT 27 DEAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Greece has no alternative but to stick to a new bailout package agreed on October 27, and its membership of the euro zone would be an "open question" if voters were to reject that package, Finnish Finance Minister Jutta Urpilainen said Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PREVIEW: PHILIPPINES OCTOBER CPI LIKELY UP 5.1% DUE TO TYPHOONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Supply shocks in the Philippines caused by several typhoons likely spurred inflation to a quicker pace in October, although economists doubt that higher consumer prices would force a policy tightening from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  amid dimmer global economic growth prospects. [ 02-11-11 0855GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111102e7b2000q3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw18