\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 793 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2011\par 09:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GREEK CABINET APPROVES REFERENDUM PLAN, VOTE SEEN BY CHRISTMAS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Greece's cabinet Wednesday approved plans for a referendum on the country's latest bailout package, despite an open revolt by ruling-party lawmakers and amid fears the referendum could stoke further uncertainty in world financial markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par KREMLIN AIDE: RUSSIA WOULD CONSIDER EXTRA SUPPORT FOR EU FUND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MOSCOW (Dow Jones)--Russia's chief presidential economic aide Wednesday said Moscow, if asked, would consider contributing money directly to the European Union debt bailout, in addition to planned contributions via the International Monetary Fund.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMANY'S SCHAUEBLE EXPECTS GREECE TO EXECUTE REFORMS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German Finance Minister Wolfgang Schaeuble said Greece's fellow euro-zone countries would lend their support should the country decide to implement the tough reforms agreed upon in the latest aid program, the German daily Financial Times Deutschland reported Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S SWAN URGES GREECE TO STICK WITH BAIL-OUT PLAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's Treasurer Wayne Swan Wednesday criticised the Greek government's decision to hold a referendum on the nation's EUR130 billion bail-out plan and urged authorities to instead quickly implement the agreement struck by European leaders in Brussels last month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ SHIRAI CONCERNED OVER IMPACT OF EURO CRISIS ON JAPAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan policy board member Sayuri Shirai said Wednesday that one of the most serious downside risks to the Japanese economy is the negative effects of the European sovereign debt crisis through "an international financial channel," indicating that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains on guard against potential economic impact from sharp market moves.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN PM: NEED TO BE ALERT OVER DOWNSIDE RISKS OF YEN'S RISE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda said Wednesday that the government needs to be alert over possible downside risks posed by the yen's "one-sided rise" and reiterated that Tokyo will keep monitoring \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements closely.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FINANCE MINISTER: TOO EARLY TO SUMMARIZE EFFECTS OF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Finance Minister Jun Azumi said Wednesday that it is too early to summarize the effects of Tokyo's recent yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA VICE FINANCE MINISTER: GLOBAL INFLATIONARY PRESSURES TO REMAIN HIGH IN 2012 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China still faces many challenges, including rising protectionism, global inflationary pressures and the ongoing sovereign debt crisis in Europe, China Vice Finance Minister Li Yong said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA VICE FINANCE MINISTER: CHINA HAS STABILIZED PRICE INCREASES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China has stabilized price increases and the economy is moving in the right direction, China Vice Finance Minister Li Yong said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA OFFICIAL: INFLATION RISKS SUCH AS EXCHANGE-RATE VOLATILITY REMAIN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean Vice Finance Minister Shin Je-yoon said the government remains committed to price stability despite easing inflation in October,