\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Fin Min Azumi: Too Early To Summarize Effects Of Latest  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 181 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2011\par 06:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister Jun Azumi said Wednesday that it is too early to summarize the effects of Tokyo's recent yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We're not yet at a stage to summarize the effects," Azumi said at a parliamentary session.\par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched a new round of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, after the yen hit a record high of Y75.31 versus the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , estimated at around Y7 trillion, pushed the dollar to Y79.55 from around Y75.65 before the move. The pair last stood at Y78.11.\par \par \uc2 Azumi declined to comment on any further yen-selling action, but repeated that the ministry will keep monitoring the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and will take appropriate steps if needed.\par \par \uc2 Azumi also said the ministry always keeps in close contact with its counterparts overseas and said Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was "not an exception."\par \par \uc2 -By Tatsuo Ito, Dow Jones Newswires; +813-6269-2780; tatsuo.ito@dowjones.com [ 02-11-11 0555GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111102e7b2000b4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw137\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2055\pichgoal600
