\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 794 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2011\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: AUSTRALIA'S RBA CUTS RATES AS INFLATION EASES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cut interest rates Tuesday for the first time in two and a half years, fine tuning economic policy as inflation risks fade, unemployment rises and the global outlook worsens. (published 0501 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S RBA TO KEEP RATES STEADY AT 4.50% IN DEC - SURVEY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  won't cut interest rates again at a policy meeting next month, a unanimous survey of economists said Tuesday in the wake of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2 's first rate cut in two and a half years. (published 0520 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA OCTOBER PMI +5.1 POINTS TO 47.4 POINTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's manufacturing sector contracted at a slower pace in October but the sector continues to hurt from the strong exchange rate and weak demand, a performance gauge produced by an industry group published Tuesday shows. (published 2230 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: CHINA OFFICIAL PMI FALLS IN OCTOBER; OUTLOOK UNCERTAIN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's official Purchasing Managers Index fell in October contrary to the third consecutive increase forecast by economists, indicating tight monetary policy and weakening export demand have started to affect manufacturing sector growth. (published 0517 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: CHINA HOUSING PRICES POST BIGGEST SEQUENTIAL DROP SO FAR THIS YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Housing prices in China recorded their biggest sequential drop so far this year in October, a privately compiled index showed, suggesting the once red-hot sector is finally on the verge of reaching a decisive turning point after a nearly two-year-long property tightening campaign. (published 0546 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: JAPAN THREATENS FURTHER YEN ACTION, SEEKS G-20 SUPPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A day after ordering a massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister threatened further action Tuesday, saying he will make "appropriate decisions" at the right time if speculators again push the yen sharply higher, hurting Japanese exporters. (published 0315 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN ECONOMY MINISTER: TO CONTINUE TAKING DECISIVE STEPS ON \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IF NEEDED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's economy minister Motohisa Furukawa said Tuesday the government remains poised to conduct further yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves abruptly and appears to be driven by speculation. (published 0109 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: S KOREA OCTOBER EXPORTS WEAKEST IN 2 YEARS; INFLATION SLOWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's October exports growth slumped to a two-year low while inflation for the month fell within the Bank of Korea's target band for the first time this year, underscoring increasing growth risks for Asia's fourth-largest economy as the world braces for a potential global slump. (published 0238 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: INDIA SEPT EXPORTS $24.8 BLN, UP 36.3% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's merchandise exports in September climbed 36.3% from a year earlier to $24.8 billion, according to provisional data issued Tuesday by the Ministry of Commerce. (published 0521 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDIA OCTOBER \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  MANUFACTURING PMI RISES TO 52.0 VS 50.4 IN SEPT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's manufacturing activity in October expanded--though modestly--indicating an improvement in business conditions from a month ago as growth in new orders accelerated, a survey showed Tuesday. (published 0521 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TAIWAN \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  OCTOBER PMI 43.7 VS 44.5 SEPTEMBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  Taiwan Purchasing Managers Index fell to 43.7 in October from 44.5 in September, as new domestic and export orders fell substantially, \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC Holdings PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Tuesday. (published 0204 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: INDONESIA SEPTEMBER TRADE SURPLUS NARROWS TO $2.72B \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's trade surplus narrowed to $2.72 billion in September from $3.76 billion in August due to a fall in the prices of commodities globally, the official Central Statistics Agency said Tuesday. (published 0435 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: INDONESIA OCTOBER CPI +4.42% ON YEAR; +4.80% EXPECTED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's consumer price index rose 4.42% in October from a year earlier, faster than the 4.61% increase in September because of a low base of comparison, the official Central Statistics Agency said Tuesday. (published 0409 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: THAI OCTOBER CPI UP 4.19% ON YEAR; +4.5% EXPECTED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's consumer price index rose 4.19% in October from a year earlier, faster than September's 4.03% increase, Commerce Ministry Permanent Secretary Yanyong Phuangrach said Tuesday. (published 0428 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par HEARD ON THE STREET: CHINESE PROPERTY COLLAPSE 101 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It starts in the property sector, the main domestic growth driver. Government controls on speculators have already started to bring residential property prices down. National average prices for residential property fell 0.23% month-to-month in October, the second month in a row of falling prices, according to data released Tuesday by the China Real Estate Index System. (published 0440 GMT) [ 01-11-11 0600GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111101e7b1000cu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
