\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Call 08:15 GMT Hourly News Briefing\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eva Szalay   \par \uc2 656 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2011\par 09:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Snapshot:\par \par \uc2 Bunds are sharply higher, equities are lower, the euro clocked up the biggest single-day percentage drop against the dollar in more than a year in Monday's New York session while the world saw a "baby-Lehman" in \uc2 MF Global\uc2 's collapse. French banking stocks are plummeting, with \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  and \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  down near 9%. The Greek government has called for a referendum on the E.U. aid package, China's manufacturing sector is slowing and the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  eases policy rates. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday is thought to be the largest on record, with the BOJ rumoured to have splashed out a respectable Y10 trillion to stop the yen strengthening as investors fret about economic growth. And the good news? Erm...\par \par \uc2 --To receive the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Call once a day by email, go to: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://fxtrader.dowjones.com/page/fx-call-signup.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://fxtrader.dowjones.com/page/\b  \highlight22fx\b0 \highlight22 -call-signup.html}}}\par \par \uc2 Levels:\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD: 1.3775\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY: 78.10\par \par \uc2 EUR/CHF:1.2145\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD: 1.0398\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD: 1.6026\par \par \uc2 Heads Up:\par \par \uc2 N/A: FOMC starts two-day meeting\par \par \uc2 0930 GMT: UK 3Q GDP\par \par \uc2 0930 GMT: UK Oct CIPS manufacturing PMI\par \par \uc2 1400 GMT: US Oct ISM manufacturing report\par \par \uc2 1400 GMT: US Sept construction spending\par \par \uc2 2030 GMT: US Oct API statistical bulletin\par \par \uc2 Top News:\par \par \uc2 --Greek Prime Minister George Papandreou announced a surprise referendum on Greece's bailout program Monday, in a high-stakes gamble that could put his controversial austerity policies on stronger political footing but that risks toppling the country's embattled Socialist government.\par \par \uc2 --A day after ordering a massive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister threatened further action Tuesday, saying he will make "appropriate decisions" at the right time if speculators again push the yen sharply higher, hurting Japanese exporters. Jun Azumi also voiced hope that Tokyo's latest effort to weaken the yen to defend its struggling export-led economy will win backing overseas despite market expectations to the contrary.\par \par \uc2 -- China October official PMI falls to 50.4 from September's 51.2, dashing expectations of a rise to 51.7. The second straight decline shows manufacturing slowing to near contraction on Beijing's tightening measures and slowing global growth. The drop "signals that future economic growth will continue to slow down," says the China Federation of Logistics and Purchasing. But the \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI (smaller sample, mostly smaller companies) rises to 51.0 from September's 49.9. Home prices in 100 major cities fall 0.23% M/M, the deepest this year, suggesting the once red-hot sector is near a turning point.\par \par \uc2 -- The RBA eases for the first time in 2 1/2 years and says it's shifting to neutral, cutting its key rate 25 bps to 4.5%, as inflation risks fade, unemployment rises and the global outlook worsens. The cut was widely expected, but AUD/USD still falls 1.0527 just before the announcement. All forecasters in a Dow Jones snap poll see the RBA on hold in December.\par \par \uc2 -- UK Nationwide home prices rise 0.4% Y/Y, the first gain in seven months, but the weak economy continues to weigh on the prospects for the residential property market. "The outlook remains uncertain, but with the UK economic recovery expected to remain sluggish, house-price growth is likely to remain soft in the period ahead, with prices moving sideways or drifting modestly lower over the next 12 months," says Nationwide chief economist Robert Gardner.\par \par \uc2 -- China rattles the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  saber, threatening retaliation if US measures targeting Beijing's CNY policy are enacted. The Senate bill, which could slap tariffs on countries with "misaligned \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," would hurt Chinese and US interests, says He Ning, head of the Commerce Ministry's Department of American & Oceania, according to the China Daily. "We cannot ignore it and will definitely reciprocate in kind."\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020111101e7b1003xq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
