\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ(10/31)  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervenes \highlight\uc2  On Yen To Cap Sharp Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 628 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2011\par 03:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par C2\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL) \par    By Takashi Mochizuki \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO -- After keeping the market waiting for more than a week, the Japanese government launched a new round of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The government moved to cap a continued rise in the yen that is undermining the economy and dragging down the key export sector. It was the first time since Aug. 4 that Japan has stepped into the market to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came after the dollar fell to a post-World War II record low of 75.31 yen in early Asian trading.\par \par \uc2 Japanese government officials and corporate leaders have repeatedly expressed deep concern about the yen's strength and the damage it could do to the economy by hurting exporters and intensifying deflation. The government recently implemented a series of measures aimed at mitigating the effect of the yen's rise, but large exporters have piled pressure on Tokyo to do more.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Jun Azumi has repeatedly said that Tokyo would take "decisive steps" when necessary against the strong yen. He has blamed speculators for the rise in the yen, which he said did not reflect the fundamentals of the slow-growth Japanese economy.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan moved Thursday to help weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , adding 5 trillion yen to its asset purchase program of 50 trillion yen. BOJ Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa specifically cited the yen's strength as one reason for the move. Adding more money to the economy helps to lower the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At a highly anticipated summit of EU leaders on Wednesday, agreement was reached on private-sector writeoffs of 50% for holders of Greek debt, as well as on bolstering the euro zone's bailout fund.\par \par \uc2 Japanese officials had hoped that an agreement on the European problems will help stop money flowing into Japan, seen as a "safe haven" in times of global economic uncertainty. Despite the extra cash from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and progress in Brussels, the yen has remained strong, apparently prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to act on its long-threatened yen-selling in the market.\par \par \uc2 It is unclear whether this latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be able to reverse the yen's rise, given the short-term impact of the last attempt. The Japanese government spent 4.5 trillion yen, about $59 billion at current rates, in the August \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to push the dollar up. It was the country's biggest single-day action ever and quickly sent the dollar some three yen higher. But the gains proved to be short-lived and the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon hit another record low.\par \par \uc2 The government's plan to deal with the strong yen is aimed at helping smaller companies hit by the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength and increases the funding available to finance any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 46 trillion yen ($603 billion) from 31 trillion yen.\par \par \uc2 While the government is under domestic pressure to act, officials have acknowledged that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  wouldn't be seen favorably by major trading partners in Europe and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 In addition, the U.S. and other countries are pressuring China to allow greater flexibility in the value of the yuan, an effort that could be undercut if Japan is perceived to be regulating its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The issue of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  management is expected to be a leading topic at this week's meeting of G-20 leaders in Cannes, France. Japanese officials have argued that their actions are different than those taken by other countries, with only infrequent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at smoothing out sharp market moves, rather than at reversing the yen's rising trend.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 30, 2011 22:57 ET (02:57 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020111031e7av000s6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
