\par\par \b \uc2 DJ 2nd UPDATE: BOJ Gov Supports  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 , Urges  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  To Work On Own Fiscal Issues\b0\par\par\uc2 565 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2011\par 09:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- BOJ governor expresses support for government's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , hoping the action will stabilize \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market\par \par \uc2 -- Says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  also needs to work urgently on own worsening fiscal conditions\par \par \uc2 -- Warns JGB issuance would not take place smoothly if market thinks BOJ financing government debt\par \par \uc2 (Adds more Shirakawa remarks at a press conference in paragraph 6-7.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Megumi Fujikawa \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OSAKA (Dow Jones)--The Bank of Japan's governor expressed his support for the government's decision to directly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to weaken the yen, while at the same time urging it to work on its own worsening fiscal situation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The bank strongly expects that such an action will contribute to stable price formation in the (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) market," BOJ Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa told business leaders in Osaka, western Japan on Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It can be said that the recent appreciation of the yen is one example of the spillover effect of the sovereign debt problem in Europe into Japan through increasing risk aversion by global investors," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier Monday, the government launched a new round of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , moving to cap a continued rise in the yen that is undermining the economy and dragging down the key export sector.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pushed the dollar to Y79.55 from around Y75.65 before the move. The pair stood at Y78.12 as of 0850 GMT, after the yen hit a record high of Y75.31 in early Asian trading Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also views \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates as one important factor that could change the outlook for the economy and prices, although it won't steer monetary policy with an aim to directly affect movements in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, Shirakawa said at a press conference after the meeting.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The BOJ sees the yen's recent appreciation amid Japan's recovery from the March earthquake as a downside risk since it could have huge impact on corporate profits, exports and business sentiment," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To contain downside risks to the domestic economy, the BOJ decided at its latest meeting last week to expand its asset-buying fund by Y5 trillion to Y55 trillion through additional purchases of Japanese government bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But as the euro-zone debt crisis raises concerns over the credibility of government debt, Shirakawa stressed the need for Japan itself to improve its own fiscal conditions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the market isn't confident about the government's plans for fiscal rehabilitation, market participants will start to become hesitant about holding government debt, and this in turn will lead to a rise in interest rates, Shirakawa said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's national debt stands at around double the country's annual economic output--by far the largest among the world's rich nations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amid pressure on the BOJ to buy more JGBs with longer maturities, Shirakawa warned that the issuance of government bonds would not take place smoothly as is the case currently, if market participants misinterpret such an action as fiscal financing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition to the asset-purchase program, the BOJ currently buys Y1.8 trillion of JGBs every month via regular outright buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Megumi Fujikawa, Dow Jones Newswires; +81-3-6269-2786; megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 31, 2011 04:54 ET (08:54 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020111031e7av002n9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
