\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA:  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Gives  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Exporters A Break, But For How Long?\b0\par\par\uc2 1,037 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2011\par 12:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helps country's exporters as earnings demonstrate yen's sting\par \par \uc2 -- Tokyo will have to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop impact from fading\par \par \uc2 -- Major exporters welcome move but remained skeptical on long-term effects \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Andrew Monahan \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The Japanese government's massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Monday threw the country's exporters a lifeline just as many of them are reporting bad earnings and plans to move production overseas.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The amount of yen Tokyo unloaded is thought to be a record for a single day. While Finance Minister Jun Azumi would not comment on the price tag for Monday's move, traders said Japan likely sold a total of around Y7 trillion, which would far exceed the estimated Y4.5 trillion sold in the last \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Aug. 4. The government apparently held its fire once European trade began, confining the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to Asian trading hours.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  initially pushed the greenback up as much as 5% against the yen, to Y79.55, its highest since the August effort. But the yen quickly recouped some of its losses, as Japanese exporters and other investors took advantage of its sharp fall to buy it at attractive levels.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The sharp drop in the yen was particularly welcome as it came at the end of the month, when many Japanese exporters need to buy the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for regular settlements. They suddenly got a lot more yen for their dollars, after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sent the greenback as high as Y79.55, from the record low it had hit earlier in the day at Y75.31.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But if Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda wants to stave off the serious threat of renewed yen strength, his government will have to keep shelling out big time. Even then, Japan will remain captive to forces largely beyond its control, as the safe-haven yen could again surge on flare-ups in the European debt crisis or any Federal Reserve easing, traders say. Investors often buy the yen when global financial markets are turbulent, as Japan's large current account surplus and persistent deflation make the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  a safe place to "park" funds, despite Japan's own fiscal problems.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Failure to give Japan's export-driven economy more than a fleeting respite from a strong yen would add to headaches for Noda's government, as it struggles with a split parliament that has slowed the response to the March 11 earthquake, tsunami and nuclear reactor accident.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The strong yen makes Japan's electronics, televisions and other goods less competitive overseas and diminishes overseas earnings repatriated to Japan. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recent rises have jeopardized the rapid progress made recovering from supply chain shocks in the wake of the March disasters. It has clouded earnings forecasts, already scaled down due to sluggishness in the U.S., China and Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The guarded reaction to Monday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's key technology and auto firms reflected skepticism it would bring long-term benefits, even as these firms rushed to sell their suddenly more valuable dollars and euros for yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Executive vice president Makoto Kubo at Japanese electronics conglomerate \f28 \uc2 \f2 Toshiba Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (6502.TO) called Monday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "extremely positive," but also said that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  alone can't steer the market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earnings statements from other firms revealed how much damage the soaring \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has already done.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TDK Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (6762.TO), a manufacturer of electronic materials and recording media, said it will cut about 12% of its global workforce, in part due to the strong yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speaking after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Noda said the move was meant "to keep downside risks to the Japanese economy from materializing." The premiere has said steps his government recently announced to help firms hurt by the strong yen are meant to keep Japanese industry from "hollowing out." The appeal of offshore production, however, is unlikely to fade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Electronics giant \f28 \uc2 \f2 Panasonic Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . offered a stark reminder of that dynamic, suggesting it still prefers put its yen to work overseas to avoid \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange losses. "It's extremely difficult to make new business investments in Japan now," Chief Financial Officer \f28 \uc2 \f2 Makoto Uenoyama\f28 \uc2 \f2  said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While companies gird for more strong yen-challenges, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which gives the orders on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , has already come under fire for ordering up Monday's move.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  executive board member Jose Manuel Gonzalez-Paramo said Monday that while \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can do as it pleases, unilateral \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have negative effects on global stability.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa reminded any critical Europeans that their debt problems have been a driver of the yen's gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It can be said that the recent appreciation of the yen is one example of the spillover effect of the sovereign debt problem in Europe into Japan through increasing risk aversion by global investors," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Whether the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can help stabilize the yen as Shirakawa also said, however, isn't clear. Uncertainty persists over Europe despite the continent's leaders last week reaching agreement on a framework plan to keep debt problems from spreading, analysts say. Persistent speculation that the Federal Reserve could implement further monetary easing, even if it passes on such a step at this week's policy-setting Open Market Committee meeting, will also keep weighing on the dollar against the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Doubts are already creeping in about the euro-zone agreement last week while some Fed officials are highlighting the prospects of more asset purchases," said Mitul Kotecha, head of global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at French bank \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Agricole\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's actions will likely come up at this week's meeting of leaders of the Group of 20 industrialized and emerging economies in Cannes, France. The U.S. and other countries are expected to pressure China to allow greater flexibility in the value of the yuan, an effort that could be undercut if Japan is perceived to be regulating its own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Andrew Monahan, Dow Jones Newswires; 81-3-6269-2783; andrew.monahan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Juro Osawa contributed to this article.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 31, 2011 07:59 ET (11:59 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020111031e7av003vq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
