\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Dollar's many woes complicate  \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Steven C. Johnson  \par \uc2 1,028 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2011\par 20:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 31 (Reuters) - When it comes to weakening the yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators are the least of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's problems.\par \par \uc2 That's because when policymakers \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to limit yen strength, as they did Monday, they square off against a formidable array of forces, including U.S. monetary policy, Chinese reserve managers and global investors from Texas to Tokyo united by one desire: to sell the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Investors and market analysts say that explains why prior efforts to weaken the yen against the dollar have failed and why the chances of success this time around are equally slim.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the third time this year after the yen hit a record high of 75.31 per dollar, spending an estimated $65 billion and at one point pushing it some 5 percent lower.\par \par \uc2 More may be on the way, particularly after data last week showed speculators had doubled their bets in favor of the yen in the week to Oct. 25, the highest since around the last time policymakers \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August.\par \par \uc2 Few expect much bang for the buck, though. Japan's move failed to push the dollar above 80 yen, and analysts at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  forecast the dollar would soon return to 75-76 yen.\par \par \uc2 "This is not necessarily about independent yen strength or speculative forces driving it up, though I appreciate there's an element of that," said Simon Derrick, a strategist at BNY Mellon in London.\par \par \uc2 "Rather this is about broad-based dollar weakness. We are in the midst of a 10-year dollar downtrend and there are no signs the forces that have driven that are about to change."\par \par \uc2 Japan is an export-oriented economy and a strong yen makes products more expensive abroad -- the last thing needed for an already weak economy that was ravaged in March by a major earthquake, a tsunami and a nuclear disaster.\par \par \uc2 But Derrick notes that not even the severity of that disaster and the blow it dealt Japan's economy interrupted the dollar's steady decline against the yen.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the yen soared to what was then a record high against the dollar after the earthquake, prompting official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  a few days later.\par \par \uc2 And the pattern is much the same elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 "We've had something close to an existential crisis in the euro zone. We had the UK \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  print more money," he said. "But as with the yen, both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are doing well against the dollar. That tells you a lot about the problems facing the dollar."\par \par \uc2 UP AGAINST THE FED\par \par \uc2 Though up broadly on Monday, the dollar has shed nearly 4 percent against six major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this year and is down more than 30 percent since the start of 2001.\par \par \uc2 One impediment has been loose monetary policy. The Federal Reserve recently pledged to hold interest rates at zero until at least 2013 and the debate about more easing has heated up.\par \par \uc2 Several policymakers have talked about adding to the $2.3 trillion the Fed has already poured into the financial system by resuming purchases of mortgage-backed bonds.\par \par \uc2 While U.S. economic data has shown signs of improvement in recent weeks, economists say growth remains well below the pace needed to make a real dent in a 9.1 percent jobless rate.\par \par \uc2 "The Fed hasn't changed its stance, and that's really the problem," said Stephen Jen, president of London-based hedge fund SLJ Macro. "It's more likely than not that we will see QE3 eventually, so (Japan) may have done this preemptively, knowing the Fed is probably gearing up to take action again."\par \par \uc2 Some also fear the euro zone is on the verge of recession, and with most countries being forced to tighten their fiscal belts to rein in large deficits, markets expect the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut interest rates by year end.\par \par \uc2 As a result, Japanese investors may be content to park their money in Japanese government debt for safe keeping.\par \par \uc2 That matters, Jen said, because Japanese investors play a large role in driving the yen exchange rate. With interest rates at or near zero for more than a decade, retail investors typically seek higher returns abroad when risk appetite is high. But when opportunities fade, that money comes home.\par \par \uc2 "The dollar is such an international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that its trajectory is not dictated by American investors but that's not so with the yen," he said. "As soon as Japanese investors have second thoughts about investments overseas, you have pressure in the dollar-yen exchange rate."\par \par \uc2 U.S. DEFICIT BACK IN FOCUS\par \par \uc2 There may be other reasons to avoid the dollar in the months ahead, especially if there is a breakdown in the politically tense negotiations about how to shave $1.5 trillion from the U.S. budget deficit over the next decade.\par \par \uc2 If a Nov. 23 deadline comes and goes, that would trigger automatic cuts and, some fear, prompt another ratings agency to cut the United States top AAA rating. For details, see [ID:nN1E79L08V]\par \par \uc2 If that happens, Derrick said China and other large holders of dollars could increase efforts to diversify their massive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, keeping pressure on the dollar and complicating Japanese efforts to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 Japan could declare a level beyond which it would not let the dollar fall. That's worked for Switzerland, which struggled for much of the year to contain massive franc appreciation against the euro as the euro zone debt crisis deepened.\par \par \uc2 But that probably wouldn't win Japan any friends at this week's summit of Group of 20 leaders in France.\par \par \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  called such a policy for the world's fourth largest exporter and third largest economy, "politically unacceptable globally," adding it could prompt other export-led Asian countries to follow suit, "something both the U.S. and Europe are loath to see."\par \par \uc2 "So this looks like a one-off unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Mark McCormick, a strategist at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2 . "Historical precedent suggests it will be ineffective."\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Chizu Nomiyama)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ANALYSIS)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111103e7av001gn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
