\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Euro zone jitters hurt stocks; dollar jumps\b0\par\par\uc2 1,041 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2011\par 15:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Dollar rises 3 pct vs yen after Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * S&P 500 on track for best month in nearly 20 years\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 MF Global\uc2  goes bankrupt on worries about European bets\par \par \uc2 * German Bunds, U.S. Treasuries rally on safe-haven bids\par \par \uc2 (Recasts lead, updates with closing U.S. prices)\par \par \uc2 By Richard Leong\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Reuters) - Global stock markets tumbled Monday as renewed doubts about the euro zone's debt crisis plan last week put an abrupt end to their October rally.\par \par \uc2 A stronger dollar following \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen also stoked selling in equities, commodities and other risky assets and buying in low-risk government bonds.\par \par \uc2 Worries about the European debt crisis took a toll on \uc2 MF Global\uc2 . The U.S. futures broker, whose heavy bet on the region's debt sent its shares plummeting in recent days, filed for bankruptcy Monday.\par \par \uc2 MF's bankruptcy, while not a catalyst in the stock market sell-off, was a sobering reminder that any exposure to the euro zone remains risky until its debt crisis is resolved.\par \par \uc2 Despite the day's losses, the benchmark Standard & Poor's 500 index was poised to gain 10.8 percent on the month, its biggest percentage rise since December 1991.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's rise "is part of it, but a lot of it is also rethinking Europe. There was a lot of ebullience after the meeting and that is starting to fade a bit," said Stephen Massocca, managing director of Wedbush Morgan in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 Fading optimism on a lasting cure for the euro zone's debt woes knocked down oil and other commodity prices.\par \par \uc2 U.S. crude oil futures settled down 13 cents at $93.19 a barrel, partially recovering from a 1.4 percent drop. The greenback earlier jumped to a three-month high against the yen, making dollar-priced commodities more expensive for investors holding other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Lower metal prices from a firmer dollar hurt mining stocks, while banking shares succumbed to selling on renewed doubts over European leaders' plan to prevent their sovereign debt problem from spiraling into a global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 "After a solid month of gains, the (higher) dollar is giving traders a reason to shy (away) from the risk trade and take some profits," said Peter Cardillo, chief market economist at Rockwell Global Capital in New York.\par \par \uc2 Japan sold the yen for the second time in less than three months, saying it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to counter speculative moves that were hurting the world's third-biggest economy. Traders estimated the Bank of Japan could have purchased $65 billion to $75 billion against its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dollar, which had fallen to a record low of 75.31 yen\par \par \uc2 earlier in Asian trade, rose more than 4 percent to as high as 79.55 yen. It was last up 3.1 percent at 78.18 yen, with traders saying more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely be needed for a more durable impact.\par \par \uc2 The euro gave up most of last week's gains on the dollar's advance. It was last down 2.2 percent at $1.3844, retreating further from a seven-week high around $1.4247 last Thursday on news of the euro zone's debt-rescue plan.\par \par \uc2 The euro zone common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  looked to end October up 3.4 percent, for its best monthly performance in six months. But speculation about a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rate cut Thursday could limit gains for now.\par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN DEBT PLAN DOUBTS\par \par \uc2 Japan told the head of Europe's bailout fund Monday that it would continue to buy its bonds. But like fellow potential investor China, it did not commit to putting cash into a proposed special purpose vehicle to enhance the rescue fund's firepower.\par \par \uc2 With the decline in the euro, equities gave back some of last week's gains, with some analysts expecting the market pullback to be temporary.\par \par \uc2 "It's nothing more than we were overbought," said Jeffrey Saut, chief investment strategist at Raymond James in St. Petersburg, Florida. "We are going to see professional money chasing equities higher into the end of the year."\par \par \uc2 The MSCI world equity index dropped 2.4 percent, pulling back from its highest levels in nearly three months hit last week. It is on track to gain 10 percent in October -- the biggest one-month rise since April 2009.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks fell as the spike in the U.S. dollar weighed on commodity prices and dried up bids for other risky assets.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones industrial average lost 276.10 points, or 2.26 percent, to end at 11,955.01. The Standard & Poor's 500 Index was down 31.79 points, or 2.47 percent, at 1,253.30. The Nasdaq Composite Index fell 52.74 points, or 1.93 percent, to 2,684.41.\par \par \uc2 The pan-European FTSEurofirst 300 index closed down 2.2 percent but managed to end up 7.9 percent for October, the biggest one-month rise since July 2009.\par \par \uc2 In Tokyo, the Nikkei rose 3.3 percent in October, the biggest monthly percentage rise in eight months\par \par \uc2 The stock market sell-off rekindled bids for bonds. U.S. and German government debt prices advanced as peripheral euro zone government debt came under renewed pressure.\par \par \uc2 Amid worries about \uc2 MF Global\uc2 's euro zone exposure, Italian 10-year government bond yields climbed back above 6 percent to levels last seen in August before the ECB stepped in to buy Spanish and Italian debt in the secondary market.\par \par \uc2 German Bund futures jumped almost 3 points to 136.57, while the U.S. 30-year Treasury bond rallied about 5 points in price to yield 3.14 percent. The 30-year U.S. yield rose 22 basis points, the biggest monthly yield jump since January.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold prices fell as the spike in the dollar spooked precious metals investors. Spot gold was last down 1.5 percent at $1,723.71 an ounce, reducing its monthly gain to 5.7 percent.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Chuck Mikolajczak and Gertrude Chavez-Dreyfuss in New York and Kirsten Donovan and Brian Gorman in London; Editing by Dan Grebler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-GLOBAL (WRAPUP 12)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111103e7av0011w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
