\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Stronger dollar hurts stocks, commodities\b0\par\par\uc2 843 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2011\par 15:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Dollar gains 3 pct vs yen after Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 MF Global\uc2  goes bankrupt on worries about European bets\par \par \uc2 * U.S. crude futures shed 0.8 pct; gold falls over 1 pct\par \par \uc2 * German Bunds, U.S. Treasuries rise on safe-haven bids\par \par \uc2 (Recasts, updates market action)\par \par \uc2 By Richard Leong\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Reuters) - World equities fell Monday as commodity shares sank on a stronger dollar following \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 Rising doubts about the euro zone's plan to stem its debt crisis also weighed on investor sentiment.\par \par \uc2 Worries about the European debt crisis took a toll on \uc2 MF Global\uc2  . The U.S. futures broker, whose heavy bet on the region's debt sent its shares plummeting in recent days, filed for bankruptcy Monday.\par \par \uc2 U.S. crude oil futures shed 1.4 percent as the greenback jumped to a three-month high against the yen, making dollar-priced commodities more expensive for investors holding other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Lower metal prices from a firmer dollar hurt mining stocks, while banking shares succumbed to selling on renewed doubts over European leaders' plan to prevent their sovereign debt problem from spiraling into a global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 "After a solid month of gains, the (higher) dollar is giving traders a reason to shy from the risk trade and take some profits," said Peter Cardillo, chief market economist at Rockwell Global Capital in New York.\par \par \uc2 Japan sold the yen for the second time in less than three months, saying it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to counter speculative moves that were hurting the world's third-biggest economy. Traders estimated the Bank of Japan could have purchased $65 billion to $75 billion against its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dollar, which had fallen to a record low of 75.31 yen earlier in Asian trade, rose more than 4 percent to as high as 79.55 yen. It was last up 2.9 percent at 78.02 yen, with traders saying more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely be needed for a more durable impact.\par \par \uc2 The euro gave up most of last week's gains on the dollar's advance. It was last down 1.4 percent at $1.3954 , retreating further from a seven-week high around $1.4247 last Thursday on news of the euro zone's debt-rescue plan.\par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  still looked set to end the month up nearly 5 percent for its best monthly performance in just over a year. But speculation about an interest rate cut Thursday by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could limit its gains for now.\par \par \uc2 The dollar might also come under pressure with Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke likely to repeat his worries about the slow pace of economic recovery when the Fed ends its two-day policy meeting Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN DEBT PLAN DOUBTS\par \par \uc2 Japan told the head of Europe's bailout fund Monday that it would continue to buy its bonds. But like fellow potential investor China, it did not commit to putting cash into a mooted special purpose vehicle to enhance the rescue fund's firepower.\par \par \uc2 With the decline in the euro, equities gave back some of last week's gains.\par \par \uc2 "Last week we saw a huge rise in equity markets largely on the revelation of a structure of a plan, with no detail on the funding," said Jeremy Batstone-Carr, a strategist at Charles Stanley in London.\par \par \uc2 The MSCI world equity index dropped 2 percent, pulling back from its highest levels in nearly three months hit last week.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks fell as the spike in the U.S. dollar weighed on commodity prices and dried up bids on other risky assets.\par \par \uc2 Around noon , the Dow Jones industrial average was down 150.46 points, or 1.23 percent, at 12,080.65. The Standard & Poor's 500 Index was down 17.04 points, or 1.33 percent, at 1,268.05. The Nasdaq Composite Index was down 29.67 points, or 1.08 percent, at 2,707.48.\par \par \uc2 The pan-European FTSEurofirst 300 index fell 1.4 percent after rising 4.1 percent last week, while emerging stocks slipped 1.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 The stock market sell-off rekindled bids for bonds. U.S. and German government debt prices advanced as peripheral euro zone government debt came under renewed pressure.\par \par \uc2 Italian 10-year government bond yields climbed back above 6 percent to levels last seen in August before the ECB stepped in to buy Spanish and Italian debt in the secondary market.\par \par \uc2 German Bund futures jumped 1.6 points to 135.27, while the U.S. 30-year Treasury bond rallied nearly 3 points in price to yield 3.24 percent.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold prices fell more than 1 percent as the spike in the dollar spooked precious metals investors. Spot gold was last down 0.9 percent at $1,725.99 an ounce, halving its earlier decline.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Rodrigo Campos in New York; Emelia Sithole-Matarise, Jessica Mortimer, Kirsten Donovan and Brian Gorman in London; Editing by Dan Grebler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-GLOBAL (WRAPUP 9)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111103e7av0011q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
