\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-BOJ Shirakawa urges action to avert global crisis\b0\par\par\uc2 453 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2011\par 08:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Hope \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  leads to stable \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  - Shirakawa\par \par \uc2 * Adds BOJ always thinking of what more it can do\par \par \uc2 * Global slowdown, yen rise to weigh on Japan growth\par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, detail)\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2 OSAKA, Oct 31 (Reuters) - Bank of Japan Governor Masaaki Shirakawa said policymakers need to make every effort to prevent Europe's debt woes from triggering another global financial crisis, issuing his strongest warning to date of the risk posed to the world economy ahead of a Group of 20 summit in Cannes.\par \par \uc2 Shirakawa also expressed hope that Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday would ease yen rises driven largely by heightened uncertainty over the global economic outlook.\par \par \uc2 "What is most important in the immediate future is to fully ensure financial market stability and prevent (Europe's) problem from developing into a global financial crisis akin to the one engendered by the Lehman shock," he said in a speech to business leaders in Osaka, western Japan, on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Europe has made some progress in dealing with its debt woes but must strengthen efforts on laying out details to regain market trust over the region's financial system, he said.\par \par \uc2 On Japan's economy, Shirakawa said growth was expected to remain moderate for the time being due to the global slowdown and the yen's strength, but will then resume a moderate recovery.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ has already taken bold monetary easing steps, such as buying corporate debt under its asset-buying programme, but is always considering what more it can do to support the economy, Shirakawa said.\par \par \uc2 "I won't say there is nothing more monetary policy can do," Shirakawa said. He added, however, that monetary easing alone cannot pull Japan out of deflation and that efforts by the government and private sector were also needed to boost \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's potential to grow.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a record high against the dollar, saying it acted to counter speculative moves that did not reflect the health of the Japanese economy.\par \par \uc2 The move came after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week eased monetary policy by boosting asset purchases, spurred by the yen's rises to record highs, the global economic slowdown and Europe's debt crisis, which threatened Japan's recovery prospects.\par \par \uc2 "Recent yen rises can be explained as an example of Europe's sovereign debt problem affecting Japan by strengthening global investors' risk aversion," Shirakawa said.\par \par \uc2 "Under such circumstances, the finance ministry \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market today. The BOJ strongly hopes that such moves will lead to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market stability," he said.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Edmund Klamann and Joseph Radford)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/BOJ (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111103e7av000cl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
