\par\par \b \uc2 PRECIOUS-Gold eases as dollar soars on  \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 843 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2011\par 12:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Gold slides as dollar gains from BOJ's yen selling\par \par \uc2 * Near-term outlook dominated by macro picture\par \par \uc2 * Coming up: U.S. manufacturing data on Tuesday\par \par \uc2 (Changes dateline, previously NEW YORK/LONDON, updates prices, adds analyst comments)\par \par \uc2 By Chris Kelly\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, (Reuters) - Gold lost nearly 1 percent in thinner-than-usual trade Monday, after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen sent the dollar soaring and commodities and other risk assets into a tailspin at the start of the week.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -led selling pulled bullion down for a second straight day, extending a phase of consolidation from last week's short-covering surge that had lifted the price to its highest in more than a month, above $1,750 an ounce.\par \par \uc2 Despite the loss, gold posted a 6 percent gain for this month, recovering from a near-11 percent slide in September, when prices hit a record $1,920.30.\par \par \uc2 "The movements in the dollar ... you have to chalk that up as the number-one negative for commodities in general on the day," said Bill O'Neill, partner of LOGIC Advisors in Upper Saddle River, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 "It's more of a risk-off day ... gold has become a very choppy, volatile trading affair, which makes it a little less attractive as a flight-to-safety vehicle," he said.\par \par \uc2 Bullion slipped as the greenback jumped to a three-month high against the yen after the government of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen's strength, making dollar-priced commodities more expensive for investors holding other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Helping gold recover some of the day's losses was a decision by the New York Federal Reserve to suspend conducting business with brokerage \uc2 MF Global\uc2 , which filed for bankruptcy protection after a tentative deal to find a buyer fell apart.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was down 1.1 percent at $1,722.65 an ounce at 2:46 p.m. EDT.\par \par \uc2 In New York, the benchmark December COMEX contract shed $22 or about 1.3 percent, to settle at $1,725.20 an ounce, after dealing in a range from $1,705.50 to $1,746.50.\par \par \uc2 Futures volumes stood at around 118,000 lots in late New York trade, about 40 percent below the 30-day norm, according to Thomson Reuters preliminary data.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 MF Global\uc2  was not a big surprise. Clearly it's not a bank, it's not on the scale of \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2 , but that is what helped the (gold) market to bounce off today's lows," VTB Capital analyst Andrey Kryuchenkov said.\par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, investors will turn their attention to a batch of economic data and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy meetings, which may give the market a better sense of direction.\par \par \uc2 "This week, it's all about the economic data and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy meetings," said Mitsubishi analyst Matthew Turner.\par \par \uc2 "There's no real sense of direction, except for the fact that it is trading more like a risk asset."\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Federal Reserve and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meet this week to discuss monetary policy, while the Group of 20 industrialized nations meet in the French city of Cannes to devise a plan to stabilize markets.\par \par \uc2 The correlation between gold and the European equity market rose to a multi-month high of nearly 50 percent, meaning gold was more likely to move in tandem with stocks, while gold's negative correlation to the dollar strengthened to -40 percent on Monday from around -30 percent last week.\par \par \uc2 BUYERS CAUTIOUS\par \par \uc2 Buyers in the physical market were on the sidelines, which led to gold bar premiums easing to a range between $1 and $1.50 an ounce over spot prices, from about $1.50 last week.\par \par \uc2 "We saw some light buying from Thailand," said a Singapore-based dealer. "If prices drop below $1,700, physical buyers are expected to return."\par \par \uc2 Investment interest in gold has been rekindled in recent weeks after euro zone leaders progressed toward an agreement to solve the region's debt crisis, albeit painstakingly, which pushed up the euro and as a result, helped send the gold price up 6 percent last week.\par \par \uc2 Last week, money managers raised their bullish bets in gold futures and options to the highest in four weeks, according to data from the U.S. Commodity Futures Trading Commission.\par \par \uc2 In exchange-traded fund flows, global holdings of gold ended last week with a 543,000-ounce inflow, the largest weekly increase since the week ending Aug. 19.\par \par \uc2 So far in October, global gold ETF holdings have risen by more than 714,000 ounces to their highest in five weeks at 67.769 million ounces. This would be the first monthly rise in holdings since July.\par \par \uc2 In other precious metals, platinum was last down over 2 percent at $1,607.75, while palladium was down at $648.72 and silver shed more than 2 percent to $34.22 an ounce.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Amanda Cooper in London; editing by Jason Neely and Dale Hudson)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-PRECIOUS/ (UPDATE 8)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111031e7av00180\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
