\par\par \b \uc2 REFILE-TIMELINE-Japan battles strong yen\b0\par\par\uc2 1,261 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2011\par 06:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Refiles to correct dateline to Oct 31)\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 31 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  unilaterally in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to weaken the yen after it scaled another record high against the dollar on Monday, Finance Minister Jun Azumi said.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo's foray into the market -- its second in less than three months -- follows repeated warnings by policymakers that they were ready to act to prevent the yen's strength from hurting the world's third-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 Here are some milestones in the yen's 140-year history:\par \par \uc2 1871 - The yen becomes Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of the Meiji Restoration, which marked the start of Japan's modernisation and opening to the rest of the world. Japan adopts the gold standard.\par \par \uc2 1949 - After World War Two the dollar's fixed rate is set at 360 yen via the Bretton Woods system, partly to help stabilise prices in the Japanese economy.\par \par \uc2 1959 - The dollar/yen exchange rate is liberalised. The margin of fluctuation is set at 0.5 percent on either side of its dollar parity.\par \par \uc2 1963 - The margin of fluctuation is widened to 0.75 percent.\par \par \uc2 1971 - The United States abandons the gold standard. The end of the Bretton Woods system of fixed exchange rates forces a realignment of world \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dec. 1971 - The Smithsonian Agreement sets the dollar/yen exchange rate at 308 yen, and allows it to fluctuate in a wider band between 301.07 yen and 314.93 yen.\par \par \uc2 1973 - Japanese monetary authorities decide to let the yen float freely against the greenback, and it appreciates as far as 263 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 1978 - The yen pushes through 200 to the dollar for the first time, strengthening as far as 177.\par \par \uc2 1980 to 1985 - The yen's appreciation halts and partially reverses despite Japan's big trade surpluses. Higher U.S. interest rates see Japanese investors put money in dollar assets.\par \par \uc2 1985 - The Group of Five industrial nations, the predecessor to the G7, sign the Plaza Accord in which they agree that the dollar is overvalued and that they will move to weaken it. The yen climbs from its pre-accord level around 240 to 211 in October and 200 in November, a 20 percent rise in just a few months.\par \par \uc2 1986 - The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falls further to around 190 yen in January, 167 yen in April and 153 yen in August.\par \par \uc2 1987 - In February, six of the G7 nations sign the Louvre Accord, which aims to stabilise \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and halt the dollar's broad decline. The dollar still falls from near 153 to 137 in April and 120.80 by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 1988 - On Jan. 4, the dollar falls to a post-war low of 120.45 yen in Tokyo trade, a level that holds as the low for more than five years. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars and sell yen that day on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Aug. 17, 1993 - The dollar declines to a new post-war low of 100.40 yen in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 June 21, 1994 - The dollar falls through the key 100 yen level and touches a record postwar trough of 99.85 yen in New York trade before finishing at 100.30 yen.\par \par \uc2 April 19, 1995 - The dollar hits a record postwar low at 79.75 yen after U.S.-Japanese trade frictions spark heavy selling. By the end of the year it is near 103.40.\par \par \uc2 1998 - The Asian financial crisis sees the yen weaken to nearly 148 to the dollar in August, even after U.S. authorities join the Bank of Japan to buy yen, spending $833 million, in June.\par \par \uc2 In October, the dollar tumbles from near 136 yen to 111.50 yen, as carry trades unwind following the near-collapse of hedge fund major \uc2 Long-Term Capital Management\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 1999 - The yen strengthens further despite repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , reaching 102 in November.\par \par \uc2 2001 - Following the Sept. 11 attacks on the United States, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for dollars.\par \par \uc2 2003 - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  begins massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the yen's rise against the dollar, partly to shield Japanese exporters as the economy remains stuck in its post-bubble slump and deflation. The MOF spends 20.4 trillion yen ($200 billion) over the year, nearly all of it to buy dollars and sell yen.\par \par \uc2 2004 - The MOF spends 14.8 trillion yen ($145 billion)\b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first quarter of the year, including 1.67 trillion yen buying dollars on Jan. 9 alone.\par \par \uc2 2005 - The yen hits a high of 101.67 yen in January but then falls, marking 121.40 in December. Yen carry trades and Japanese investors shifting funds into foreign assets drive the slide.\par \par \uc2 June 2007 - The dollar hits a 4-1/2 year high of 124.14 yen.\par \par \uc2 July 2007 - The yen's broad depreciation takes it to a 22-year low on a real effective exchange rate (REER) basis. Since January 2005 the yen loses 25 percent of its value on a REER basis.\par \par \uc2 October 2008 - The yen hits a 13-year high of 90.87 against the dollar, with its surge prompting the G7 to issue a statement singling out the yen in warning on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market volatility.\par \par \uc2 December 2008 - The dollar falls through 90 yen for the first time in 13 years after a bill to rescue U.S. automakers fails in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 September 2009 - The yen marks an eight-month high of 88.23 against the greenback, but later loses ground as Japan's finance minister tries to tone down earlier comments suggesting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely.\par \par \uc2 October 2009 - The yen reaches a fresh eight-month high versus the dollar of 88.01 as market players probe how far Japanese authorities will allow the yen to rise.\par \par \uc2 Nov. 27, 2009 - The yen hits a 14-year high of 84.82 on trading platform EBS.\par \par \uc2 Sept. 15, 2010 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the first time in six years, selling yen to stem a rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the dollar hits a 15-year-low at 82.87 yen.\par \par \uc2 March 18, 2011 - G7 nations jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem yen strength when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  spikes to a record high of 76.25 yen to the dollar in the aftermath of a massive earthquake, on speculation that Japanese firms would repatriate some of their huge foreign assets to pay for reconstruction.\par \par \uc2 Aug. 4 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  unilaterally to curb the yen's gains that officials fear threaten to derail the economy's recovery from a slump triggered by the earthquake in March.\par \par \uc2 Aug. 19 - The dollar hits a record low of 75.94 yen.\par \par \uc2 Oct. 26 - The greenback falls to a record low of 75.71 yen, with investors going short on the dollar on expectations the Federal Reserve will opt for more monetary easing.\par \par \uc2 Oct. 27 - The yen reaches yet another all-time high of around 75.66 to the dollar, even after further monetary policy easing by the Bank of Japan.\par \par \uc2 Oct. 28 - Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda says the government will take all steps available and work with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent a strong yen from driving companies abroad.\par \par \uc2 Oct. 31 - Finance Minister Jun Azumi says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  unilaterally to weaken the yen, after it scaled a record high of 75.31 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Sources: Reuters, Bank of Japan, Bank of England\par \par \uc2 (Writing by Mathew Veedon and Eric Burroughs; Editing by Joseph Radford)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /YEN TIMELINE (CORRECTED)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111031e7av00075\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
