\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Slide On Doubts About Europe\b0\par\par\uc2 2,685 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2011\par 23:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA       11955.01  -276.10  -2.26% \par Nasdaq      2684.41   -52.74  -1.93% \par S&P 500     1253.30   -31.79  -2.47% \par FTSE 100    5544.22  -158.02  -2.77% \par Xetra DAX   6141.34  -204.85  -3.23% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  100 0/32    +3/32 0.250%  -4.5BP \par 5Y Tsy  100 3/32   +22/32 0.981% -14.0BP \par 10Y Tsy 99 31/32 +1 16/32 2.130% -17.4BP \par  \par USD/JPY    78.15-18  +3.33% \par Range      79.55-75.58 \par EUR/USD    1.3855-58  -2.02% \par Range      1.4170-1.3830 \par AUD/USD    1.0524-28  -1.74% \par Range      1.0712-1.0508 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol     9.50/11.81% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    12.75/14.30% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    14.10/16.10% \par  \par Comex Gold         $1,719.10 -$28.10 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $93.19   -$0.13 \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par    STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks finished a historic month of gains on a sour note, as concerns about the plan to stave off Europe's debt woes drove a selloff Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average shed 276.10 points, or 2.26%, to 11955.01. Despite the losses, the blue-chip measure finished October up 9.5%, its third-biggest monthly percentage gain in its 115-year history.\par \par \uc2 All 30 components finished the month in positive territory for the first time since September 2010, led by a 28% gain by Caterpillar and 21% rise by DuPont. The index snapped a string of five straight months of losses.\par \par \uc2 But Monday's decline was the biggest one-day percentage drop since Oct. 3, which marked the most recent bottom for stocks. \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  fell 52 cents, or 7.1%, to 6.83, while \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2  declined $1.93, or 5.3%, to 34.76.\par \par \uc2 "Doubt seems to be creeping back into the market," said Jim Dunigan, managing executive of investments at PNC Wealth Management. "You're not going to solve Europe's problems by waving the magic wand and expecting it to be over."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index slid 31.79 points, or 2.47%, to 1253.30. It finished the month up 11%, its largest monthly percentage gain since December 1991. This marked the first month that the index has gone without two consecutive days of declines since October 2006.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite declined 52.74 points, or 1.93%, to 2684.41. The technology-oriented index registered its best monthly percentage gain since September 2010.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Greece Prime Minister George Papandreou called for a popular referendum on the European Union aid-and-debt deal, a disclosure which created some doubt about the deal's approval and which caught some market participants by surprise.\par \par \uc2 "We're obviously very sensitive to the European markets," said Stephen Leuer, a floor trader at X-FA Trading. "Maybe the deal is in jeopardy, or maybe it's simply not a sure thing. Any sort of doubt is definitely going to set us back."\par \par \uc2 Investors initially cheered the euro zone's plan last week to combat Greece's debt problems, expand a bailout fund and recapitalize the region's biggest banks.\par \par \uc2 But the plan still faces many questions and any hiccups could threaten to derail the recent rally.\par \par \uc2 "People are coming to the realization that, with the market being up as much as it is this month, it's prudent to take some chips off the table," said Maury Fertig, chief investment officer at Relative Value Partners.\par \par \uc2 In corporate news, trading in shares of \uc2 MF Global Holdings\uc2  was halted. The broker-dealer, run by \uc2 Jon Corzine\uc2 , filed for bankruptcy protection in a New York court. It had a tentative deal to sell assets to Interactive Brokers Group as of late Sunday, but the agreement fell apart as talks continued overnight, said people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 MF Global\uc2 's stock has plummeted this month, while yields on its bonds have soared, as the company has reeled from its exposure to European sovereign debt. The stock closed Friday down 16% at $1.20. Shares are down 71% this month and 86% in 2011.\par \par \uc2 Jefferies Group\uc2  said its exposure to \uc2 MF Global\uc2 's debt securities is less than $9 million in marked-to-market positions, which it says "resulted from facilitating client orders as part of normal-course market making." Shares dropped 1.46, or 9.9%, to 13.26.\par \par \uc2 Capping off a volatile month, Asian stocks ended mostly lower Monday, with regional resources stocks falling as the U.S. dollar was broadly higher after Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda said the government conducted the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ward off damage to the export-driven economy from the yen's strength.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed the dollar as much as 5% higher to Y79.55 from around Y75.65 before the move, after it touched a post-World War II record low of Y75.31 in early Asian trading. Copper and gold prices were also down over 2.0% as the dollar's rise makes the metals appear costly for holders of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mostly higher for October as European leaders hammered out an outline plan to address the region's debt problems, but key details were still awaited.\par \par \uc2 "With more questions than answers markets will be hungry for further details over coming weeks and until then it is difficult to see risk appetite stretching too far," said Mitul Kotecha, strategist at \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  in a note.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei Stock Average ended down 0.7% after choppy trade, with the index up 3.3% for the month, but still down 12.1% for the year. Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index declined 0.8%, but finished the month up 12.9%, while China's Shanghai Composite Index was 0.2% lower on the day and up 4.6% for October.\par \par \uc2 Australia's S&P/ASX 200 was down 1.3%, but up 7.2% for the month, while South Korea's Kospi Composite lost 1.1%, but still was up 8.0% in October.\par \par \uc2 Qantas Airways bucked the Sydney sell-off, rising 4.4% after Australia's workplace umpire Fair Work Australia ordered the termination of industrial action at the company early Monday morning. Analysts said while the decision was good for Qantas, bringing some certainty to its business and the chance to deal with its structural and flexibility issues, concerns around competition it faces from airlines with lower cost bases remained. In addition, Dow Jones Newswires reported the head of Australia's baggage-handlers' union said it considered "civil disobedience," such as sit-ins or road blockages, as an option in the dispute.\par \par \uc2 In New Zealand, the NZX-50 rose 0.2% to 3332.56 in quiet trading on light volume. \uc2 Telecom New Zealand\uc2  rose 0.2% to NZ$2.535 despite Fitch Ratings downgrading its foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issuer default rating and senior unsecured debt rating.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro experienced its biggest single-day percentage drop against the dollar in more than a year, as jitters about the euro-zone sovereign debt crisis resurfaced.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has given back all of its gains since Thursday, when European leaders announced a plan to stabilize the region's financial system. The agreement aims to boost the euro-zone bailout fund's firepower, cut Greece's debt load and recapitalize the region's banks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While billed as a "comprehensive package," market watchers say the region's leaders still have many details to hammer out, and implementation will be tricky.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the meantime, fears that a Greek default could drag down Italy and other European economies remained in full force, weighing down the common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Monday, the euro was at $1.3858 from $1.4147 late Friday, its biggest one-day percentage decline since August 2010, according to EBS via CQG.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We're getting a dose of reality," said Ron Leven, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "Now people are focusing on the fine print and there's a lot of uncertainties in that fine print."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro's swoon came alongside a jump in 10-year Italian government bond yields, which rose above 6%. Meanwhile, European stocks, including shares of many of the region's top banks, sank. \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell 9.6% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  slumped 9.8% in Paris. The costs to insure European sovereign debt also rose. On Monday, the five-year credit default swap spread on Italy was 45 basis points wider at 447 basis points, according to data provider Markit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors got another jolt late Monday following reports that Greece's government would hold a referendum on the newly minted aid package, and would also hold a vote of confidence on the government. Such developments add another layer of uncertainty for the debt-ridden country and for the euro zone's ability to dig its way out of the ongoing crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Adding to the euro's woes was a massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Japanese government to weaken the Japanese yen. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spent an estimated Y7 trillion, about $90 billion, to cap a rise in the yen that has undermined the economy and made exports less competitive abroad. That gave the dollar a boost against the yen, rising to Y78.17 from Y75.83. The euro was at Y108.34 from Y107.29.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar and the yen have been among the favored safe-haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in recent market volatility. But the threat of further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the yen leaves the dollar as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of choice in times of risk aversion, giving it additional momentum against the euro and other growth-sensitive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier Monday, Japanese Finance Minister Jun Azumi reiterated the government will continue to take "decisive steps" against "speculative movement in the market." But analysts say it will be difficult for Japan's government and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stop general market trends, which continue to lean toward a stronger yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the U.K. pound was at $1.6082 from $1.6129. The dollar was at CHF0.8765 from CHF0.8629. The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at about 76.484 from about 75.067.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors took U.S. Treasurys on a one-way ride, knocking out a chunk of the month's euro zone-inspired losses as reality about the still-churning credit crisis sank back in.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury prices climbed steadily through the session largely because of renewed worries about the euro-zone sovereign debt problems. The action comes in stark contrast to the tone earlier this month, when growing hopes about a sweeping plan to resolve the region's credit woes diminished demand for financial refuge and put a major dent in Treasury prices. [ 31-10-11 2203GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But it didn't take long for market participants' applause to fade after the euro-zone plan came out Thursday. Investors took a closer look at the agreement, couldn't find much detail about how to actually implement the euro-zone leaders' goals, and realized that a lot of the proposed measures will weigh on near-term economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We're now back to reality," said Marc Ostwald, strategist at Monument Securities. "We're still short of details, and there's all the noise related to Italy--a gridlock there is a massive problem for the euro zone."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This type of back and forth between hopes and fears is becoming commonplace, with many traders seeing benchmark 10-year Treasury yields finding a comfortable home between 2.0% and 2.5% for the rest of the year. Market participants get giddy in the immediate aftermath of apparently positive euro-zone plans, then fade when they realize the logical gaps.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italy became the first euro-zone issue to re-take center stage as leaders there struggle to agree on fiscal reforms to lighten the nation's debt load. This morning, the five-year CDS spread on Italy was 28 basis points wider at 430 basis points, according to data provider Markit. That means it now costs an average of $430,000 a year to insure $10 million of debt issued by the country. Yields on the country's 10-year debt surged above 6.0%, while five-year yields soared to a euro-era high of 5.97%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MF Global\f28 \uc2 \f2 's bankruptcy filing early Monday only added to the uncertainty. The broker-dealer's bets on European sovereign debt crippled its earnings, creating a major loss for the firm last quarter. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\f28 \uc2 \f2  this morning suspended the firm from conducting new business with the government as a primary dealer.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These fresh anxieties, even after the Dallas Federal Reserve reported its region's business activity unexpectedly expanding, helped push benchmark 10-year notes up 1 16/32 in price in late day trading as yields fell more than 17 basis points to 2.130%. That helped Treasurys recover some ground for the month, although yields still managed to climb about 20 basis points in October. Bond prices move inversely to their yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 30-year bond was the leading performer Monday, up 4 6/32 as yields tumbled nearly 21 basis points to 3.140%. Yields rose 22 basis points for the month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ray Remy, head of fixed-income trading at Daiwa Capital Markets America, said "tremendous concern" about the strength of the yen might have also played a role in fueling the Treasurys rally. The Japanese government spent an estimated Y7 trillion to try weakening the yen--that in turn put dollars in their hands, which are likely being used to buy Treasurys.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The week ahead presents an onslaught of new headline risks, analysts say, which will ultimately serve safe-haven Treasurys well. First up is the two-day Federal Open Market Committee meeting. Remy doesn't expect any new announcements from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at the conclusion of the meeting Wednesday, but said there's been plenty of chatter about a possible communication that the policy rate will be kept on hold until the labor market shows improvement, or a "twist on the twist" that could involve the Fed halting short-term note sales.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 MF Global\f28 \uc2 \f2  Files For Bankruptcy Protection \par Greek PM Calls For Referendum On EU Aid Deal \par US Stocks Tumble On Doubts About Europe \par Chicago Survey Signals Slower US Growth \par Honda Profit Tumbles; Thai Floods Hit US Output \par Risk Of Euro-Zone Recession Remains \par EFSF Plans 10-year Bond Amid Higher Funding Costs \par ILO Warns On New Global Jobs Recession \par US Probing Fannie Multifamily Deal \par Obama Meeting With Merkel, Sarkozy \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2145  New Zealand  3Q      Labour Cost Index \par 2145  New Zealand  3Q      Quarterly Employment Survey \par 2230  Australia    Oct     Australian PMI \par 2300  S Korea      Oct     CPI \par 2350  Japan        Oct     Bank of Japan Monetary Policy \par                            Meeting Minutes \par 0000  S Korea      Oct     Trade data \par 0000  S Korea      Oct     HSBC South Korea Manufacturing PMI \par 0030  Australia    3Q      House Price Indexes: Eight Capital Cities \par 0100  China        Oct     CFLP China Manufacturing Purchasing Managers' \par 0200  Taiwan       Oct     Taiwan Manufacturing PMI \par 0230  China        Oct     HSBC China Manufacturing PMI \par 0330  Australia    Nov     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  cash rate decision \par 0400  Indonesia    Oct     CPI \par 0400  Indonesia    Sep     Trade Balance \par 0500  India        Oct     India Manufacturing PMI \par 0515  Japan        Oct     Auto sales \par 0530  Australia    Oct     Commodity Price Index \par 0700  Thailand     Oct     CPI \par 0830  Hong Kong    Sep     Retail Sales \par 1330  Singapore    Oct     Singapore PMI \par N/A   Japan        Sep     Provisional Labour Survey \par 0700  UK           Oct     Nationwide House Price Index \par 0930  UK           3Q      GDP \par 0930  UK           Oct     CIPS Manufacturing PMI \par 1400  US           Oct     ISM Manufacturing Report \par 1400  US           Sep     Construction Spending \par 2030  US           Oct     API Statistical Bulletin \par N/A   US           Oct     Domestic Au