\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Europe Stocks Close Lower On Rescue Deal Skepticism\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrea Tryphonides   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 756 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2011\par 19:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 European stocks sank Monday as investors lost faith in the proposals set out by European leaders last week to solve the region's sovereign-debt crisis, and at the same time the euro slumped after \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  caught investors off guard.\par \par \uc2 Banks were particularly hit as investors became wary of the euro zone's rescue package with the lack of detail weighing heavily on sentiment.\par \par \uc2 Commenting on the rescue package, Bob Janjuah, global head of tactical asset allocation at Nomura said, "This latest round of euro-zone shock and awe is, in my view, nothing more than a confidence trick and has possibly even set up an even worse final outcome. With respect to the Greek debt 'write off', the bank 'recapitalization', and the structured credit technology being applied to the European Financial Stability Facility, my takeaway is fudge, fiction and fantasy."\par \par \uc2 The extreme bearishness that entered markets Monday is in contrast to what has been a bumper month for the major equity markets. The Stoxx Europe 600 index ended 7.7% higher month-to-date, boosted by moves by euro-zone leaders to sort out the sovereign-debt debacle.\par \par \uc2 But by Monday, confidence had disappeared and the index ended down 2.2% at 243.48. The U.K.'s FTSE 100 closed 2.8% lower at 5544.22 but finished up 8.1% on the month. It has registered the best monthly gain since 1992. Germany's DAX fell 3.2% to 6141.34 and France's CAC-40 closed down 3.2% at 3242.84. At the close of European equity markets, the Dow Jones Industrial Average was 1.5% lower and the \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index was down 1.6%.\par \par \uc2 Comments made by top officials at the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did little to help nerves. Outgoing ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet warned that plans to reduce the value of Greek sovereign bonds shouldn't set a precedent for other euro-zone members struggling with high debt loads. Mr. Trichet's comments came as he relinquishes his role to Mario Draghi this week. Mr. Draghi marks his inaugural rate-setting meeting and press conference as ECB president this Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, Monday's downbeat economic data will be of particular interest to members of the ECB ahead of Thursday's meeting. The number of people without jobs in the euro zone hit a new record in September after the biggest month-to-month rise in unemployment in two years. Some 16.198 million people were unemployed in September, 10.2% of the euro-zone's working population. At the same time, the annual rate of inflation in the euro zone was unchanged in October at its highest level in three years, with consumer prices rising 3% in the 12 months to October.\par \par \uc2 It was unsurprising then that European banks were the biggest decliners Monday. \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  fell 9.6%, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  declined 8.6% and \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  slid 9.8%. In London, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  fell 2.9% following its third-quarter results. Although the results were better-than-expected overall, the economic backdrop continues to look tough, particularly for the \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  business said analysts. Banks were also pushed lower on news that \uc2 MF Global Holdings\uc2  moved to file for bankruptcy protection in New York, after an attempt to sell itself to Interactive Brokers Group failed.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the dollar soared after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen in a mammoth dollar-buying offensive. This however led to a corresponding fall in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the euro. Meanwhile, Italian 10-year bond yields breached the psychologically important 6% level, worrying policy makers who had hoped the plans agreed upon by European leaders last week would bring some stability. At 1650 GMT, the euro was down 1.5% versus the dollar at $1.3923.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  move by Japan also weighed on gold prices. Gold is denominated in dollars and a stronger greenback makes it appear more expensive to buyers who use foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Most actively traded gold for December delivery on the Comex division of the \uc2 New York Mercantile Exchange\uc2  was down $27 per ounce, or 1.6%, at $1,720.20. Light, sweet crude for December delivery was down $1.68, or 1.8%, at $91.63 on the \uc2 New York Mercantile Exchange\uc2  by the close of European equity markets. [ 31-10-11 1812GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1106 1185   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111031e7av001p4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
