\par\par \b \uc2 REPEAT:EUROPEAN MIDDAY BRIEFING FROM DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\b0\par\par\uc2 1,097 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2011\par 12:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats for some subscribers)\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN MARKETS OUTLOOK: U.S. stocks are expected to start sharply lower, said David Morrison at U.K. spreadbettor GFT. Investors are responding to the sharp pull-back in the euro following the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the rise of the yen, he said. This led to a sharp rally in the dollar and a corresponding fall in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At 1030 GMT, S&P 500 futures were down 11.30 points at 1269.60, Nasdaq 100 futures were down 17.75 points at 2378.75 and Dow industrial futures were down 90.00 points at 12080.0.\par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN MARKETS: European markets were lower midday, with banks weighing amid fears that euro-zone leaders would continue to procrastinate in dealing with the euro-zone debt crisis ahead of this weekend's G-20 meeting. "At some stage the EU will have to come up with the actual implementation of their plans in order to encourage investors," said Gary Jenkins of \uc2 Evolution Securities\uc2 . Banks were the leading decliners, with the Stoxx Europe 600 index for the sector off 2.5%.\par \par \uc2 At 1030 GMT, the Euro Stoxx 50 was down 1.9% at 2414.57, while London's FTSE 100 was down 1.0% at 5643.28, edging lower with mining shares leading the losses amid falling metal prices in Asia. \uc2 Xstrata\uc2  was down 4.7%, with \uc2 Kazakhmys\uc2  down 4.0%. Traders noted that optimism about a successful solution to the euro-zone debt crisis had waned, with market participants looking to meetings of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Group of 20 industrialized and developing nations toward the end of the week for further clues on the way forward. Banking stocks slid as investors booked last week's hefty profits made by the sector; Lloyds was down 3.8%. Data showed U.K. mortgage activity lost some ground in September. "Weak economic conditions don't bode well for the housing market," said Newedge.\par \par \uc2 At 1025 GMT, December bunds were up 0.93 at 134.60, while December gilts were up 0.93 at 127.47. The dollar was up at Y77.92, the euro was down at $1.3996 and the pound was down at $1.6014.\par \par \uc2 ===========================\par \par \uc2 TOP STORIES:\par \par \uc2 EURO-ZONE UNEMPLOYMENT HITS NEW RECORD IN SEPTEMBER: The number of people without jobs in the euro zone hit a new record in September after the biggest month-to-month rise in unemployment in two years, official data showed.\par \par \uc2 OECD SEES WEAK GROWTH UNTIL 2013: Advanced economies are looking at two years of weak growth and high unemployment, and the outlook is likely to worsen unless the euro zone fails to reign in its sovereign debt crisis, the OECD said.\par \par \uc2 BARCLAYS REVENUE BEATS ESTIMATES AND PEERS: \uc2 Barclays\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Bob Diamond\uc2  said he is "absolutely committed" to a goal to hit a 13% return-on-equity target by 2013, despite a decline in investment-banking revenue this year that analysts fear could stall the plan's timeline.\par \par \uc2 ITALIAN BONDS BREACH 6% YIELD: Italian government bonds buckled, with five-year yields climbing to euro-era highs and the 10-year yield rising above the psychologically important 6% level as the post-European Union summit enthusiasm in risky assets fizzles out.\par \par \uc2 ============================\par \par \uc2 INSIGHT & ANALYSIS FROM DOW JONES NEWSWIRES:\par \par \uc2 THE WALL STREET JOURNAL: Greece was promised a heap of debt relief at last week's summit of European leaders. But there is little that looks like salvation.\par \par \uc2 INTERVIEW: The International Energy Agency called for oil producers to raise output by 500,000 barrels a day until the year-end, for easing tight supply and helping reduce the risk of another global recession, according to a top official.\par \par \uc2 DJ NEWSWIRES SURVEY: \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  is likely to join peers in reporting dismal third-quarter earnings from its investment bank Tuesday, when the Swiss bank may also announce more job cuts and further restructuring as it strives to cope with tough markets.\par \par \uc2 THE WALL STREET JOURNAL: The head of Europe's bailout fund takes his tour of potential Asian investors to Japan next, after Beijing proved unwilling to open its checkbook until Europe could offer more plan details.\par \par \uc2 ===========================\par \par \uc2 STILL TO COME\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ET/GMT    COUNTRY/PERIOD \par 0830/1230  US   Oct     ISM-NY Report on Business \par 0830/1230  CAN  Aug     GDP \par 0830/1230  CAN  Sep     Industrial product & raw materials price indexes \par 0945/1345  US   Oct     ISM-Chicago Business Survey - Chicago PMI \par 1030/1430  US   Oct     Texas Manufacturing Outlook Survey \par 1300/1700  US   Oct     Dow Jones Economic Sentiment Indicator \par 1950/2350  JPN  Oct     Bank of Japan Monetary Policy Meeting Minutes \par N/A        JPN          Bank of Japan Governor Shirakawa speech in Osaka \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ===========================\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OTHER NEWS:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SPAIN'S \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  SAYS ECONOMY FLATLINED IN 3Q: Spain's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said that growth in the euro zone's fourth-largest economy flatlined in the third quarter as domestic demand continued to contract.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA MAY PROVIDE $10B TO EURO-ZONE BAILOUT: Russia's chief presidential economic aide said that Moscow is ready to help the euro zone solve its debt crisis by providing up to $10 billion through the IMF, shying away from bilateral contributions to a special European stability fund.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ECB WEIDMANN CASTS DOUBT ON EURO-ZONE MEASURES: European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governing Council Member Jens Weidmann doused hopes that the latest set of measures adopted to resolve the euro-zone crisis could alone resolve the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bloc's debt and banking woes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  UNDER PRESSURE TO NAME PERMANENT CEO: Pressure is building on \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  to accelerate the search for a permanent chief executive officer, Swiss weekly Sonntag reports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GERMAN RETAIL SALES RISE BUT MISS EXPECTATIONS: German real seasonally adjusted retail sales rose in September from the previous month but were below expectations, preliminary data from the Federal Statistics Office showed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW CHINESE OWNERS TO INVEST EUR610M IN SAAB AUTO: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Saab Automobile\f28 \uc2 \f2 's new Chinese owners Pang Da and Youngman plan to invest some EUR610 million in the loss-making car maker in a bid to turn the Saab Auto brand around and make it profitable as early as 2014.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 UK BUSINESS CONFIDENCE WEAKENS IN OCTOBER: U.K. businesses became much more gloomy about their prospects in October, indicating that the economy is unlikely to pick up soon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GERMANY WANTS EU LEAD ON FINANCIAL-TRANSACTION TAX: Germany's finance minister said the EU should take the global lead in introducing a financial-transaction tax, the Financial Times reports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Simon Varcoe, Dow Jones Newswires; 4420-7842-9449; simon.varcoe@dowjones.com [ 31-10-11 1141GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 70737   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111031e7av001e4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
