\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Shares Seen Lower;  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 2,864 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2011\par 12:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SNAPSHOT: \par ======== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European bourses seen lower, core govt bonds higher; euro lower; oil futures higher, spot gold and copper lower\f28 \par \par \uc2 -Asian Shares Down; Yen Falls As \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervenes\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 -ECB Special Measures Not Needed When EFSF Launched -Trichet\par \par \uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  To Continue To Buy EFSF Bonds\par \par \uc2 -German Finance Minister Wants EU To Lead On Financial-Transaction Tax -FT\par \par \uc2 -Germany's Roesler, Westerwelle Call For Changes In EU Treaties- Report\par \par \uc2 -U.S. Weighs Waiting on AIG Share Sale\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 MF Global\uc2  Nears Bankruptcy Filing - Source\par \par \uc2 Watch for (GMT): 0700 GER Sep Retail Trade; 1000 EU Sep Unemployment; 1000 EU Oct Flash Estimate euro area inflation\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & US/ASIAN SUMMARIES: \par ======== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stock markets were mostly lower Monday, with shares in Tokyo choppy as the yen tumbled after Japanese authorities \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cap the strong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  triggered broad-based gains for the U.S. dollar, with the euro and Australian down sharply. Copper and gold prices were also down over 2.0% as the dollar's rise makes the metals appear costly for holders of other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The increased volatility in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market as a result of the (Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should see the U.S. dollar well supported for the rest of the session," said Tim Waterer, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealer at CMC Markets in Sydney.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks markets in the region were lower as investors were cautiously awaiting further details of the euro-zone rescue proposal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stock markets are likely to start lower in line with trends elsewhere, with bunds and gilts higher. The euro, copper and spot gold are lower, with oil futures higher.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ======\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stock markets are likely to start lower while investors take a more discerning look at last week's euro-zone debt agreement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For Monday's opening, Cantor Index is calling the FTSE down 33 at 5669, the DAX off 38 at 6308, and the CAC down 18 at 3330.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite all the cheering about Europe's latest debt deal, worries are mounting that it could fail without stronger economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the current pace of expansion, unemployment will stay high and incomes will stall. Debt-saddled governments will have an even tougher time generating revenue to pay bills. That could spark more default fears or higher interest rates in Greece, Italy and other countries under pressure.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Eurozone politicians were very successful in putting a positive spin on [last week's] summit but there are still significant potential pitfalls in the road ahead," said Jane Foley, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rabobank\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A handful of French banks, and some German and Italian are still widely considered to be vulnerable. Clearly this position could be worsened if contagion makes a comeback -- and this is still a significant risk."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The upcoming summit of the Group of 20 industrial and developing nations in Cannes must match Europe's crisis-resolution efforts and address long-standing issues that hamper the global economy, Herman Van Rompuy, president of the European Council, and Jose Manuel Barroso, president of the European Commission, said in a letter addressed to their G-20 partners Saturday evening.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stock futures are lower, after markets finished slightly higher Friday, extending the week's huge gains, as investors digested plans from European leaders to combat the region's debt woes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Friday's small gains came a day after the Dow surged more than 300 points as investors cheered a European plan to combat Greece's debt issues, expand a bailout facility and recapitalize the region's biggest banks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This is just a pause after a pretty big surprise," said Michael Sansoterra, portfolio manager of the RidgeWorth Large Cap Growth Fund. "Folks are by and large a little less pessimistic. The reality is [Europe's] plan is a positive step in the right direction, but there's still a lot of heavy lifting to do."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Questions still remain regarding how Europe will implement its bold plans and whether they will be enough to resolve the Continent's debt crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "People are wondering if it's safe to go back into the water," said John De Clue, senior vice president at U.S. Bank Wealth Management. "Everyone applauds leaders in Europe for taking some steps forward, but we've been down this road before, and there still aren't a lot of details released yet."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The power of the U.S. consumer gets a look this week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. auto makers \f28 \uc2 \f2 General Motors Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (GM), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ford Motor Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (F) and Chrysler Group LLC are each expected to report on Tuesday an increase in October new-vehicle sales from a year ago, with Chrysler again expected to post the largest sales gains for the month, according to car-shopping website \f28 \uc2 \f2 Edmunds.com\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. retailers are also slated to report same-store sales results for October on Thursday, off a 1.6% rise a year ago. Consumer spending usually slows in October as customers take a break from back-to-school spending in preparation for the holiday season.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Several U.S. media firms are due to release their quarterly earnings, including \f28 \uc2 \f2 Time Warner Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (TWX), NBC Universal's majority owner, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Comcast Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (CMCSA), and \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBS Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 .(CBS).\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 :\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ======\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets came alive after news the Japanese government had stepped into the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to contain the yen's strength, the first such move since Aug. 4.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  came after the dollar fell to a post-World War II record low of Y75.31 in early Asian trading.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The action drove the dollar sharply higher to Y79.55, from around Y75.65 before the move; the pair was recently at Y79.19, from Y75.83 late Friday in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The sharp, large jump in the dollar-yen would suggest that this was a very large amount (of dollar-buying)," said Sumino Kamei, senior analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\f28 \uc2 \f2  UFJ.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro lost ground against the dollar, and rose against the yen after the Japanese government's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Still, the euro-zone's debt deal lurked in the background.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We have an agreement but not all the details yet," said Greg Anderson, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York, who expects the market's focus will shift away from the debt crisis to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policies and economic data this week. The U.S. Federal Reserve and European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hold policy meetings this week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 =====\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bunds and gilts may start higher Monday as market participants digest the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and skepticism toward Europe's ability to execute its debt agreement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Friday core bonds recovered after a tepid Italian bond auction that saw soft demand. Bonds issued by other highly indebted euro zone countries also fell while other risky assets also gave up their gains, with traders coming to the view that markets had risen too much too quickly following the EU summit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rome, significantly, had to pay higher rates above the key red-line level of 6.0% to investors on Friday to raise fresh funds -- not a good sign, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "At this level, the rates are not sustainable for long," Giuseppe Maraffino from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  warned, stressing how important it is that they come down again in light of Italy's poor growth rate and vast mountain of debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasurys are higher Monday, as doubts crept up Friday on the effectiveness of the euro zone's debt-crisis resolution and Treasury bonds regained some favor from investors, sending benchmark yields lower from an 11-week peak.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The price rally was a reprieve from a selloff on Thursday when investors cheered announcement of a comprehensive package to recapitalize European banks, reduce Greece's debt burden and strengthen the region's bailout fund.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euphoria appeared to run out of steam Friday as many investors fretted that details still need to be firmed up on the debt deal. Some traders cautioned that implementation of such a grand design could be difficult, especially wooing private investors and sovereign-wealth funds to contribute to the euro zone's crisis-fighting capacity.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It was a short-lived bright spot," said Scott Graham, head of the U.S. government trading desk at BMO Capital Markets in Chicago.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Graham said he remains "constructive" on Treasury bonds and would buy bonds on a further selloff. The 10-year yield is unlikely to trade below 2% but a fall to 2.15% is reasonable, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 George Goncalves, head of U.S. interest rate strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nomura Securities International\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York who turned bearish on Treasury bonds this month, said the 10-year yield could rise to 2.75% in the short term.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Guy Haselmann, head of U.S. interest rate strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Nova Scotia\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York, stayed bullish on Treasury bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Tokyo Monday, Japanese government bond futures dropped below 142 for the first time since Sept. 5 as the yen's plunge sparked selling in safe-haven JGBs. December JGB futures were recently down 0.1 at 142.02.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ENERGY:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 =======\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude prices were higher in Asian trade Monday as the market continued to be cheered by Europe's debt rescue promise, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New York's main contract, light sweet crude for delivery in December, gained 14 cents to $93.46 per barrel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brent North Sea crude for December delivery rose 21 cents to $110.12.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude markets were "supported by optimism that European leaders will come up with (a) solution to the region's debt crisis," Phillip Futures said in a report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders were also looking ahead to a Franco-German meeting on Tuesday, where politicians from the two sides were expected to further discuss the debt rescue plan broadly agreed upon last week, the report added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Hope for a resolution to Europe's issues rose ahead of a Tuesday meeting between French and German politicians," it stated.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This meeting could be a key issue," Phillip Futures commodity analyst Ker Chung Yang told AFP.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He added that traders were also eyeing a US Federal Reserve meeting and the US jobs report both due later in the week for market leads.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 METALS:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ====== [ 31-10-11 1122GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LME base metals are sharply lower, with copper slipping over 2.0% as a higher USD weighs on prices. Dollar-denominated commodities appear costlier to holders of other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  when the greenback strengthens. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  says constraining macroeconomic issues in Europe, as well as concerns about a hard landing in China, remain in place and will continue to provide hurdles. LME 3-month copper is at $7,990/ton, down $184.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold could reassert its status as a safe-haven asset this week if risk appetite wanes amid lingering concerns over the euro-zone's debt problems, Mitsui Global Precious Metals says in a report. Sentiment remains cautious as many commentators see last week's European bailout deal as "little more than a short-term fix," the house says. "Many major questions concerning implementation remain unanswered, and the positive sentiment...may weaken when this realization is fully absorbed," it says. Gold is lower in Asia as a firm USD damps investor appetite; spot gold is at $1,712.40 a troy ounce, down $31 from its previous close.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; dennis.baker@dowjones.com [ 31-10-11 1122GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA         12231.11  +22.56 +0.18% \par Nasdaq        2737.15   -1.48 -0.05% \par S&P 500       1285.09   +0.50 +0.04% \par FTSE 100      5702.24  -11.58 -0.20% \par Xetra DAX     6346.19   +8.35 +0.13% \par CAC40         3348.63  -19.99 -0.59% \par Above are closing prices \par  \par Nikkei 225   9036.82  -13.65  -0.15% \par Hang Seng    19791.74 -227.50 -1.14% \par S&P/ASX 200   4326.70  -26.60 -0.61% \par Taiwan Index  7595.13  -20.93 -0.27% \par S.Korea Kospi 1907.11  -22.37 -1.16% \par Shanghai Comp 2460.41  -13.00 -0.53% \par Bombay SenSex 17723.12 -81.68 -0.46% \par Dow Future    12063.00 -107.00 -0.9% \par NASDAQ Future 2374.63  -21.88  -0.9% \par S&P Future    1268.00  -12.90  -1.0% \par Above are as of 0525 GMT \par  \par USD/JPY      79.20-21         +4.70% \par Range        79.55    75.58 \par EUR/USD      1.4006-08        -0.96% \par Range        1.4170   1.4001 \par AUD/USD      1.0536-40        -1.62% \par Range        1.0712   1.0522 \par GBP/USD      1.5982-86        -0.89% \par Range        1.6133   1.5968 \par USD/CHF      0.8727-32        +1.17% \par Range        0.8731   0.8612 \par EUR/GBP      0.8761-64        -0.11% \par Range        0.8783   0.8751 \par \u203\'00\'CBUR/CHF      1.2224-28       +0.18% \par Range        1.2228   1.2196 \par Above are as of 0450 GMT vs NY close \par  \par  \par USD/JPY Vol Option Contract 10.06%/10.74% \par Friday: 10.01%/10.69% \par EUR/USD Vol Option Contract 12.85%/13.55% \par Friday: 13.32%/14.08% \par AUD/USD Vol Option Contract 15