\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,283 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2011\par 11:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: EURO-ZONE UNEMPLOYMENT HITS NEW RECORD IN SEPTEMBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The number of people without jobs in the euro zone hit a new record in September after the biggest month-to-month rise in unemployment in two years, official data showed Monday. (published 0215 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: EURO-ZONE INFLATION RATE UNCHANGED AT THREE-YEAR HIGH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The annual rate of inflation in the 17 countries that share the euro was unchanged at its highest level in three years in October. (published 0200 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  STAGES RECORD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , PUSHES DLR UP 5% AT PEAK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese government launched a fresh round of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Monday, spending an estimated Y5 trillion-Y6 trillion to cap a rise in the yen that has undermined the economy and dragged down the key export sector. (published 0624 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ GOV SUPPORTS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , URGES \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO WORK ON OWN FISCAL ISSUES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan's governor expressed his support for the government's decision to directly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to weaken the yen, while at the same time urging it to work on its own worsening fiscal situation. (published 0854 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN PLEDGES SUPPORT FOR EFSF, AFTER CHINA DEMURS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The head of a European bailout fund on Monday won assurances that Japan will continue to invest in the beefed-up European Financial Stability Facility to help tackle the continent's debt crisis, providing some welcome news for the fund after China made no commitment to invest. (published 0636 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ITALIAN BONDS FALL SHARPLY, FIVE-YEAR YIELD AT EURO ERA HIGHS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italian government bonds buckled Monday, with five-year yields climbing to euro-era highs and the 10-year yield rising above the psychologically important 6% level as the post-European Union summit enthusiasm in risky assets fizzled out. (published 0855 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: ITALY OCTOBER PRELIMINARY CPI +0.6% MM; 1995 HIGH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italian consumer prices rose to a 16-year high of 0.6% in October from the previous month, on higher energy prices and as an increase in the sales tax bites in, preliminary data from the national statistics agency Istat showed Monday. (published 0216 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  YEN-SELLING \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ESTIMATED AT Y7T-Y8T - SENIOR TRADER \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Monday might have exceeded Y7 trillion, a senior trader at a major bank said. (published 0709 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  PM NODA: CONDUCTED \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO WARD OFF ECONOMIC DOWNSIDE RISKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda said Monday that his government conducted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  earlier in the day to ward off damage to the export-driven economy from the yen's strength, which he said was due to speculative moves. (published 0605 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IGARASHI: TOO EARLY TO JUDGE NEW ROUND OF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  OVER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A senior official in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance ministry said Monday it is too early to assess the impact of the country's latest yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as the action may not have ended yet. (published 0128 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ECB PARAMO WARNS AGAINST SOLO \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX INTERVENTIONS\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AFTER \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MOVE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is in principle against \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the bank's executive board member Jose Manuel Gonzalez-Paramo said Monday, the first comments by an ECB official after Japan sold large amounts of yen to weaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  earlier in the day. (published 0855 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ECB GONZALEZ-PARAMO EXPECTS JAPAN TO BUY EFSF BONDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  executive board member Jose Manuel Gonzalez-Paramo said Monday Japan's government is "very likely" to take part in upcoming bond auctions of the European Union's bailout fund. (published 0744 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ECB'S WEIDMANN REITERATES CRISIS MEASURES NOT ENOUGH-REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governing Council Member Jens Weidmann Monday doused hopes that the latest set of measures adopted to resolve the euro-zone crisis could alone resolve the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  block's debt and banking woes. (published 0834 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ECB'S TRICHET: GREEK DEBT WRITE-DOWN MUST BE A ONE-OFF - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The partial write-down of Greek sovereign debt shouldn't be repeated for any other euro-zone country, outgoing European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Jean-Claude Trichet told the BBC. (published 0814 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SPAIN'S \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  SAYS ECONOMY FLATLINED IN 3Q \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spain's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Monday that growth in the euro zone's fourth-largest economy flatlined in the third quarter as domestic demand continued to contract. (published 0122 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: GERMAN SEPTEMBER RETAIL SALES REAL ADJ +0.4% ON MONTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German real seasonally adjusted retail sales rose in September from the previous month but were below expectations, preliminary data from the Federal Statistics Office showed Monday. (published 0717 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TRICHET: ENSURING FINANCIAL STABILITY IS NOT THE ECB'S JOB \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Governments, rather than central banks, are responsible for maintaining financial stability, out-going European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Jean-Claude Trichet said Monday in an interview broadcast on CNBC. (published 0131 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: FRENCH SEPT PPI +0.2% ON MONTH; +6.1% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French producer prices rose slightly in September, led by energy and power prices, the national statistics agency, or Insee, said Friday. (published 0806 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: ITALY SEPTEMBER JOBLESS RATE 8.3%, HIGHEST SINCE NOVEMBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italian unemployment rose in September to its highest level in almost a year as the labor market deteriorated for both adult men and women, while youth unemployment reached a record high, national statistics agency Istat said Monday. (published 0130 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par KREMLIN AIDE: READY TO HELP EUROPE DEBT SITUATION VIA IMF \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia's chief presidential economic aide said Monday that Moscow is ready to help the euro zone solve its debt crisis through the International Monetary Fund. (published 0633 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UK BUSINESS SECRETARY: CONSIDERING LOAN SECURITIZATION, FINANCIAL WINDOWS FOR MID-SIZE COMPANIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K. government is considering various means of credit easing, including the securitization of loans by small and medium-sized enterprises as well as specialist financial windows for mid-sized companies, U.K. Business Secretary Vince Cable said Monday. (published 0801 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: UK MORTGAGE APPROVALS DIP IN SEPTEMBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mortgage approvals declined in the U.K. in September and net lending to businesses shrank, data from the Bank of England figures showed Monday. (published 0135 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDONESIA PRESIDENT: G-20 TO DISCUSS WAYS TO STABILIZE, STIMULATE GLOBAL GROWTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Leaders attending the Group of 20 industrial and developing nations summit in Cannes this week will discuss ways to stabilize and stimulate global economic growth, President Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono said Monday. (published 0155 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par PREVIEW: CHINA OFFICIAL OCTOBER PMI LIKELY UP AT 51.7 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's official purchasing managers index, a gauge of manufacturing activity, likely rose in October, further alleviating concerns over a slowdown in the world's second-largest economy. (published 0352 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PREVIEW: HK SEPTEMBER RETAIL SALES GROWTH LIKELY STEADY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong's retail sales continued to increase at a steady pace in September, underpinned by spending by visitors from mainland China, but the recent correction in financial markets might hurt consumer sentiment in the months ahead, economists said. (published 0437 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PREVIEW: INDONESIA OCT CPI LIKELY +4.80% ON YEAR, +0.17% ON MONTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's on-year inflation rate accelerated slightly to 4.80% in October from 4.61% in September because of a low base of comparison, according to a median forecast of nine regional economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires. (published 0411 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PREVIEW: INDONESIA SEPTEMBER TRADE SURPLUS LIKELY $2.72 BLN VS $3.76 BLN AUG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's trade surplus in September likely fell sharply from August, because of weaker prices for nearly all its export commodities, economists said. (published 0414 GMT) [ 31-10-11 1058GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111031e7av001bp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
