\par\par \b \uc2 S Korea Won Down Vs Dollar After  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervention \highlight\uc2 ; Bonds Up\b0\par\par\uc2 507 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2011\par 09:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                     Close     Change \par  USD/KRW            1,110.00  +5.10 \par  JPY/KRW            13.9798   -0.5775 \par  3-Year Treasurys   3.51%     -3 bps \par  5-Year Treasurys   3.63%     -4 bps \par  10-Year Treasurys  3.87%     -6 bps \par  20-Year Treasurys  4.05%     -7 bps \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--The South Korean won fell against the U.S. dollar late Monday, as the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  received strong support in Asian trade after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to weaken the Japanese yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened the session higher against the dollar, with risk appetite staying buoyant after last week's plans by European leaders to combat the region's debt woes. After the dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese authorities, however, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gave up early gains and turned lower as players covered their short-dollar positions quickly.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Bank of Korea was also suspected of buying the dollar around the key KRW1,100 level, before the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities on the yen, according to four Seoul-based traders Monday. Two of the traders said the BOK could have bought at least $300 million to smooth the won's gains.\par \par \uc2 The BOK is last suspected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Oct. 4, likely selling the dollar at levels above KRW1,200, and the last time it was suspected of carrying out dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  goes back to Aug. 1, likely buying dollars near KRW1,049.\par \par \uc2 An official at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market team declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the dollar is likely to maintain its longer-term downward trend against the won, but for this week, it is expected to hover around the KRW1,100 level with investors cautiously checking key economic events, such as policy meetings of the Federal Open Market Committee and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A trader at a local bank tipped the dollar to move between KRW1,080 and KRW1,120 this week.\par \par \uc2 The won rose 6.1% against the dollar in October.\par \par \uc2 Korean government bonds rose despite better-than-expected domestic data, as losses in local shares prompted investors to seek safe-haven bonds, market participants said. Korea's stock index snapped its three-day winning streak and ended 1.1% lower Monday.\par \par \uc2 Government data showed South Korea's industrial output rose 6.8% year on year in September, higher than a 5.7% rise expected by economists in a Dow Jones Newswires poll.\par \par \uc2 For this week, investors are likely to stay cautious ahead of the FOMC meeting and the ECB policy meeting, watching out for the possibility of additional quantitative easing, said Ough Chang-sup, a fixed-income analyst at Meritz Securities.\par \par \uc2 He said he expects the three-year yield to trade in a 3.50%-3.65% band this week.\par \par \uc2 December bond futures ended 15 ticks higher at 104.04.\par \par \uc2 -By Jieun Shin, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-3700-1905; jieun.shin@dowjones.com [ 31-10-11 0823GMT ]\par \par