\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 805 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2011\par 06:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 3RD UPDATE: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN INTERVENES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKET TO CAP STRONG YEN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese government launched a new round of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Monday, moving to cap a continued rise in the yen that is undermining the economy and dragging down the key export sector. (published 0300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN MOF CONTINUES TO REMAIN IN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKET - 3 SOURCES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market after staging a dollar-buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  earlier Monday, three people familiar with the matter said. (published 0342 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AMOUNT OF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN FX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  SEEN EXCEEDING Y5T - 2 SOURCES \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Monday is seen exceeding Y5 trillion and nearing Y6 trillion, two people close to the matter said. (published 0412 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN GOVT FUJIMURA: ONE-SIDED YEN RISE WAS RISK TO JAPAN ECON \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's top government spokesman said Monday that the government's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  earlier in the morning was intended to prevent a downturn in Japan's economy due to the yen's dramatic rise against the dollar. (published 0244 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: S KOREA SAYS 3Q INDICATORS WEAK DESPITE STRONGER SEP OUTPUT DATA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean Finance Minister Bahk Jae-wan on Monday said economic momentum deteriorated during the third quarter despite better-than-expected industrial output growth in September, but reiterated the government will do its best to meet its 2011 economic growth target of 4.5%. (published 0155 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: AUSTRALIA RATE CUT ON EDGE AS CREDIT GROWS STRONGLY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian credit growth was strong in September, adding to evidence of a modest pick-up in the commodity-rich economy over recent months and ensuring any decision by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cut interest rates on Tuesday will be tight. (published 0322 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: AUSTRALIAN SEPTEMBER CAPITAL-CITY HOUSE PRICES DOWN 0.2% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian capital-city house prices continued to fall in September, with a recent rebound in the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  worrying brokers that the steady declines in the market so far this year could continue. (published 0321 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PBOC ADVISER LI: CHINA INFLATION TO DROP TO 2.8% IN 2012 - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's consumer price index growth may drop sharply to 2.8% in 2012, the Beijing Times reported Monday, citing a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  adviser. (published 0229 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN MOF OFFICIAL: JAPAN TO CONTINUE TO BUY EFSF BONDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A senior official from the Japanese finance ministry said Monday that the country will buy more bonds from the European Financial Stability Facility, but may not maintain the same purchasing rate. (published 0253 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDONESIA PRESIDENT: G-20 TO DISCUSS WAYS TO STABILIZE, STIMULATE GLOBAL GROWTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Leaders attending the Group of 20 industrial and developing nations summit in Cannes this week will discuss ways to stabilize and stimulate global economic growth, President Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono said Monday. (published 0155 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: SHANGHAI TO SELL CNY7.1B BONDS IN NOVEMBER - SOURCES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The municipality of Shanghai plans to directly sell CNY7.1 billion worth of local government debt in November, in the largest such debt offering this year, three people familiar with the situation told Dow Jones Newswires Monday. (published 0354 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: CHINA OFFICIAL OCTOBER PMI LIKELY UP AT 51.7 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's official purchasing managers index, a gauge of manufacturing activity, likely rose in October, further alleviating concerns over a slowdown in the world's second-largest economy. (published 0352 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: HK SEPTEMBER RETAIL SALES GROWTH LIKELY STEADY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong's retail sales continued to increase at a steady pace in September, underpinned by spending by visitors from mainland China, but the recent correction in financial markets might hurt consumer sentiment in the months ahead, economists said. (published 0437 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: INDONESIA OCT CPI LIKELY +4.80% ON YEAR, +0.17% ON MONTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's on-year inflation rate accelerated slightly to 4.80% in October from 4.61% in September because of a low base of comparison, according to a median forecast of nine regional economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires. (published 0411 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: INDONESIA SEPTEMBER TRADE SURPLUS LIKELY $2.72 BLN VS $3.76 BLN AUG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's trade surplus in September likely fell sharply from August, because of weaker prices for nearly all its export commodities, economists said. (published 0414 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJE(10/31)HEARD ON THE ST:DEAL WON'T SOLVE GREEK PENSIONS TRAGEDY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the outlook for Greek banks is grim, spare a thought for the fate of Greek pensions. The government is considering a transfer of state-owned assets to pension funds to make up for losses following the proposed 50% Greek sovereign-debt haircut: a potential 4 billion euros hit. But that won't put Greek pensions on any more of a sustainable footing. (published 0430 GMT) [ 31-10-11 0500GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111031e7av000dn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
