\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Asian Shares Down; Nikkei Choppy, Yen Falls As  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 801 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2011\par 04:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Asian equities falter amid caution ahead of key events and data\par \par \uc2 -- Yen tumbles and the Nikkei gains briefly as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 -- Euro and Australian dollar fall amid broad-based gains for U.S. dollar\par \par \uc2 (Adds information, quotes, updates/adds market levels)\par \par \uc2 By Shri Navaratnam and Wei-Zhe Tan\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian stock markets were lower Monday, with shares in Tokyo choppy as the yen tumbled after Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  triggered broad-based gains for the U.S. dollar, with the euro and Australian down sharply. Copper and gold prices were also down over 2.0% as the dollar's rise makes the metals appear costly for holders of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The increased volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as a result of the (Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should see the U.S. dollar well supported for the rest of the session," said Tim Waterer, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at CMC Markets in Sydney.\par \par \uc2 Stocks markets in the region were lower as investors were cautiously awaiting further details of the euro-zone rescue proposal.\par \par \uc2 "With more questions than answers markets will be hungry for further details over coming weeks and until then it is difficult to see risk appetite stretching too far," said Mitul Kotecha, strategist at \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  in a note. Kotecha also said that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meetings in Australia, the U.S. and Europe, as well as several key data releases this week, including the U.S. jobs data Friday, will influence investors' decisions.\par \par \uc2 Australia's S&P/ASX 200 fell 0.8%, South Korea's Kospi Composite lost 0.9%, Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index declined 1.1%, and China's Shanghai Composite Index was 0.5% lower. Japan's Nikkei Stock Average was choppy on confirmation of the yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and was recently down 0.2% after climbing as much as 0.8%.\par \par \uc2 Dow Jones Industrial Average futures were down 51 points in screen trade.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets came alive after news the Japanese government had stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to contain the yen's strength, the first such move since Aug. 4.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came after the dollar fell to a post-World War II record low of Y75.31 in early Asian trading.\par \par \uc2 The action drove the dollar sharply higher to Y79.55, from around Y75.65 before the move; the pair was recently at Y79.19, from Y75.83 late Friday in New York.\par \par \uc2 "The sharp, large jump in the dollar-yen would suggest that this was a very large amount (of dollar-buying)," said Sumino Kamei, senior analyst at \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ.\par \par \uc2 The euro lost ground against the dollar, and rose against the yen after the Japanese government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at $1.4023 against the dollar, from $1.4147 late Friday in New York, and at Y111.06 against the yen, from Y107.29.\par \par \uc2 In Tokyo, the stock market briefly turned higher, as news of the yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped support exporter stocks. \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  rose 1.7% and \uc2 Sony\uc2  climbed 0.6%.\par \par \uc2 "The key issue is how much the Japanese government has a sense of crisis and whether it will continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Masatoshi Sato, senior strategist at Mizuho Investors Securities.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  rose 2.2% as investors continued to buy into the tech giant after last week's strong third quarter earnings prompted ratings upgrades from several brokerage houses.\par \par \uc2 In Sydney, Qantas bucked a broadly weaker market, rising 4.0% after the airline was cleared Monday to restore service following a ruling by an Australian tribunal to end a prolonged labor dispute that had forced the carrier to ground its fleet world-wide over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Japanese government bond futures dropped below 142 for the first time since Sept. 5 as the yen's plunge sparked selling in safe-haven JGBs. December JGB futures were recently down 0.1 at 142.02.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was at $1,710.10 per troy ounce, down $33.30 from its New York settlement Friday. December Nymex crude oil futures were down 79 cents at $92.53 per barrel on Globex.\par \par \uc2 -Shri Navaratnam, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4142; shri.navaratnam@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 31-10-11 0349GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1106 4125 5037 62560