\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: China IRS Down; To Continue To Fall -Trader\b0\par\par\uc2 1,527 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2011\par 09:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0815 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS are slightly lower as demand for month-end money is shrinking. The one-year IRS are 3 bps lower at 3.53%-3.63% and the five-year IRS are down 2 bps at 3.60%-3.70%. The benchmark seven-day repo rate is 8 bps lower at 4.96%. "The swaps should continue to fall as monetary conditions will be easier broadly in the future," says a Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0810 GMT [Dow Jones] Monument Securities expects the bid-to-cover ratio to be "poor, barring a news-driven short squeeze" at Germany's EUR5 billion auction of the 1.25% October 2016 federal note Wednesday. Germany's last two bobl auctions were technically uncovered, as the total amount of bids remained below the offer size. The previous issue of this bobl in September saw the lowest coverage ratio for five-year German debt since 1999. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0810 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/MYR is higher at 3.0730 vs 3.0620 late Friday, tracking gains in the USD in Asian trade; trading is choppy, with bids ranging from 3.0410 to 3.0815, amid thin volume as investors stay on the sidelines ahead of the G-20 summit starting Thursday. "The bid-ask spread is very wide and volatility is rising," says a local trader. "At the moment, most of the trading is biased towards the USD" after Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, says the trader. He tips the pair in a 3.0600-3.0750 band for the day. (jason.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0808 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts open the session sharply higher, as stock markets fade lower and worries over the euro-zone debt crisis resurface. The bond market is also benefiting from a \uc2 Lloyds Banking Group\uc2  survey, which showed British businesses became a lot gloomier about their prospects in October. Ahead, the BOE publishes data on lending to individuals for September, with \uc2 Barclays\uc2  expecting mortgage approvals to drop marginally. The December gilt contract is 55 ticks higher at 127.09 and the 2s/5s/10s open at 0.52%/1.36%/2.58%. (nick.cawley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0808 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY is down, but is hovering near its daily upside limit of 0.5% above the central parity on continued corporate demand for the USD. The pair is at 6.3540, after hitting Monday's limit at 6.3549, and compared with 6.3586 late Friday in afternoon trade. It's tipped to consolidate at the current level for the rest of the session, says a Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. "It's possible the PBOC may let the central parity rebound a bit tomorrow after it's met some internal monthly target for appreciation, but the fixing probably won't fall back by more than around 20 pips," he says. The central parity is set at a fresh record low of 6.3233 from 6.3290 Friday. Offshore, one-year USD/CNY NDFs are up at 6.3540/6.3590 from 6.3500/6.3550 late Friday. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0803 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY not in the clear yet, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analyst Karen Jones, even though the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday has been impressive. To confirm a reversal, says Jones, the four-year downtrend at 79.83 and then the 55-week moving average at 80.57 need to be overcome. USD/JPY sank to a fresh all-time low of 75.31 Monday and that triggered the MOF to instruct the BOJ to stop the rot, with the subsequent solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushing the spot as high as 79.55. USD/JPY now trades at 78.91, Jones pegs support at 78.09 and 77.40. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0801 GMT [Dow Jones] The Japanese government's solo JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will prove insufficient to continue dragging down the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which will now respond to external factors beyond Tokyo's control, says Mitul Kotecha, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 fx\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2 . While the large amount of JPY-selling pushed the USD/JPY up sharply as it caught the market with many JPY longs, the effect could be temporary without any rises in U.S. interest rates to lend the greenback more fundamental support, he says. "In order for USD/JPY to sustain a move higher it will require both a widening in yield differentials and easing risk aversion," Kotecha says. Neither is certain now, he adds. "Doubts are already creeping in about the eurozone agreement last week while some Fed officials are highlighting the prospects of more asset purchases." The USD/JPY is at 78.90, down from a post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  high of 79.55.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0753 GMT [Dow Jones] Some banks have reached their daily trading limits with certain counter-parties due to heavy trading volume ignited by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , senior dealers say. "I've heard line-full is happening many places in the market," one dealer says, adding that the phenomenon thins overall trading volume thus making the market more volatile. Something similar took place when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August, but today's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen affecting more institutions, dealers say. "I see at one point that the dollar/yen's bid and ask flipping over," another dealer says, adding that some banks are not being affected at all as they have no trading limit via CLS trading. According to EBS data, trading activity in the USD/JPY was more than 10 times higher than average throughout Asian trade.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0747 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY swaps consolidate with an upward bias as liquidity remains tight amid strong corporate demand for the USD. One-year \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps are at a discount of 130 pips to the spot exchange rate from a discount of 210 pips late Friday. It's tipped to consolidate around minus-150 pips for now, says a Shenzhen-based foreign bank trader; "it's the last day of the month and demand for dollars is very strong. Liquidity has been tight for a while now, so it looks like some forecasts of an RRR cut may have been premature." The USD/CNY is at 6.3543 from 6.3586 Friday. Offshore, one-year USD/CNY NDFs are up at 6.3580/6.3630 from 6.3500/6.3550 late Friday. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0738 GMT [Dow Jones] Nordea says NOK firmer on strong Norway 3Q earnings as well as optimism that E.U. leaders may be able to tackle the euro-zone debt crisis following last week's summit agreements. Nordea notes that last time EUR/NOK was trading at these levels, the cross fell quickly to 7.49 only to stage a sharp turnaround. "Needless to say, the picture continues to be quite cloudy at the moment," the bank says. EUR/NOK trades at 7.6799. (charles.duxbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0735 GMT [Dow Jones] India overnight call rates are a tad down as liquidity is ample, says a dealer with a state-run bank. The interbank call rate is at 8.53%, a tad down from 8.55% at Friday's close, while the collateralized borrowing and lending obligation, or CBLO, is at 8.49%, off the intraday high of 8.60%. "Many banks are on the lending side as their cash positions are covered ahead of the reporting Friday this week," he says. Banks have to maintain 6% of their total deposits as cash reserves with \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2 . They have to report their cash balances on the last Friday of every fortnight. The dealer tips the call rates to remain in a 8.55%-8.60% range for the rest of the session, while CBLO rates may trade in a 8.25%-8.50% range. Banks borrowed INR523.85 billion at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's repo auction earlier in the day. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0728 GMT [Dow Jones] Strong seasonal demand will support gold prices near-term, according to a report from \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . The house tips near-term support for the yellow metal at $1,690 a troy ounce and then at $1,650/oz, noting robust fundamental, technical and macroeconomic drivers for the market. "Investment flows have turned positive and are key in leading prices higher against a gold fertile backdrop. We continue to expect gold prices to be cushioned amid the seasonally strong period for demand," the house says. BarCap is forecasting a 4Q11 average of $1,875 a troy ounce and a 2012 average of $2,000/oz. Spot gold is trading lower in Asia Monday as the stronger USD pressures dollar-denominated commodities. Gold trades at $1,712.16 a troy ounce, down $31.24 from its previous close.(clementine.wallop@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 31, 2011 04:15 ET (08:15 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020111031e7av002co\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
