\par\par \b \uc2 BASE METALS: LME Metals Fall After  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervention \highlight\uc2  Jolts Markets\b0\par\par\uc2 528 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2011\par 13:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Base metals fall after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reverse yen strength, boosting dollar\par \par \uc2 --Margin increases also seen to damp interest in the metals\par \par \uc2 --Sentiment shifts likely to keep trade choppy, but analysts expect markets to remain under pressure\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Rhiannon Hoyle \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Base metals are lower on the London Metal Exchange Monday after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets strengthened the U.S. dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is thought to have spent around 5 trillion to 7.5 trillion yen ($60 billion-$80 billion) to buy dollars against the yen, pushing the pair up to JPY79.55 in an effort to weaken the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after the greenback printed another record low against the yen at JPY75.31 at the start of Asian trading hours. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is thought to be one of the biggest dollar-buying efforts on record.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 1229 GMT, LME three-month copper traded at $7,945.50 a metric ton, down $229.50, or 2.8%, on Friday's PM kerb close. In Europe, EUR/USD was down 1.1% at $1.39950.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The metal markets are priced in dollars, and subsequently tend to trade with an inverse correlation to the greenback.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Anybody who kept a position over the weekend is crying into their coffee this morning because they didn't anticipate the moves by the BOJ [Bank of Japan]," said a trader.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, an increase in margin requirements for copper, aluminum and nickel--announced Monday and to take effect close of business Nov. 1--will make it more expensive to trade the metals and may subsequently add to pressure on prices, market participants said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Initial margins for copper will be raised to $820/ton from $750/ton and margins for aluminum lifted to $158/ton from $155/ton, said \f28 \uc2 \f2 LCH.Clearnet Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., which clears trades for the LME. Initial margins for nickel will also be increased, rising to $2,670/ton, from $2,500/ton.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts remain cautious on the outlook for the complex, despite last week's 14% rebound in the copper price, with the markets' demand outlook still clouded by fears over the euro zone, U.S. and Chinese economies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We feel the economic outlook is weak and that will weigh on prices in the months ahead," said FastMarkets.com analyst William Adams. "Changes in short-term sentiment are likely to keep prices choppy and there are likely to be countertrend moves as we saw last week, but these are expected to run into overhead and dominant selling."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In other metals Monday, three-month aluminum traded down 1.5% at $2,208/ton, three-month zinc was down 2.3% at $1,940.25/ton, three-month nickel was 1.5% lower at $19,400/ton and three-month tin had fallen 0.9% to $21,900/ton. Three-month lead recorded the largest decline, having slid 3.8% to $2,010/ton.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Rhiannon Hoyle, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 (0)20 7842 9405; rhiannon.hoyle@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Eva Szalay contributed to this report.) [ 10-31-11 0846ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 61030 82052   \par \