\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Japan's Move To Cap Yen Lifts Gold's Prospects\b0\par\par\uc2 1,327 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2011\par 19:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 2:38 (Dow Jones) Investors may give gold a second look as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  alternative after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's effort to cap the yen's recent rise has cast doubt on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's status as a haven, says Rob Kurzatkowski of optionsXpress. If Japan joins Switzerland in successfully dulling the allure of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , "you're going to see traders reconsider gold." It is widely seen as both an alternative \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a haven. Comex December gold fell 1.3% amid the day's broad asset selling. (tatyana.shumsky@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2:21 (Dow Jones) Credit default swaps on major US banks are several points wider as stocks and other assets slide amid impacts including MF's bankruptcy, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and renewed euro-zone worries. The biggest movers are also the CDS of the majors with the highest price--\uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  (MS) and \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  (BAC). MS goes to 3.34 percentage points from 3.12 while BAC widens to 3.31 from 3.08, according to Markit data. Citi (C) and \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  (GS) widen about 0.2 point while \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  (JPM) and \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  (WFC), which have the cheapest CDS of the bunch, rise about 0.1. (katy.burne@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2:12 (Dow Jones) MF shares remain halted as the NYSE first stopped trading this morning amid the potential for a deal with Interactive Brokers (IBKR). That fell through, prompting the troubled broker-dealer's bankruptcy filing. Don't expect the stock to go anywhere soon. It's subject to review by the Big Board, but in most cases the exchange will switch to suspend the shares for a 10-day period before they are delisted. At that point, MF could begin trading again on other platforms like the pink sheets, unless regulators say otherwise. Shares closed Friday at $1.20. (jacob.bunge@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2:10 (Dow Jones) The \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\uc2  released its next round of Treasury sales and purchases for the so-called \b \highlight22\uc2 Operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  Twist stimulus program. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to buy $45B worth of long-dated Treasurys via 13 \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in November, and sell $43B worth of short-dated notes through six sales \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  that month. The market is holding on its sharp gains Monday, with benchmark 10-year notes up 30/32 to yield 2.196%. (cynthia.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2:08 (Dow Jones) The selling of yen has put billions of dollars in the hands of the Japanese government, and if August's Treasury bidding statistics are any indication, analysts expect the money to be spent on US government debt. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's last round of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in August -- a month when the country's Treasury holdings expanded by $21.8 billion and foreigners bought a notably high amount of US debt during the Treasury Department's routine sales, according to CRT Capital Group. "This money has to go somewhere and so we'll use this history to anticipate buying in [medium-term Treasurys] and strong foreign buying in 10-year notes at the upcoming [sale]," said CRT's head of government bond strategy David Ader. Benchmark 10-year notes recently up 30/32 in price to yield 2.198%. (cynthia.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2:08 (Dow Jones) High-frequency traders and active customers who used MF's clearing and trading services for hedging can't stay idle in the financial markets, says Rob Kurzatkowski, senior commodity analyst with optionsXpress, which is owned by Schwab (SCHW). "They're moving around from the firm, because you can't sit around and wait if this is your livelihood. We are definitely getting a lot more calls, a lot more interest." (tatyana.shumsky@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:55 (Dow Jones) \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets appear to be taking a cue from global markets' mood, as the euro tracks an upward move in US stocks. Though the euro remains deep in the red against the dollar, it has pared some of the day's losses and is off session lows. Still, analysts say any attempts for a euro rebound will likely be thwarted by investors, who will still sell the euro on any rally. That's because there are still a lot of unknowns in the euro zone, including a lack of clarity surrounding the new plan to deal with the debt crisis and no certainty as to when -- and how -- the plan will be implemented. The euro most recently changes hands at $1.3953, down about 1.4% on the day, according to EBS via CQG.(erin.mccarthy@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:52 (Dow Jones) Outside the New York Mercantile exchange in downtown Manhattan, the mood among traders and floor employees is grim after \uc2 MF Global\uc2  files for bankruptcy protection. "Everybody's running for the door," says Jeffrey Grossman, who works in the oil options pit of the Nymex for BRG Brokerage, of traders who had previously been clearing through \uc2 MF Global\uc2 . Many floor traders describe a rush to find new clearinghouses to take on trades. Others say they are trading as little as possible out of fear of a major market move. "A lot of guys left, cleared out," says one natural gas trader. (dan.strumpf@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:52 (Dow Jones) As MF enters bankruptcy, the comparisons to \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2 ' 2008 collapse are running rife, and the gold market is not immune. "When Lehman's vanished, the price of gold lost almost 20%," says Adrian Ash, director of research at BullionVault.com. But he adds that while MF is a large counter-party, the impact is unlikely to be on the same scale for the gold market. (tatyana.shumsky@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:48 (Dow Jones) MF's customers "aren't going to hang around for too long," says INTL FCStone (INTL) CEO Sean O'Connor. "We are having people call us. I'm sure they're calling our competitors too." INTL FCStone is a direct competitor to MF in clearing and brokering on the Comex, among other international exchanges. (tatyana.shumsky@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:46 (Dow Jones) Worries begone! \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  does a turnaround on \uc2 Amerigroup\uc2  (AGP), going to buy from sell on the health insurer and raising its stock price 50% to $66. "We are now more bullish on Medicaid managed care, where we had been concerned that 3Q results would feature a margin crunch, particularly for \uc2 Amerigroup\uc2 ." Goldman adds, "The downside risk to margins is still there, but 3Q results lead us to conclude that the worst risk is behind us and we focus back to the very strong growth opportunity as states convert $50-100B or more of Medicaid spending to managed care over the next several years." AGP gains 1.8% to $56.60, putting this month's surge at 45%.(kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:42 (Dow Jones) Apartment operator Aimco (AIV) gets downgraded to outperform from strong buy at Raymond James in the wake of the company disclosing during Friday's 3Q conference call that leasing traffic slumped in October. "This phenomenon is likely limited to Aimco due to its higher mix of lower-income tenants," says Raymond James. It adds, "This is certainly a development that must be monitored closely in the months ahead as rent pressures are building most acutely on households with stagnant incomes." The company's 3Q results were seen as solid, and executives tried telling industry watchers not to worry too much because about the traffic slump because "the most important part of our business is the renewal business." (dawn.wotapka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Equities Market Talk Editors, \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Paul Vigna, 212 416-2184,paul.vigna@dowjones.com \par Visit the Market Talk website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 10-31-11 1438ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020111031e7av000ji\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
