\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ BLOG/MarketBeat: Can  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Win This Round of The  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Game?\b0\par\par\uc2 424 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2011\par 17:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's Market Beat blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/marketbeat"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/marketbeat}}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 By Tom Lauricella\par \par \uc2 The Japanese government threw everything it could at the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets overnight, staging what appears to be its biggest round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in history to try and set back the surge in the yen.\par \par \uc2 While the exact timing may have been a little bit of a surprise, pretty much every \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst with an opinion (that would be all of them) had been expecting some action from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was enough to boost the dollar to well over 79 yen from south of 76 yen on Friday. It's now changing hands around 78 yen.\par \par \uc2 Most analysts think it will be tough for Japan to reverse the yen's strength on any sustained basis. However, the yen was surprisingly stable against the dollar through much of the August and September market turmoil, so perhaps it won't be off to the races soon.\par \par \uc2 At Nomura, they don't see the yen making further big moves from here. Here's some of what they had to say overnight:\par \par \uc2 We suspect that this JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a one-day event, as was the case with the past three \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  (15 September 2010, 18 March 2011 and 4 August 2011). After all, Japan wants to avoid being labeled a _\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator by G7 companies -- a criticism that a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  would certainly provoke. With the US Congress urging China to enhance the flexibility of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we suspect that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will refrain from the kind of consecutive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  carried out by Switzerland, central banks in Asian countries, and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself during the era of massive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2003-04. In other words, if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's persistent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were to be allowed, that would constitute a dramatic shift in international politics regarding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies, in our view.\par \par \uc2 We expect USD/JPY to move based on fundamentals again after today's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ... In sum, it was hard to explain USD/JPY in the 75 range based on the US-Japan yield differential in the 2yr zone, which actually implies rates around 78 are justified. This \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  brought USD/JPY back into conformity with the yield differential.\par \par \uc2 We maintain our year-end USD/JPY forecast of 79.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 10-31-11 1214ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020111031e7av000e4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
