\par\par \b \uc2 Investors Place Bets As Japan Tries Again To Curb Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neil Shah   \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL   \par \uc2 690 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2011\par 22:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Is the third time the charm?\par \par \uc2 The Japanese government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Monday to put the brakes on a soaring yen that is hurting the country's key export sector and economy.\par \par \uc2 The yen immediately dropped, but investors are at odds over whether this tactic--the third this year--will succeed over the long haul.\par \par \uc2 Some investors are buying yen, betting Japan will fail. They note that the yen bounced back from this year's two other \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as well as an attempt in September 2010.\par \par \uc2 But, in a change from previous episodes, others are now saying this time could be different.\par \par \uc2 Constantine Ponticos, managing director, at Pareto, a firm that manages some $40 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said his firm's Absolute Return fund bought yen following \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Pareto is a unit of \uc2 Bank of New York Mellon Corp\uc2 . (BK).\par \par \uc2 He views Japan's latest dollar-buying as a relatively modest ploy to help Japanese exporters trade their stash of dollars for yen at an attractive price before month-end.\par \par \uc2 "Exporters have been all over the weak yen," he said. "I expect speculators and other investors" to do the same.\par \par \uc2 Hedge funds and retail investors have also piled into the yen Monday, traders say, following a flurry of trade recommendations by Wall Street banks like \uc2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\uc2 . (JPM) and \uc2 Credit Suisse Group AG\uc2 . (CS, CSGN.VX).\par \par \uc2 "At this point, there is no reason to think that this time is any different from previous unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Andrew Cox, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 Citigroup Inc\uc2 . (C) in New York.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese government's efforts are already wearing off. After surging 5% against the yen, the dollar slipped, finishing 3% higher at Y78.16 to the dollar in Monday's session. Before \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar had hit a new post-World War II low of Y75.31.\par \par \uc2 But others are taking the view that Japan's effort shouldn't be written off so easily.\par \par \uc2 John Floyd, of Floyd Capital Management LLC, said it's possible Japan could eventually succeed in pushing the yen lower for longer. A key factor: The weakening global economic recovery, especially China and other emerging markets that are key trading partners for Japan.\par \par \uc2 A pronounced weakness in global growth that hits Japan's export-driven economy could end up changing investors' minds about the yen, Floyd said.\par \par \uc2 "The big question is if in fact we are seeing the beginning of a more pronounced global slowdown," he said. "Japan is going to be one of the places most impacted."\par \par \uc2 In a downturn, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the behest of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , could mount an "unsterilized" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by buying dollars and increasing the supply of yen in the market, breaking from past \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . These so-called "unsterilized" purchases would boost the effectiveness of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and act as an economic stimulus.\par \par \uc2 A few weeks ago, Floyd made a small bet that the dollar would rise against the yen, using an options strategy that limited his risk. The position has been slightly profitable, but Floyd is holding steady, just in case the recent yen weakness has legs.\par \par \uc2 There are also signs that Japan's government is digging in its heels, judging from how it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 For one, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's effort was much bigger than unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August 2011 and September 2010. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's willingness to help finance Europe's rescue efforts could mute criticism among euro-zone countries of its latest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tinkering, giving Japan more flexibility.\par \par \uc2 Alessandro Balsotti, an analyst at JW Partners, a Milan, Italy-based research and advisory firm for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedge funds, said "plenty of people" are buying yen in defiance of the Bank of Japan, but he's wary this time.\par \par \uc2 This trade "did work quite well the last two times, but I don't trust it this time," he said.\par \par \uc2 -By Neil Shah, Wall Street Journal; 212-416-2619; neil.shah@wsj.com [ 10-31-11 1740ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020111031e7av0004l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
