\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Surge On European Debt Deal\b0\par\par\uc2 2,617 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 2011\par 00:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      12208.55 +339.51  +2.86% \par Nasdaq     2738.63  +87.96  +3.32% \par S&P 500    1284.59  +42.59  +3.43% \par FTSE 100   5713.82 +160.58  +2.89% \par Xetra DAX  6337.84 +321.77  +5.35% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  99 28/32    -1/32 0.313%  +2.0BP \par 5Y Tsy   99 2/32   -17/32 1.195% +10.7BP \par 10Y Tsy 97 25/32 -1 17/32 2.380% +17.7BP \par  \par USD/JPY    75.98-6.00  -0.23% \par Range      76.28-75.68 \par EUR/USD    1.4186-88  +2.02% \par Range      1.4246-1.3866 \par AUD/USD    1.0720-24  +3.10% \par Range      1.0752-1.0386 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    10.0/10.91% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol   13.35/15.01% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    14.7/16.83% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,747.70 +$24.20 \par Nymex Crude (NY)       $93.96  +$3.76 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks rocketed higher Thursday as investors cheered an agreement reached by European leaders in their bid to resolve the euro zone's debt crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average closed with a gain of 339.51 points, or 2.9%, at 12208.55, moving above the 12000 level for the first time since early August and closing at the highest level since July 28. The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index rallied 42.59 points, or 3.4%, to 1284.59, reentering positive territory for 2011. The Nasdaq Composite leapt 87.96 points, or 3.3%, to 2738.63.\par \par \uc2 Financial stocks led the surge, and all sectors in the S&P 500 advanced, as investors warmed to the voluntary 50% write-down investors were set to take on sovereign Greek bonds, plus an increase in the firepower of the euro-zone bailout fund, to about $1.4 trillion. Greece will aim to reduce its debt to 120% of gross domestic product by 2020.\par \par \uc2 "More time has been bought," said Hank Herrmann, chief executive of \uc2 Waddell & Reed Financial\uc2 . "This doesn't look like they're just kicking the can down the road. It is more serious than things in the past."\par \par \uc2 The blue-chip Dow stocks that swing the most on euro-zone debt developments were the strongest gainers, as were its most economically sensitive stocks. \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  gained 63 cents, or 9.6%, to $7.22 and \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  jumped 98 cents, or 9.5%, to $11.34. The S&P 500's financial components leapt 6.2%.\par \par \uc2 The Thursday mega-rally pushed major stock indexes toward a few historic milestones. With two sessions remaining in October, both the blue-chip Dow Jones Industrial Average and the S&P 500 were poised for their largest monthly percentage gain in a quarter century. The Nasdaq Composite's October rally would be its largest in point terms since January 2001 if the gains as of Thursday keep up.\par \par \uc2 Trading volume was 6.59 billion shares in New York Stock Exchange composite volume, the eighth largest volume day of the year and a contrast with the light trading often seen on the market's advances in recent months.\par \par \uc2 Asian shares rallied Thursday after European leaders announced a deal for private holders of Greek bonds to take a voluntary writedown on the debt. Most Asian markets were already trading higher prior to the announcement, but the gains accelerated following news of the Greek deal,\par \par \uc2 The Shanghai Composite was the laggard among the major markets, rising just 0.3%, although it was the fourth consecutive session of gains for China shares.\par \par \uc2 The Shanghai index is likely to face strong resistance at 2500 for the next few sessions as it may consolidate after rising 5.0% so far this week, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "Investors are re-pricing stocks for a hoped-for monetary policy shift away from the 'prudent' stance adopted a year ago," said ING economist Tim Condon in a note.\par \par \uc2 However, some analysts warned that expectations the government will ease its monetary stance were overdone and Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao's comments about the need to "fine-tune" policy shouldn't be exaggerated.\par \par \uc2 "The index is running on optimism induced by Premier Wen's comments, but I think the upside is limited from current levels. A broader loosening of monetary policy is unlikely because inflation remains high," said Zhang Gang, an analyst at Central China Securities.\par \par \uc2 Baidu Inc\uc2 .'s (BIDU) third-quarter earnings jumped 80% as China's Internet-search leader continued to gain more users and bolster the revenue it gleaned from each. The company also said online-marketing customers increased 12% to 304,000, as revenue per customer jumped 65%. Its American depositary shares were up 9.1% at $150.99 in after-hours trading.\par \par \uc2 And in results due later in the day, \uc2 China Telecom\uc2 's (0728.HK) third-quarter ex-upfront connection fee net profit is likely to have risen 15% on the year to CNY4.34 billion, according to an average forecast of six analysts in a Dow Jones poll. Revenue excluding the fees is likely to have risen 11% to CNY61.88 billion.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares ended higher Thursday, led by mainland developers after the state-run China Securities Journal said Beijing may make its macroeconomic policy more accommodative by loosening credit controls and increasing public expenditure in the fourth quarter. The index also got a boost as European markets opened higher due to the positive news coming out of Wednesday's EU summit on the European debt crisis and reports China would invest in Europe's bailout fund.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said they expect the benchmark index to trade in a 18,900-20,400 range over the next two weeks as concerns about euro-zone debt ease.\par \par \uc2 "We can now see potential upside going forward as the political leaders in the euro zone have pretty much reached a consensus on containing the debt problems and it appears that China's tightening policy will soon ease up a bit," said Alvin Cheung, associate director of Prudential Brokerage Ltd.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks posted strong gains Thursday in a relief rally after European leaders secured a deal to reduce Greece's debt, which helped banks and financial shares, while a stronger euro also pushed up major exporters.\par \par \uc2 "The fact that they agreed on something was positive," said Kenichi Hirano, operating officer at Tachibana Securities. He added, however, that any plan could be vulnerable unless the Greek economy itself recovers.\par \par \uc2 Crude oil futures prices surged 4.2% in U.S. trading Thursday, hitting their highest level in nearly three months after the European deal on Greece's debt and data which showed the U.S. economy grew by its highest level in a year.\par \par \uc2 Gold and silver futures rallied to a five-week high as optimism over the European debt deal and a weaker dollar fanned demand for a store of value assets such as precious metals.\par \par \uc2 Base metals closed sharply higher on the London Metal Exchange Thursday, emboldened by optimism on Europe. LME three-month copper led the gains, soaring 6.5% to a five-week high before closing the session 6.1% higher.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Europe's marathon session that struck an accord on Greece's debt sent the euro surging to its highest level in seven weeks against the dollar Thursday, with investors hoping the agreement would usher the end of a crisis that has rattled markets for much of the last two years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The renewed optimism over Europe sent the dollar plunging across the board. It hit a new record low against the yen at Y75.66 and suffered its largest one-day percentage drop against the euro since July 2010 as traders exited positions considered safe from global turmoil. With investors gravitating toward high-yielding positions, the greenback suffered because of its low yield appeal and growing questions over the fiscal health of the U.S.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the face of broad pessimism, European leaders struck a deal at the 11th hour to implement a "voluntary" 50% reduction of Greece's debt held by private investors. Bowing to pressure for the "big bazooka" some analysts had hoped for, leaders also found a way to leverage its bailout facility by at least four times as much as the current EUR440 billion, to buttress the bond markets of Spain and Italy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts cautioned that Europe wasn't yet out of the financial or economic woods. However, euphoric investors--buffeted for weeks by grim euro zone headlines--responded by sending the euro up nearly four cents intraday before closing up more than 2% on the session.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We think it's the beginning of the end," said Douglas Borthwick, managing director at Faros Trading, speaking about the European deal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Because markets have been "married to European difficulties, it's very hard to switch views from being short euros to long euros," Borthwick said. That is creating momentum for the euro as investors dramatically scale back on bets the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will fall. Although risks remain, "the end of the wall of worry could see more gains in the euro."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Thursday in New York, the euro was at $1.4189 from $1.3906 late Wednesday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y75.94 from Y76.20, while the euro was at Y107.70 from Y105.98. The U.K. pound was at $1.6083 from $1.5962. The dollar was at CHF0.8598 from CHF0.8807.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , slid to its weakest since early September. It closed near 74.955 from 76.246.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The encouraging news out of Europe coalesced with surprisingly upbeat news on the U.S. economy. Separate reports showed growth accelerated by 2.5% last quarter, its fastest pace in a year. The data helped soothe concerns of a new recession.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite doubts about how long the current euro-inspired euphoria will last, Thursday's cheery atmosphere drove a major dent in the U.S. Treasury market, propelling long-dated yields to their highest marks in months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. government bond prices fell steadily through the session as the safety that these assets offer seemed to lose its appeal, at least for now. The afternoon's seven-year note auction received little interest compared with recent similar sales, a function of the overall distaste for the sector's low-yields in light of euro-zone leaders finally delivering a plan to address their region's debt crisis. [ 27-10-11 2203GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite a lack of detail, broad strokes of the euro-zone agreement--applying a 50% haircut on Greek debt, leveraging the bailout fund four- to five-fold and bolstering banks' capital cushions to 9%--were enough to satisfy market participants who'd grown accustomed to hoping for progress and getting nothing in return.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's a collective sigh of relief that we're not going to crash into a wall," said Art Steinmetz, chief investment officer at OppenheimerFunds. The euro-zone deal "is not big enough in my view, but we'll now see several months of relative calm with that as the last major event for the year."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors, however, should enjoy whatever relatively smooth sailing they can get. Steinmetz sees the euro-zone hurdles creeping back up once the European banks start revealing their recapitalization plans. The push to keep euro-zone banks well-cushioned will inevitably force those institutions to deleverage or stop lending--both of which will hinder economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In late Thursday afternoon trading in New York, benchmark 10-year notes sank 1 17/32 in price as yield bounced nearly 18 basis points to 2.380%. That's the highest yield since early August. The 30-year bond fell 4 2/32 in price as yields soared more than 20 basis points to 3.428%--the highest since early September. Bond prices move inversely from their yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds fell Thursday as additional easing measures from the Bank of Japan fell short of market expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's not a minus for JGBs, but surely it's not plus to the market. It won't help weakening the yen either," said Atsushi Ito, a senior analyst at UBS Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Looking ahead, investors will pay attention to whether \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will conduct a yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is still trading around record highs against its U.S. counterpart.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese government bonds ended lower as liquidity tightened following the PBOC's consecutive withdrawals in the money market, and as some investors turn to the stock market after the Shanghai Composite Index's recent sharp gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among the wealth of data due from Japan Friday, the country's core CPI likely rose 0.2% on the year in September, following August's 0.2% rise, according to a Dow Jones and Nikkei poll of economists. Tokyo core CPI--an early indicator of price trends for the rest of Japan--is tipped to have declined 0.4% on the year in October, likely as a result of a smaller contribution from energy prices to the Tokyo CPI compared with the nationwide indicator.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, industrial output likely fell 2.2% in September from a month earlier, marking the first decline in six months, according to a median forecast of economists polled by Dow Jones and the Nikkei. Output rose 0.8% in August.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par EU Deal On Greek Debt Gets Early Welcome \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hewlett-Packard\f28 \uc2 \f2  To Keep PC Business \par Europe Banks Vow Not To Issue Shares In Recap \par Capital Burden On European Banks Lighter Than Expected \par Baidu 3Q Profit Jumps 80%, Predicts Strong 4Q Revenue \par Exxon Profit Soars 41%; Production Drops \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Altria\f28 \uc2 \f2  Earnings Rise On Higher Margins \par Sarkozy Cuts French 2012 GDP Growth View To 1% \par AMD Swings To Profit; Shares Jump \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Las Vegas Sands\f28 \uc2 \f2  3Q Net Nearly Doubles \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Motorola Mobility\f28 \uc2 \f2  Narrows Loss \par Blackstone, Wells Fargo Buying Commercial Property Portfolio \par Chrysler Posts Profit Again \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Metlife\f28 \uc2 \f2  Net Surges On Derivatives, Investments, Annuities \par Tepco To Adopt Cost-Cutting Plans Ahead Of Government Assistance -Nikkei \par Autoparts Makers May Top 1st-Half Earnings Guidance -Nikkei \par Tepco To Get Y900B In Public Aid -Nikkei \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 JR East\f28 \uc2 \f2  To Buy Tokyu's Rail Car Manufacturing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -Nikkei \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2300  S Korea      Sep Balance of payments \par 2330  Japan        Sep CPI (Nation), CPI ex-Food (Nation) \par 2330  Japan        Oct CPI (Tokyo), CPI ex-Food (Tokyo) \par 2330  Japan        Sep Household Spending \par 2330  Japan        Sep Labour Force Survey \par 2350  Japan        Sep Preliminary Industrial Production \par 2350  Japan        Oct Provisional Trade Statistics for 1st 10 days of \par                    Month \par 0030  Japan      