\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Euro Whipsaws As EU Leaders Work On Debt, Bank Deals\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stephen L. Bernard   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 687 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2011\par 23:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Like a roller coaster, the euro had its ups and downs Wednesday but essentially finished right where it started.\par \par \uc2 A steady stream of headlines coming out of a summit of European leaders sent the euro on its wild ride, signaling just how sensitive investors are to any possible plans to stabilize the region's debt crisis. The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded at a six-week high of $1.3976 and as low as $1.3798 during the global session.\par \par \uc2 Late Wednesday, it was trading at $1.3906 against the dollar from $1.3908 late Tuesday, according to EBS via CQG. The euro rose to Y105.98 from Y105.80.\par \par \uc2 "There were no real surprises and no real disappointments," Brian Dolan, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .com in Bedminster, N.J., said of the final sum of headlines that came out of Europe. That helped lead the euro to a relatively flat day in U.S. trading.\par \par \uc2 However, Dolan noted that the true reaction to the news won't come until European investors start trading Thursday. Most of the headlines came out after European markets closed for the day, so there it is still too early to say how things will shake out.\par \par \uc2 A constant barrage of headlines early in the New York trading session Wednesday initially sent the euro higher, particularly news that German lawmakers had approved a plan to endorse leveraging the European Financial Stability Facility, the region's bailout fund. However, that rally was quickly stopped in its tracks after news hit that European officials and banks were deadlocked on how to write down the value of Greek debt.\par \par \uc2 But the resilient euro staged another rally, climbing on news that German Chancellor Angela Merkel and French President Nicolas Sarkozy were ready to negotiate with banks on a Greek debt restructuring and talk that countries in Asia might provide some financial support to the battered euro zone.\par \par \uc2 There was some broad agreement on plans, including increasing bank capital reserve ratios, which would help protect banks in times of stress. European leaders have been trying to hammer out plans to deploy EFSF funds, restructure Greek debt and recapitalize banks.\par \par \uc2 The dollar, meanwhile, rose slightly against the yen as many investors remain on watch for potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese government. The dollar gained against the yen after some upbeat economic data out of the U.S. buoyed the greenback and encouraged hopes that the economy will continue to grow. But such a move still doesn't preclude Japan from jumping into to the market to weaken the yen in an effort to help exporters.\par \par \uc2 "We've heard rumblings from the [\b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 ], and if we touch Y75, that seems to be a psychologically round number," said John McCarthy, managing director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ING Capital Markets in New York. "If we approach [Y75] in a quick manner, you could see \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is scheduled to meet Thursday.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  unilaterally in the market in early August will only fleeting results. The government sold more than Y4.5 trillion during that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pushing the yen from about Y77 to Y80. It took only five days for the yen to fall back below pre-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose to Y76.16 from Y76.09 late Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The greenback also climbed against the Swiss franc, which effectively stopped receiving safe-haven flows after its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set a floor in the euro-franc exchange rate and pledged to defend that level with unlimited resources. The dollar rose to CHF0.8807 from CHF0.8778.\par \par \uc2 The U.K. pound was at $1.5981 from $1.5996. The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at about 76.209 from about 76.122.\par \par \uc2 -By Stephen L. Bernard, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-4528; stephen.bernard@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Anusha Shrivastava contributed to this report. [ 10-26-11 1716ET ]\par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020111026e7aq00090\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
