\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks End Higher; Asian Markets To Digest Summit News\b0\par\par\uc2 3,222 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2011\par 00:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Indian markets are closed Thursday for Diwali Balipratipada.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      11869.04 +162.42  +1.39% \par Nasdaq     2650.67  +12.25  +0.46% \par S&P 500    1242.00  +12.95  +1.05% \par FTSE 100   5553.24  +27.70  +0.50% \par Xetra DAX  6016.07  -30.68  -0.51% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  99 29/32  -1/32 0.289% +1.9BP \par 5Y Tsy  99 22/32 -10/32 1.064% +6.2BP \par 10Y Tsy  99 9/32 -22/32 2.203% +7.8BP \par  \par USD/JPY    76.19-22  +0.16% \par Range      76.32-75.72 \par EUR/USD    1.3907-10  +0.01% \par Range      1.3974-1.3800 \par AUD/USD    1.0400-04  -0.25% \par Range      1.0434-1.0324 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    10.05/11.1% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    14.75/15.25% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    16.25/17.3% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,723.50 +$23.10 \par Nymex Crude (NY)       $90.20  -$2.97 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks finished a rocky day with strong gains as investors grew optimistic about European leaders' ability to contain the region's debt crisis, taking hope from the possibility of Chinese investment in the euro-zone bailout fund.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average added 162.42 points, or 1.4%, to 11869.04, recouping most of the previous day's losses. The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index gained 12.95 points, or 1.1%, to 1242.00, while the Nasdaq Composite added 12.25 points, or 0.46%, to 2650.67.\par \par \uc2 The gains came after a volatile session that saw the Dow shoot up 162 points in the opening minutes of the day, only to slide into negative territory later. In the afternoon, stocks rebounded to the day's highs after reports China would invest in Europe's bailout fund.\par \par \uc2 "We're marginally on the path towards something concrete coming out. Something is emerging, and that's been helpful for the market," said Quincy Krosby, market strategist at \uc2 Prudential Financial\uc2 , pointing to German parliamentary support for a leveraged bailout fund and reports about Chinese bond buying. "Leverage requires money, and if you get the Chinese involved, that's going to allow more money," she said.\par \par \uc2 The gyrations began before the opening bell, after German lawmakers approved leveraging the euro zone's emergency bailout fund. But later in the morning, reports suggested the size of the bailout fund may not be clear for several weeks, damping some of the initial enthusiasm.\par \par \uc2 Amid the flurry of headlines, investors remained skeptical about a quick resolution to Europe's debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 "The strategy right now is to keep kicking the Greek issue down the road and buy more time," said Lionel Mellul, partner at Momentum Trading Partners. "You've had a rally that's been fueled by hopes, but it looks like more of a tactical rally than something very sound."\par \par \uc2 Leading the gains were energy, financial and materials stocks. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  was the strongest performer among Dow components, rising 4.5% after the aerospace company beat third-quarter earnings estimates and raised its full-year outlook, although revenue was weaker than expected.\par \par \uc2 On the downside was \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2 's disappointing earnings result, which weighed on the Nasdaq and on consumer-discretionary stocks, the only sector to finish in the red. \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  slumped 13% after the online retailer missed earnings expectations and reported a drop in operating margins.\par \par \uc2 In economic news, U.S. durable-goods orders decreased by 0.8% in September from the prior month, largely in line with estimates, though business spending on equipment unexpectedly jumped. Separately, new-home sales rose 5.7% in September from the month before, the first gain in five months, though prices continued dropping.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended higher for a third consecutive session Wednesday on hopes that Beijing would likely make its macroeconomic policy more accommodative to stabilize economic growth.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the short-term upward trend in the market may remain intact as investors expect Beijing to take more supportive measures for small businesses. They pegged immediate resistance for the Shanghai index at 2450.\par \par \uc2 "The market is likely to consolidate with an upward bias in coming sessions as Premier Wen's comments intensified expectations for a policy 'fine-tuning,' although they aren't signaling a shift in monetary-policy stance," said Gold State Securities analyst Huang Bin.\par \par \uc2 Premier Wen Jiabao said Tuesday that China should "fine-tune" its policy at an appropriate time and by an appropriate degree, and maintain reasonable lending supply growth.\par \par \uc2 Bank of America-Merrill Lynch economist Lu Ting said Wen's pledge "signals Beijing is ready to roll out more pro-growth measures."\par \par \uc2 "It may not be defined as the turning point for policy stance yet, but Beijing's flexibility is likely needed at the moment (we see some signs of over-tightening) and this flexibility supports our call of a soft-landing," he added.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's benchmark index rose for a fourth straight session Wednesday, helped by strength in Chinese markets though the fluid situation over the euro zone's debt problems remain a key concern among investors.\par \par \uc2 "Hong Kong stocks and A-shares have been outperforming lately on easing concerns about a hard landing in China, said Daniel So, a strategist at SHK Financial.\par \par \uc2 Two of China's four largest state-owned banks Wednesday reported slower profit growth for the third quarter, as they set aside more money to cover potential bad loans amid China's cooling economy.\par \par \uc2 Bank of China Ltd\uc2 .'s (3988.HK) net profit rose 9% from a year earlier to CNY29.79 billion (US$4.6 billion) in the July-September period, on the back of higher net interest income and fee income. The gain lagged behind the 28% rise it reported in the first half. \uc2 Agricultural Bank of China Ltd\uc2 .'s (1288.HK) third-quarter net profit rose 40% to CNY34.09 billion, slowing from the 45% increase in the January-June period.\par \par \uc2 Both banks signaled they share widespread concerns about a possible increase in bad loans. The earnings reports came after Wednesday's market close.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks posted a modest loss Wednesday as investor caution prevailed ahead of the highly anticipated EU summit, while earnings-related news factored into sharp price movements among individual firms, including \uc2 JFE Holdings\uc2 , Nidec, Kao, and Nintendo.\par \par \uc2 Oil futures pulled back in U.S. trading Wednesday after the U.S. government reported a bigger-than-expected increase in oil stockpiles. The news undercut fears of supply shortages, which had pushed the market up 8% in the previous three sessions.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures settled at a five-week high, buoyed by renewed appetite in low risk assets ahead of the European Union's announcement of a comprehensive plan for Europe's debt problems.\par \par \uc2 Base metals closed the day mostly lower on the London Metal Exchange Wednesday, following a volatile trading session that saw the metals swing in wide ranges on the back of newsflow out of the Europe.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Like a roller coaster, the euro had its ups and downs Wednesday but essentially finished right where it started.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A steady stream of headlines coming out of a summit of European leaders sent the euro on its wild ride, signaling just how sensitive investors are to any possible plans to stabilize the region's debt crisis. The common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traded at a six-week high of $1.3976 and as low as $1.3798 during the global session.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Wednesday in New York, it was trading at $1.3906 against the dollar from $1.3908 late Tuesday, according to EBS via CQG. The euro rose to Y105.98 from Y105.80.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There were no real surprises and no real disappointments," Brian Dolan, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .com in Bedminster, N.J., said of the final sum of headlines that came out of Europe. That helped lead the euro to a relatively flat day in U.S. trading.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, Dolan noted that the true reaction to the news won't come until European investors start trading Thursday. Most of the headlines came out after European markets closed for the day, so there it is still too early to say how things will shake out.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A constant barrage of headlines early in the New York trading session Wednesday initially sent the euro higher, particularly news that German lawmakers had approved a plan to endorse leveraging the European Financial Stability Facility, the region's bailout fund. However, that rally was quickly stopped in its tracks after news hit that European officials and banks were deadlocked on how to write down the value of Greek debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the resilient euro staged another rally, climbing on news that German Chancellor Angela Merkel and French President Nicolas Sarkozy were ready to negotiate with banks on a Greek debt restructuring and talk that countries in Asia might provide some financial support to the battered euro zone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There was some broad agreement on plans, including increasing bank capital reserve ratios, which would help protect banks in times of stress. European leaders have been trying to hammer out plans to deploy EFSF funds, restructure Greek debt and recapitalize banks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar, meanwhile, rose slightly against the yen as many investors remain on watch for potential \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Japanese government. The dollar gained against the yen after some upbeat economic data out of the U.S. buoyed the greenback and encouraged hopes that the economy will continue to grow. But such a move still doesn't preclude Japan from jumping into to the market to weaken the yen in an effort to help exporters.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We've heard rumblings from the (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ), and if we touch Y75, that seems to be a psychologically round number," said John McCarthy, managing director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at ING Capital Markets in New York. "If we approach (Y75) in a quick manner, you could see \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is scheduled to meet Thursday.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unilaterally in the market in early August with only fleeting results. The government sold more than Y4.5 trillion during that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , pushing the yen from about Y77 to Y80. It took only five days for the yen to fall back below pre-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  levels.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar rose to Y76.16 from Y76.09 late Tuesday. [ 26-10-11 2228GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The greenback also climbed against the Swiss franc, which effectively stopped receiving safe-haven flows after its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set a floor in the euro-franc exchange rate and pledged to defend that level with unlimited resources. The dollar rose to CHF0.8807 from CHF0.8778.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K. pound was at $1.5981 from $1.5996. The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 76.209 from 76.122.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The head of the group representing banks in European debt negotiations said late Wednesday there had been no agreement yet on how much the private sector would accept on Greek debt write-downs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There has been no agreement on any Greek deal or a specific "haircut," Charles Dallara, managing director of the Institute of International Finance said in a statement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, a draft statement likely to be issued by euro-zone leaders at the end of a summit Wednesday will say that the leaders have reached an agreement on leveraging the region's bailout fund "several fold," as part of a comprehensive package of measures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The draft, seen by Dow Jones Newswires, said the euro zone has agreed on two options to leverage the European Financial Stability Facility, which include credit enhancements for debt issued by member states as well as the creation of special purpose vehicles which will be funded by both private and public investors. Final details on how this will work will be completed next month, the draft communique said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The battle between euro-zone hopes and fears fueled wild price swings in Treasurys Wednesday, but investors kept their faith in a coming resolution, dragging the market to session lows toward the end of the day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bouts of anxiety pushed Treasurys higher earlier in the session because of concerns that euro-zone leaders wouldn't hammer out a credible resolution to their region's debt crisis. But ultimately, despite the lack of any sort of grand plan, hints of progress were enough to keep investors cautiously optimistic.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The important thing to remember is that the emphasis is now on when there will be a solution, not if there will be a solution, and that's a good development," said Guy LeBas, chief fixed-income strategist at Janney Montgomery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kevin Flanagan, senior fixed-income strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2 , said tempered expectations also helped keep the somewhat upbeat mood intact because "no one expects a be-all, end-all solution" to arise from the European summit Wednesday. That's why as long as there's a credible plan, euro-zone leaders will have delivered on what they advertised, and investors desperate for some kind of progress will take it.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite a number of attempted rallies, Treasury prices sank to session lows late in U.S. trading, with benchmark 10-year notes falling 22/32 in price to yield 2.203%, while 30-year bonds lost 1 19/32 to yield 3.220%. Two-year notes shed 1/32 to yield 0.289%. Debt prices move inversely to their yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, the net Treasurys selling doesn't mean all is well with Europe. As the self-imposed deadline for a euro-zone solution creeps closer, mounting nerves about officials delivering a full-fledged plan is keeping investors from straying too far from the safety of U.S. government bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many analysts say that at this point, financial-market participants are looking for details of a concrete plan of action, not more plans to have a plan. So after weeding through the wide-ranging and often-times conflicting headlines, investors are demanding a sustainable and detailed resolution.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's still up in the air on how to boost the (euro zone bailout) fund, and the key thing for Greece is if it triggers a credit event," said Orlando Green, fixed-income strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Agricole\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London. "It'll be worrying if there's a delay in deciding the haircut."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's benchmark 10-year yield fell below the psychologically important 1% mark for the first time in two weeks, as the yen's latest spike to a record high has heightened expectations for additional easing by the Bank of Japan at its Thursday meeting.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Citigroup Global Markets Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  senior strategist Maki Shimizu expects the 10-year yield to trade in a range of 0.98%-1.02% for the rest of the week, maintaining a downward bias until around mid-November.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended higher Wednesday as the PBOC may decide not to sell three-year bonds at Thursday's auction.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  didn't ask about three-year bonds in its regular survey of banks' interest in its debt securities Wednesday, possibly indicating that it may not offer such bonds Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We may see more signs of policy change in the PBOC's open-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and I suspect the PBOC will let the rate of 1-year bill fall some time soon," a Shanghai-based local bank trader said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among events Thursday, the Bank of Japan will likely discuss additional easing at its one-day meeting, as the yen hits a fresh high against the U.S. dollar amid growing worries over the European debt crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the BOJ were to take action, the most likely option would be to increase its asset-purchase program--its primary weapon for credit easing--from the current Y50 trillion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BOJ is also slated to release its semiannual outlook report on the economy and prices. The board is likely to forecast the core CPI for fiscal 2013 to be below 1.0% on the year, the level it considers to indicate price stability, suggesting it may stick to its ultra-easy monetary policy until the spring of 2014.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par IIF's Dallara: There Is No Agreement On Greek Debt Write-Downs \par Euro-Zone Leaders Draft: Agree On Comprehensive Set Of Additional Measures \par Japan Fin Min Azumi: Will Closely Watch \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Market In Tokyo \par Sarkozy, Merkel Ready To Meet With Banks For Greece Debt Talks Wed - Source \par Bigger Haircuts Would Make Greek CDS Trigger More Likely \par Visa Profit Up 14% On Higher Transaction Volume \par Italy Delivers Reform Plan In Bid To Reassure EU \par EBA: 13 Countries Need EUR106.4B To Reach 9% Core Tier One Ratio \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Aflac\f28 \uc2 \f2  3Q Core Profit Rises On Strong Yen \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Freddie Mac\f28 \uc2 \f2  CEO To Step Down \par Gupta Pleads Not Guilty In Insider Case \par UAW Declares Chrysler Tentative Pact Ratified \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Owens-Illinois\f28 \uc2 \f2  3Q Profit Falls 17% \par US Durables Orders Dip 0.8%; Home Sales Rise \par Mf Global To Explore Options \par Oil Inventories Rise More Than Expected \par BP Gets First Drilling Permit For US Gulf Post Oil Spill \par New Zealand \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Leaves Cash Rate Unchanged At 2.50% \par New Zealand September Trade Deficit Widens On Increase In Imports \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nippon Yusen\f28 \uc2 \f2  Inks Shuttle Tanker Deal In North Sea -Nikkei \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Asahi Glass\f28 \uc2 \f2  Op Profit Seen Down 18% For Jan-Sept -Nikkei \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kyushu Electric\f28 \uc2 \f2  To Seek 5% Cut In Power Use This Winter -Nikkei \par Pretax Earnings Seen Sinking At \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nippon Yusen\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mitsui OSK\f28 \uc2 \f2  -Nikkei \par Mizuho, \f28 \uc2 \f2 China UnionPay\f28 \uc2 \f2  Joining Hands In Credit Card Ops -Nikkei \par ANA's Interim Operating Profit Seen Sliding 10% Y50B -Nikkei \par Reso