\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE: Japan Finance Minister Won't Rule Out Any Options Vs Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 694 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2011\par 05:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Japan's finance minister vows not to rule out any options to battle strong yen\par \par \uc2 -- Says issued a fresh instruction to MOF bureaucrats to stay ready to respond to any \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  developments\par \par \uc2 -- Says speculative moves stand out in yen's rise this week\par \par \uc2 (Adds later Azumi comments on BOJ, possible measures ahead of Thursday meeting 5th-7th paragraphs.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's finance minister made it clear Wednesday he won't rule out \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market to curb the yen's strength, as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's record-busting rise threatens to further damage the country's already moderating economic recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell to a new post-World War II low of Y75.73 overnight, as renewed doubts over the ability of Europeans leaders to solve their debt and banking woes drove investors to seek refuge in what they perceive to be safe investments, including the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We will not rule out any possible measure in dealing with this," Jun Azumi told reporters at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "I've just instructed the ministry staffers again to make preparations so that we can act in response to anything."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Azumi acknowledged that the yen has risen recently in response to Europe's debt crisis and weak U.S. economic data---factors some analysts say justify the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movement. Still, he called the yen's appreciation this week largely "speculative," adding that it "is not reflecting real economies at all."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He also called on the Bank of Japan to play a part in helping to weaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  through further monetary easing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think we share this sense of crisis and I hope (the BOJ) will act appropriately and in a timely manner," he said in later remarks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's policy board meets Thursday and is expected to consider fresh easing measures, most likely by expanding its asset-purchase program from the current Y50 trillion. The facility is considered to be the BOJ's primary weapon for credit easing since key interest rates have already been lowered to virtually zero.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Azumi made the remarks after he met with ministry bureaucrats, including Kenta Ichikawa, head of the ministry's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Markets Division, which executes \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  using the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as an agent.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar rose to around Y76.15 on Azumi's comments. Some traders took his remarks to mean that Japan might step into the market to sell the yen. But the gain in the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was only a little more than a tenth of a yen, suggesting many others discount the risk of imminent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  amid no convincing signs that the U.S. and Europe--both in their own economic trouble--would endorse Japanese action.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As the morning session progressed, the dollar again weakened, falling briefly below the Y76.00 level. As of 0352 GMT, the greenback was trading at Y76.04.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 About two months into his job, Azumi is under increasing pressure to undertake another \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to undercut the yen, action last taken on Aug. 4 under the leadership of his predecessor Yoshihiko Noda, currently prime minister.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yen rises are undermining the international competitiveness of Japanese exports at a time when a global slowdown is beginning to weigh more on the nation's economy, which needs a boost from exports to recover from the March 11 earthquake and tsunami. The government is also worried that Japanese manufacturers may shift more of production abroad in efforts to prevent the strong yen from eroding profits they earn through exports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We are aware that the export industry--the pillar of the Japanese economy--will be forced into a very tough situation if (the yen's uptrend) continues for long," Azumi said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's current levels are well below an average Y81.15 on which roughly 1,200 large Japanese manufacturers have told the Bank of Japan they are basing their profit forecast for the ongoing business year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com [ 26-10-11 0356GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111026e7aq0006p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
