\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Not Ruled Out:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Finance Minister\b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi   \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL   \par \uc2 505 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2011\par 03:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister made it clear Wednesday he won't rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to curb the yen's strength, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's record-busting rise threatens to further damage the country's already-moderating economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to a new post-World War II low of Y75.73 overnight, as renewed doubts over the ability of European leaders to solve their debt and banking woes drove investors to seek refuge in what they perceive to be safe investments, including the yen.\par \par \uc2 "We will not rule out every possible measure in dealing with this," Jun Azumi told reporters at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "I've just instructed the ministry staffers again to make preparations so that we can act in response to anything."\par \par \uc2 Azumi acknowledged the yen has risen recently in response to Europe's debt crisis and weak U.S. economic data -- factors some analysts say justify the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement. Still, he called the yen's appreciation this week largely "speculative," adding that it "is not reflecting real economies at all."\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 The finance minister made the remarks after he met with ministry bureaucrats, including Kenta Ichikawa, head of the ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Markets Division, which executes \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  using the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an agent.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose to around Y76.15 on Azumi's comments. Some traders took his remarks to mean that Japan might step into the market to sell the yen. But the gain in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was only a little more than a tenth of a yen, suggesting many others discount the risk of imminent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid no convincing signs that the U.S. and Europe -- both in their own economic trouble -- would endorse Japanese action.\par \par \uc2 About two months into his job, Azumi is under increasing pressure to undertake another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to undercut the yen, action last taken on Aug. 4 under the leadership of his predecessor Yoshihiko Noda, currently prime minister.\par \par \uc2 Yen rises are undermining the international competitiveness of Japanese exports at a time when a global slowdown is beginning to weigh more on the nation's economy, which needs a boost from exports to recover from the March 11 earthquake and tsunami. The government is also worried that Japanese manufacturers may shift more of production abroad in efforts to prevent the strong yen from eroding profits they earn through exports.\par \par \uc2 "We are aware that the export industry -- the pillar of the Japanese economy -- will be forced into a very tough situation if (the yen's uptrend) continues for long," Azumi said.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current levels are well below an average Y81.15 on which roughly 1,200 large Japanese manufacturers have told the Bank of Japan they are basing their profit forecast for the ongoing business year. [ 10-25-11 2149ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020111026e7aq00006\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
