\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Euro Rises As Market Hopes For End To Europe Debt Saga\b0\par\par\uc2 By Javier E. David   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 743 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2011\par 23:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The euro advanced Monday on increasing speculation that European leaders may be nearing a deal to enhance the euro zone's bailout facility, thus alleviating a source of anxiety for investors worried about the fallout from Greece's debt problems.\par \par \uc2 In the wake of a weekend summit, European Union leadership sketched the outlines of an EUR107 billion ($150 billion) plan to inject funds into distressed European banks. However, no firm decision has been reached on how much of a loss Greek investors will have to take on their debt holdings, which could result in at least a 30% write down on the value of their bonds.\par \par \uc2 Europe's debt crisis has taken a toll on the Continent's economic health, vividly illustrated by weak manufacturing data from France and Germany. The dour figures have raised the prospect that the euro zone's two economic powerhouses may fall into recession, and that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have to cut borrowing costs in order to ward off those effects.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, the euro advanced on cautious optimism that German Chancellor Angela Merkel and French President Nicholas Sarkozy can strike an accord on how to leverage the euro zone's bailout fund in a way that buttresses the euro zone's distressed economies and prevents the euro zone's problems from undercutting the global economy.\par \par \uc2 "Merkel and Sarkozy are now on the same page and moving in the same direction on the short-term issues," said Kevin Hebner, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2 . "We're moving slowly along the track but not off track."\par \par \uc2 Late Monday, the euro was at $1.3929 from $1.3891 late Friday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y76.09 from Y76.28, while the euro was at Y106.02 from Y105.94. The U.K. pound was at $1.6019 from $1.5959. The dollar was at CHF0.8805 from CHF0.8825.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 76.083 from about 76.384.\par \par \uc2 As investors piled back into stocks and high-yielding assets, the lift the greenback has gotten from investors seeking shelter from Europe's debt crisis evaporated. Those same flows have also helped push the yen -- traditionally a safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- to a record high against the dollar below Y76 just last week. The dollar flirted with that level again Monday, but fears that Japanese officials may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped contain its losses.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister vowed to take "resolute action" on the strong yen. Traders interpreted that as a possibility the world's third largest economy could enter markets to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , lest it undercut the country's efforts to engineer an export-led recovery.\par \par \uc2 With Europe's debt saga dominating headlines, the U.S. economy has quietly surprised analysts with a string of vigorous data that suggests a recession is becoming less of a probability. Many large banks have begun revising upward their expectations for third quarter growth, with economists estimating the economy expanded by at least 2% at an annualized rate.\par \par \uc2 While that pace would be better than some feared earlier this year, it is still below what is considered the U.S. economy's normal rate of trend growth. With unemployment still weak, the Federal Reserve has refused to rule out a third round of quantitative easing, or QE3, to spur job creation.\par \par \uc2 "The data in the U.S. has been half-decent, and we've had quite a bit of data surprises," said Alan Ruskin, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . "QE3 is more likely to only happen in an environment where growth starts to slow down," he said, but added that hints from Fed officials are positive for high-yielding assets like stocks and the euro.\par \par \uc2 As such, the dollar has failed to receive the benefit of the better-than-expected data. Although stock market investors have reacted positively to the prospect of more easing, the very suggestion is toxic to a greenback whose allure remains undermined by ultra-low interest rates and more than three years worth of monetary stimulus.\par \par \uc2 -By Javier E. David, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-4564; javier.david@dowjones.com [ 10-24-11 1717ET ]\par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020111024e7ao0008b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
