\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 3- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  sounds  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  alarm on strong yen\b0\par\par\uc2 853 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2011\par 02:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  finance minister warns dollar/yen moves speculative\par \par \uc2 * Says has instructed MOF staff to prepare for action\par \par \uc2 * Analysts do not rule out more \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Exports beat expectations but outlook is in doubt\par \par \uc2 (Adds business lobby's and vice finance minister's comments)\par \par \uc2 By Stanley White and Kaori Kaneko\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 24 (Reuters) - Japan's finance minister put traders on alert for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday as the yen's rise to a record high against the dollar threatened to further squeeze exporters' profits and hold back economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Japan's export growth showed signs of resilience, slowing less than expected in September, finance ministry data showed, but economists warn that persistent yen strength and Europe's sovereign debt woes pose increasing risks to external demand.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan, which meets on Thursday, will probably cut its economic forecasts because of slowing global growth but keep monetary policy unchanged unless disappointment over Europe's plans to solve its crisis roils markets.\par \par \uc2 Even if the BOJ keeps policy unchanged this week, Japan's government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not be able to hold off from taking action much longer as safe-haven flows keep the yen stubbornly high against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The dollar/yen rate fell sharply, to between 75 and 76 yen, in a short time. This is an utterly speculative move and not reflecting the economic fundamentals at all. This is regrettable," Finance Minister Jun Azumi told reporters.\par \par \uc2 "If this move becomes excessive, we have to take decisive action. I already instructed my staff on Saturday to be prepared to take action."\par \par \uc2 Azumi added that the strong yen would have a major impact on Japan's export sector, especially the auto industry, and could dent the country's economic recovery from a slump triggered by the March 11 earthquake and tsunami.\par \par \uc2 Azumi spoke after the dollar hit a record low of 75.78 yen\par \par \uc2 on trading platform EBS on Friday. That surpassed its previous record low of 75.94 yen in August and brought back into focus the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose slightly after Azumi's remark but later ceded ground to trade around 76.25 yen.\par \par \uc2 Japan's biggest business lobby echoed Azumi's concern about exports and on Monday urged \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the authorities even if they have to go it alone.\par \par \uc2 "I would like to see firm action by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including steps such as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its own," Nippon Keidanren Chairman Hiromasa Yonekura said at a news conference.\par \par \uc2 UNILATERAL \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Analysts do not rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , most likely unilateral, if the yen continues to rise.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if dollar/yen stays below 76 or falls below 75. Unless it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yen may continue to rise and verbal warnings alone may not be able to reverse that trend," said Yoshiki Shinke, chief economist at Dai-ichi Life Research Institute.\par \par \uc2 Traders in Tokyo, however, were sceptical whether the latest market action would serve as a trigger for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , citing a broad sell-off in the dollar as the main driver and data showing that margin traders have recently built up long dollar/yen positions.\par \par \uc2 Since September of last year, the government has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice on its own and once jointly with other Group of Seven rich nations to weaken the yen, but the effects of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  have proved short-lived.\par \par \uc2 The government needs to be ready to respond if the pace of speculative \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  move picks up, Vice Finance Minister Fumihiko Igarashi warned.\par \par \uc2 Japan's exports rose 2.4 percent in September from a year earlier, boosted by shipments of cars and car parts. That compared with a median forecast for a 1.0 percent increase, and followed a 2.8 percent climb in the year to August.\par \par \uc2 Imports increased 12.1 percent in September, against a forecast of a 12.6 percent rise.\par \par \uc2 The trade balance turned to a surplus of 300.4 billion yen ($3.95 billion) following the previous month's deficit. That compared with a median forecast of a 198.8 billion yen surplus.\par \par \uc2 Exports to Asia, which account for more than half of Japan's total exports, edged up 0.2 percent from a year earlier, with China taking in 2.7 percent more Japanese goods than a year ago, while exports to the United States were up 0.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese economy probably rebounded in the third quarter from the damage caused by the March 11 disaster but is expected to slow to a crawl in the final quarter due to an intensifying euro-zone debt crisis that threatens to drag down the world economy, a Reuters poll shows.\par \par \uc2 Euro zone leaders are striving to agree on new steps to reduce Greece's debt, strengthen the capital of banks with exposure to troubled euro zone sovereigns and leverage the euro zone's rescue fund to stem contagion to bigger economies.\par \par \uc2 ($1=76.13 yen)\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Tomasz Janowski and Neil Fullick)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/ (WRAPUP 3)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111024e7ao0000u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
