\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Rise; Nasdaq Back In Positive Territory For 2011\b0\par\par\uc2 3,110 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 2011\par 00:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      11913.62 +104.83  +0.89% \par Nasdaq     2699.44  +61.98  +2.35% \par S&P 500    1254.19  +15.94  +1.29% \par FTSE 100   5548.06  +59.41  +1.08% \par Xetra DAX  6055.27  +84.31  +1.41% \par  \par 2Y Tsy 99 22/32 -0/32 0.283% +0.8BP \par 5Y Tsy 99 18/32 -5/32 1.086% +2.9BP \par 10Y Tsy 99 3/32 -6/32 2.228% +2.3BP \par  \par USD/JPY    76.06-08  -0.19% \par Range      76.47-76.00 \par EUR/USD    1.3926-28  +0.60% \par Range      1.3956-1.3824 \par AUD/USD    1.0470-74  +1.18% \par Range      1.0500-1.0318 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol     9.8/10.4% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    15.2/15.9% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol   17.36/18.3% \par  \par Comex Gold        $1,652.30   +$16.20 \par Comex Gold Range  $1,636.60-$1,663.30 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $91.27    +$3.87 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks rose Monday, pushing the Nasdaq Composite into positive territory for the year and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  500-stock index to the cusp of it.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average advanced 104.83 points, or 0.89%, to 11913.62, the fourth gain in five sessions. The blue-chip measure was led by Caterpillar, which jumped $4.38, or 5%, to 91.77, after posting a strong earnings report that helped set the session's positive tone. The Dow's climb built on a four-week string of gains that has been the blue-chip index's longest weekly win streak since January.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index added 15.94 points, or 1.3%, to 1254.19, finishing 0.27% below break-even for 2011. The Nasdaq Composite surged 61.98 points, or 2.4%, to 2699.44, pushing the technology-heavy index up 1.8% for the year. The Russell 2000 index of small-capitalization stocks gained 23.61, or 3.31%, to 736.03, for the highest close since Aug. 3.\par \par \uc2 Besides Caterpillar's earnings, investors reacted to a string of merger announcements, favorable manufacturing data from China and a lack of negative headlines from Europe. Also helping was a Federal Reserve official's comment that a third round of bond buying, known as "quantitative easing," was possible.\par \par \uc2 "Any evidence that (corporations) are going to start using their capital helps the market," said James W. Paulsen, chief investment strategist at \uc2 Wells Capital Management\uc2 . "Plus we've had this continued dribble of good earnings."\par \par \uc2 Cigna added 64 cents, or 1.4%, to 45.34, after making what amounted to a bet on Medicare in a $3.8 billion deal for HealthSpring. HealthSpring focuses on Medicare Advantage, which provides elderly people Medicare services through private companies. Shares leapt 13.55, or 34%, to 53.71.\par \par \uc2 Oracle\uc2  rose 75 cents, or 2.3%, to 32.87, and RightNow Technologies surged 6.98, or 19%, to 42.94, after the business-software company said it would buy the cloud-computing firm in a deal valued at $1.43 billion.\par \par \uc2 Sara Lee\uc2  sold the majority of its North American food-service coffee and tea \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \uc2 J.M. Smucker\uc2  for $350 million in cash. \uc2 Sara Lee\uc2  edged down 12 cents, or 0.7%, to 17.65, and Smucker's stock added 52 cents, or 0.7%, to 77.04.\par \par \uc2 Mattel\uc2  agreed to buy \uc2 HIT Entertainment\uc2 , which sells the Bob the Builder and Fireman Sam toy lines, for $680 million, from a consortium led by \uc2 Apax Partners\uc2  funds. The deal also gives \uc2 Mattel\uc2  ownership of well-known preschool brands Thomas & Friends and Barney. \uc2 Mattel\uc2  rose 57 cents, or 2.1%, to 28.26.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended higher Monday, breaking a four-session losing streak after preliminary data showed stronger manufacturing activity in October.\par \par \uc2 The gains bring the Shanghai index closer to psychological resistance at 2400 points, though the market's near-term performance remains difficult to predict, said Zheshang Securities analyst Wang Weijun.\par \par \uc2 Despite the day's gains, analysts said the domestic market may continue to face difficulties due an uncertain global outlook.\par \par \uc2 "The market situation is unstable, and it will remain that way in the near term," said Capital Care Securities analyst Amy Lin. "Looking ahead to next year, there are real concerns that the global economy will come under significant pressure."\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares rose Monday, driven by easing concerns about China's economic growth and expectations that a European Union summit will find a solution to its debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 However, market watchers cautioned against excessive optimism.\par \par \uc2 "We highlight the binary risks emanating from resolution of the summit. We believe E.U. policymakers will deliver just enough to sustain the risk asset rally," said Bank of America-Merrill Lynch.\par \par \uc2 Haitong International economist Yifan Hu said: "We do not expect the European Union summit can reach any concrete measures in such a short time, but their efforts could temporarily alleviate market fear and help improve market confidence."\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks rose Monday on hopes that European leaders will forge a broad plan to address the region's debt crisis, while China manufacturing data helped sustain investor enthusiasm for Fanuc, Komatsu, and other companies directly exposed to that nation's markets.\par \par \uc2 Ongoing uncertainty over how decisively European leaders will act continues to be a volume inhibitor, Yumi Nishimura, senior market analyst at Daiwa Securities, said.\par \par \uc2 Crude-oil futures rose to their highest level in nearly three months in heavy U.S. trading Monday, settling above $90 a barrel as a condition emerged in the market suggesting a supply squeeze in the near term.\par \par \uc2 The spread between the Nymex crude-oil contracts for the current month and next month turned strongly positive Monday, entering a state called "backwardation" in which it is more expensive to buy a commodity now than in the future. This condition is the opposite of the market's state for most of the time since the financial crisis exploded in September 2008, suggesting the supply-demand picture is tightening. It is a bullish signal for oil traders, stimulating long investment or, at a minimum, the closing out of short positions.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures, meanwhile, cinched their second day of gains amid hopes of a comprehensive solution to Europe's debt crisis and robust demand for physical gold in Asia.\par \par \uc2 Base metals closed the day sharply higher on the London Metal Exchange Monday, as copper rallied to its highest PM kerb close price in more than a month. Confidence that progress is being made on resolving the euro zone's debt crisis boosted risk-related assets Monday, while strong economic data from top metals consumer China provided an extra boost for base metals.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro advanced Monday on increasing speculation that European leaders may be nearing a deal to enhance the euro zone's bailout facility, thus alleviating a source of anxiety for investors worried about the fallout from Greece's debt problems.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the wake of a weekend summit, European Union leadership sketched the outlines of an EUR107 billion ($150 billion) plan to inject funds into distressed European banks. However, no firm decision has been reached on how much of a loss Greek investors will have to take on their debt holdings, which could result in at least a 30% writedown on the value of their bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Europe's debt crisis has taken a toll on the Continent's economic health, vividly illustrated by weak manufacturing data from France and Germany. The dour figures have raised the prospect that the euro zone's two economic powerhouses may fall into recession, and that the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may have to cut borrowing costs in order to ward off those effects.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nonetheless, the euro advanced on cautious optimism that German Chancellor Angela Merkel and French President Nicholas Sarkozy can strike an accord on how to leverage the euro zone's bailout fund in a way that buttresses the euro zone's distressed economies and prevents the euro zone's problems from undercutting the global economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Merkel and Sarkozy are now on the same page and moving in the same direction on the short-term issues," said Kevin Hebner, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan Chase\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "We're moving slowly along the track but not off track."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Monday in New York, the euro was at $1.3929 from $1.3891 late Friday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y76.09 from Y76.28, while the euro was at Y106.02 from Y105.94. The U.K. pound was at $1.6019 from $1.5959. The dollar was at CHF0.8805 from CHF0.8825.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 76.083 from about 76.384.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As investors piled back into stocks and high-yielding assets, the lift the greenback has gotten from investors seeking shelter from Europe's debt crisis evaporated. Those same flows also helped push the yen--traditionally a safe-haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 --to a record high against the dollar below Y76 just last week. The dollar flirted with that level again Monday, but fears that Japanese officials may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  helped contain its losses.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister Monday vowed to take "resolute action" on the strong yen. Traders interpreted that as a possibility the world's third largest economy could enter markets to weaken its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , lest it undercut the country's efforts to engineer an export-led recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With Europe's debt saga dominating headlines, the U.S. economy has quietly surprised analysts with a string of vigorous data that suggests a recession is becoming less of a probability. Many large banks have begun revising upward their expectations for third-quarter growth, with economists estimating the economy expanded by at least 2% at an annualized rate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While that pace would be better than some feared earlier this year, it is still below what is considered the U.S. economy's normal rate of trend growth. With unemployment still weak, the Federal Reserve has refused to rule out a third round of quantitative easing, or QE3, to spur job creation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The data in the U.S. has been half-decent, and we've had quite a bit of data surprises," said Alan Ruskin, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "QE3 is more likely to only happen in an environment where growth starts to slow down," he said, but added that hints from Fed officials are positive for high-yielding assets such as stocks and the euro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 24-10-11 2203GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury investors laid low Monday as they await Wednesday's potentially groundbreaking euro-zone plan from the sidelines, leaving prices a tad lower after a tight-ranged trading session.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The fixation on Europe has led other traditional market-moving events to go mostly unnoticed--including economic reports and a recent series of Federal Reserve officials who have dropped hints about another round of quantitative easing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Instead, attention was set squarely on the few details that did arise from the euro-zone gathering over the weekend, which showed signs of progress at figuring out the region's debt problems. This included hints of a broad agreement to keep the euro-zone banking system well cushioned, and word that officials have agreed to bolster the region's bailout fund to more than EUR1 trillion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even though the euro zone's private sector shrank in October, suggesting risks of the region slipping into a recession, summit-related news was enough kept the recent sense of optimism across financial markets intact. The spotlight is now on the next meeting this Wednesday, when leaders have promised to deliver a comprehensive solution.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A plan to fix the debt crisis was originally targeted to be released Sunday, but German officials tempered expectations last week, delaying until Wednesday. That led to little investor guidance emerging from this past weekend's round of crunch-time meetings.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, losses in the Treasurys market were minor. This was partly because of pressure going into this week's round of supply, analysts say, but also a reflection of investors' unwillingness to stray too far from the safety of U.S. government bonds until hearing Wednesday's announcement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Investors are trading cautiously," said Richard McGuire, senior fixed-income strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rabobank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "People don't want to take aggressive positions ahead of the meeting, with a potential rabbit-of-out-a-hat event."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The full German Parliament is expected to vote Wednesday on proposals for enhancing the euro zone's bailout fund--a crucial event because of Germany's influential economic role in the debt-crisis negotiations. Chancellor Angela Merkel will regroup with the region's leaders after that for the highly anticipated meeting.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  says the direction Treasury yields are headed in the short run "depends critically on developments in Europe." An aggressive European response can cause investors to sell out of Treasurys and push 10-year yields to 2.5%, while a disappointing plan will lure buyers back into the market to bring 10-year yields back down to 2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In late Monday afternoon trading in New York, benchmark 10-year notes declined 6/32 in price to yield 2.228%, while two-year notes lost a fraction in price to yield 0.283%. The 30-year bond lost 8/32 in price to yield 3.267% despite the Federal Reserve's $2.5 billon long-dated purchases this morning.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speculation that European leaders will contain fallout from the region's debt crisis prodded investors to sell safe-haven Japanese government bonds Monday ahead of a European Union summit Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts said JGB prices may continue to fall gradually this week, particularly if the EU meeting Wednesday yields a plan that appears actionable even if it is not a final fix.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The yen-bond market will probably soften somewhat," said Jun Ishii, chief bond strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We forecast that markets will give a positive assessment and tone down their rejection of risk," so long as the Europeans' plan is satisfactory, he said. "Such reactions were seen when financial support for Greece was decided in May 2010 and bank stress tests were carried out in July 2010, both of which caused the risk-avoidance trend to pause temporarily."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended higher Monday as inflation concerns continued to ease after the state-run Securities Times reported a top economic planning official as saying the increase in the country's consumer price index will fall below 5% in November and December.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 National Development and Reform Commission Vice Chairman Peng Sen said China's inflation has peaked and consumer price growth started slowing in August, according to the report. But traders added that gains in bonds will likely be capped if more positive signs on the economy show up. Data Monday showed the preliminary HSBC China Manufacturing Purchasing Managers Index rebounded strongly in October, rising to 51.1 from a final reading of 49.9 in September.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will auction CNY30 billion in six-month state-fund deposits Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among the data due Tuesday, Singapore's manufacturing output likely grew 9.7% on the year in September, marking the fourth straight rise, according to the median estimate of 12 economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires. On the month, in seasonally adjusted terms, output is likely to have contracted 4.2% after rising 3.9% in August, according to the median of nine estimates in the same poll.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, India's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely raise its key policy rate 25 basis points Tuesday to control near-double digit inflation, even if it hurts short-term economic growth, according to a Dow Jones poll of 20 economists. Of the 20 economists polled, 13 expect the RBI to hike the repo rate, or its lending rate, to 8.50% from 8.25% at Tuesday's mid-year policy review. The remaining seven economists expect the RBI to stand pat due to mounting growth worries.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par Cigna Targets Seniors With $3.8B Healthspring Buy \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2  To Buy Rightnow In Deal Valued At $1.5B \par European Leaders Said To Agree On EUR1T EFSF \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Google\f28 \uc2 \f2  Agrees To Privacy Program Audits \par Trichet: ECB Will Continue To Anchor Price Stability \par FAA Seeks Checks Of Tails Of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Boeing\f28 \uc2 \f2  757 Jets \par EU May Ask IMF To Administer Special Debt Crisis Fund -Source \par Greek Writedown Said To Involve All Debt In Private Hands \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Texas Instruments\f28 \uc2 \f2  Net Off 30% \par Netflix 3Q Net Surges But Outlook Weak \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Amgen\f28 \uc2 \f2  3Q Profit Plunges On Litigation Reserve \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 SemGroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  Rejects Plains All American Takeover \par Earthquake's Death Toll Climbing In Eastern Turkey \par Swiss National Bank To Defend Franc Floor \par Europe Nears Deal On Bank Recapitalization \par Mazda, Honda To Keep Thai Plants Closed This Week -Nikkei \par BOJ Sets Up Lending Program For Flood-Hit Japanese Firms -Nikkei \par MUFG Sets Sights On Doubling Chinese Network -Nikkei \par Government To Call For Quick Sale Of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Japan Tobacco\f28 \uc2 \f2  Shares -Nikkei \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sumitomo Chemical\f28 \uc2 \f2  Operating Profit Seen Up 2% On Drug Sales -Nikkei \par SAIC Rolling Out Hybrids In Green Drive -Nikkei \par DBJ To Lend Up To Y500B To Firms Hit By Strong Yen, Disaster -Nikkei \par Skylark Eyeing 1st Net Increase In Stores In 6 Years -Nikkei \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 0100  Philippines  Aug External Trade Performance \par 0500  Singapore    Sep Industrial Production Index \par 0530  India        2Q  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  Annual Statement on \par                    Monetary Policy Mid Term Review \par 0600  Japan        Aug Revised Machine Tool Orders \par 0610  Germany      Nov GfK consumer climate survey \par 0645  France       Oct Consumer confidence survey \par 0745  France       Sep Housing starts \par 0820  Taiwan       Sep Money Supply \par 0830  Hong Kong    Sep External Merchandise Trade \par 0830  UK           Sep BBA main high street banking groups statistics \par 0830  UK           2Q  Balance of Payments \par 0845  UK           Treasury Committee - MPs quiz Mervyn King on second round \par                    of quantitative easing \par 1145  US           Oct 22 ICSC-\f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  Chain Store Sales Index \par 1255  US           Oct 22 Johnson Redbook Retail Sales Index \par 1300  US           Aug S&P / Case-Shiller Home Price Index \par 1400  US           Aug U.S. House Price Index \par 1400  US           Oct Richmond Fed Business Activity Survey \par 1400  US           Oct Consumer Confidence Index \par 2030  US           Oct 21 API Statistical Bulletin \par N/A   China        EU-China Summit in Beijing \par N/A   US           Treasury Secy Geithner tours world's largest fiber optics manufacturing \par                    facility \par  \par -By Helen Ozolins, Dow Jones Newswires; +64 4 471 5990; helen.ozolins@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 24-10-11 2203GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1160 4119   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111024e7ao001b7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
