\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 807 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2011\par 10:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SPAIN FIN MIN: JULY DEBT DEAL FOR GREECE NOT ENOUGH ANYMORE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The deal reached over the summer by the European Union and commercial banks holding Greek debt is not enough to help Greece avoid insolvency, Spain's Finance Minister Elena Salgado said Monday. (published 0758 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: GERMAN PRIVATE SECTOR GREW AT A FASTER PACE IN OCT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany's private sector growth expanded at a faster pace in October as more buoyant activity in the services sector countered a slump in the manufacturing sector, the preliminary reading of Markit's October composite purchasing managers index showed Monday. (published 0849 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: FRENCH PRIVATE-SECTOR OUTPUT SLUMPS IN OCTOBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The French private sector contracted at the fastest pace in two-and-a-half years in October, as services output slumped, according to Markit's preliminary estimate of the Managers Composite Index Monday. (published 0821 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: JAPAN HARDENS RHETORIC AFTER YEN HITS POST-WAR HIGH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's finance minister on Monday ratcheted up his warnings against the strong yen, helping to weaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the dollar by raising the specter of renewed yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Tokyo. (published 0206 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par 2ND UPDATE:\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  EXPORTS, TRADE SURPLUS RECOVER TO PRE-QUAKE LEVELS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese exports and the country's trade surplus have now recovered all their losses since the March earthquake and tsunami, though the yen's record strength, the European debt crisis and slower growth overseas threaten the economy's recovery from the disasters. (published 0330 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  PRELIMINARY CHINA PMI REBOUNDS TO 51.1 IN OCTOBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The preliminary HSBC China Manufacturing Purchasing Managers Index, a gauge of nationwide manufacturing activity, rebounded strongly in October, rising to 51.1 from a final reading of 49.9 in September, \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC Holdings PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Monday. (published 0536 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WSJ: EUROPEAN LEADERS DEBATE SEVERE OPTIONS FOR ACCORD \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European leaders took their first steps toward a new plan to stem the euro crisis, admitting that their last grand plan, agreed to only three months ago, has failed. (published 0100 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US COMPANIES PREDICT SCANT ECONOMIC GROWTH IN 2011 -NABE SURVEY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A vast majority of businesses predict the U.S. economy will grow only slightly in 2011, a more negative outlook from July when most called for more robust improvement, according to a survey of U.S. companies released Monday. (published 0401 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA STATE ECONOMIST: ECONOMIC GROWTH RATE MAY BE ABOVE 9% IN 2011 - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's economy is likely to expand by more than 9% this year, and growth should remain reasonable despite the recent slowdown, an economist with a government think-tank wrote in an article published by the state-run People's Daily on Monday. (published 0446 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: CHINA OFFICIAL: INFLATION TO FALL BELOW 5% BY NOVEMBER - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's consumer price growth will fall below 5% in November and December, a top economic planning official was cited as saying Monday, offering confidence that the government may have gained the upper hand in its fight against inflation. (published 0516 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: BENIGN AUSTRALIAN PPI DATA BRINGS RATE CUT CLOSER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An interest rate cut in Australia moved a small step closer Monday as producer price inflation proved more benign than expected in the third quarter. (published 0313 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA TO OFFER BANKS INCENTIVES TO SUPPORT SMALL FIRMS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's banking regulator said Monday it will offer incentives to encourage banks to boost their financial support for cash-hungry small businesses. (published 0419 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: SINGAPORE CPI INFLATION EASES A BIT; VINDICATES EASING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's consumer prices rose more slowly than expected in September as the pace of increase in transportation, clothing and recreation costs eased, vindicating the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's decision to ease monetary policy. (published 0559 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MOODY'S: THAI FLOODS LIKELY TO COST THAILAND 2% OF 2011 GDP \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Flooding in Thailand is likely to cost 2% of the country's 2011 gross domestic product, but the government has ample fiscal space to absorb related expenses, reports by Moody's Investors Service found Monday. (published 0358 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par VIETNAM OCTOBER CPI UP 0.36% ON MONTH, UP 21.59% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Vietnam's consumer price index rose 0.36% on month in October, slower than a 0.82% rise in September and an increase of 0.93% in August, the government's General Statistics Office said Monday. (published 0304 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : DOLLAR LITTLE CHANGED VS YEN ON \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FEARS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was little changed against the yen on Monday in Asia as heightened caution over possible market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  kept investors away from active trading. (published 0509 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WSJE(10/24) CRUNCH TIME FOR FRANCO-GERMAN RELATIONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Decisions taken over the coming week will define Europe's destiny for generations. (published 0430 GMT) [ 24-10-11 0806GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111024e7ao000mo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
